      "sourceStartLine": 2830,
      "content": "\\n\\n     S�m�pya liberation is described in these verses (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n3.24.43-47), verses I have already discussed in Bhagavat-sandarbha\\n(anuccheda 84), verses that describe Kardama Muni's attainment of\\nliberation:\\n\\n\\nmano brahma�i yu�j�na�. . .\\n     . . .labdh�tm� mukta-bandhana�. . .\\n . . .bhagavad-bhakti-yuktena\\n     pr�pt� bh�gavat� gati�\\n\\n\\n     \"He fixed his mind upon the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\\nParabrahman, who is beyond cause and effect, who manifests the three modes\\nof material nature, who is beyond those three modes, and who is perceived\\nonly through unfailing devotional service.*\\n     \"Thus he gradually became unaffected by the false ego of material\\nidentity and became freed from material affection. Undisturbed, equal to\\neveryone, and without duality, he could indeedf see himself also. His mind\\nwas turned inward and was perfectly calm, like an ocean unagitated by\\nwaves.**\\n     \"He thus became liberated from conditioned life and became self-\\nsituated in transcendental devotional service to the Personality of\\nGodhead, V�sudeva, the omniscient Supersoul within everyone.*\\n     \"He began to see that the Supreme Personality of Godhead is situated\\nin everyone's heart, and that everyone is existing on Him, because He is\\nthe Supersoul of everyone.*\\n     \"Freed from all hatred and desire, Kardama Muni, being equal to\\neveryone because of discharging uncontaminated devotional service,\\nultimately attained the path back to Godhead.\"*\\n\\n     Now we will discuss s�yujya liberation, which was attained by Agh�sura\\nand other demons. �r�mad-Bh�gavatam clearly explains that s�yujya\\nliberation is not auspicious and desirable. It is not like s�lokya and the\\nother kinds of liberation. Some individual souls may desire to taste bliss\\nby merging into the Lord's existence. In these situations the Lord may by\\nHis potency allow them to thus taste a small particle of the remnants of\\nthe bliss He has already tasted. However, the souls that merge into the\\nLord in this way do not become Him. Neither do they attain all His powers.\\nFor example they do not attain His power to create universes. This is\\ndescribed in the following words of the B�hat-�ruti:\\n\\n\\nyadaina� mukto nu pravi�ati modate ca k�m��� caiv�nubhavati\\n\\n\\n     \"The liberated soul then merges into the Lord. Then he is happy. Then\\nhis desires are fulfilled.\"\\n\\n     In the M�dhyandin�yana-�ruti it is said:\\n\\n\\nbrahm�bhisampadya brahma�� pa�yati brahma�� ���oti\\n\\n\\n     \"Then the soul merges into Brahman. When Brahman sees, the soul also\\nsees. When Brahman hears, the soul also hears.\"\\n\\n     This state of merging into the Lord is attained by a very slight\\nintervention of the Lord's potency. The scriptures explain:\\n\\n\\n�datte hari-hastena\\n\\n\\n     \"With His own hand Lord K���a gives s�yujya liberation.\"\\n\\n     Sometimes, if the Lord wishes to enjoy pastimes with that individual\\nsoul, the Lord may send him out of that merged condition and make him a\\npersonal associate of the Lord. �i�up�la and Dantavakra are examples of\\nthis. First they attained s�yujya liberation, and then afterwards they\\nbecame personal associates of the Lord. �r� N�rada describes them in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (7.1.47):\\n\\n\\nvair�nubandha-t�vre�a\\n     dhy�nen�cyuta-s�tmat�m\\nn�tau punar hare� p�r�va�\\n     jagmatur vi��u-par�adau\\n\\n\\n     \"These two associates of Lord Vi��u-Jaya and Vijaya-maintained a\\nfeeling of enmity for a very long time. Because of always thinking of K���a\\nin this way, they regained the shelter of the Lord, having returned home,\\nback to Godhead.\"*\\n\\n     In this way we have described how, by directly seeing the Supreme\\nLord, the individual soul may quickly attain s�lokya or the other kinds of\\nliberation. Now we will show how the soul may gradually attain liberation\\nand meet the Supreme Personality of Godhead. This gradual attainment of\\nliberation is also described in the scriptures. For example, Aj�mila's\\nattainment of perfection is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(6.2.40-44):\\n\\n\\nsa tasmin deva-sadana\\n     �s�no yogam �sthita�\\npraty�h�tendriya-gr�mo\\n     yuyoja mana �tmani\\n\\n\\n     \"In Hardwar, Aj�mila took shelter at a Vi��u temple, where he executed\\nthe process of bhakti-yoga. He controlled his senses and fully applied his\\nmind in the service of the Lord.*\\n\\n\\ntato gu�ebhya �tm�na�\\n     viyujy�tma-sam�dhin�\\nyuyuje bhagavad-dh�mni\\n     brahma�y anubhav�tmani\\n\\n\\n     \"Aj�mila fully engaged in devotional service. Thus he detached his\\nmind from the process of sense gratification and became fully absorbed in\\nthinking of the form of the Lord.*\\n\\n\\nyarhy up�rata-dh�s tasmin\\n     adr�k��t puru�an pura�\\nupalabhyopalabdh�n pr�g\\n     vavande �iras� dvija�\\n\\n\\n     \"When his intelligence and mind were fixed upon the form of the Lord,\\nthe br�hma�a Aj�mila once again saw before him four celestial persons. He\\ncould understand that they were those he had seen previously, and thus he\\noffered them his obeisances by bowing down before them.*\\n\\n\\nhitv� kalevara� t�rthe\\n     ga�g�y�� dar�an�d anu\\nsadya� svar�pa� jag�he\\n     bhagavat-p�r�va-vartin�m\\n\\n\\n     \"Upon seeing the Vi��ud�tas, Aj�mila gave up his material body at\\nHardwar on the bank of the Ganges. He regained his original spiritual body,\\nwhich was a body appropriate for an associate of the Lord.*\\n\\n\\ns�ka� vih�yas� vipro\\n     mah�puru�a-ki�karai�\\nhaima� vim�nam �ruhya\\n     yayau yatra �riya� pati�\\n\\n\\n     \"Accompanied by the order-carriers of Lord Vi��u, Aj�mila boarded an\\nairplane made of gold. Passing through the airways, he went directly to the\\nabode of Lord Vi��u, the husband of the goddess of fortune.\"*\\n\\n     The meaning of these verses is clear. Of the two, the quick attainemnt\\nof liberation is better than the gradual attainment of liberation.\\n\\n"
