      "content": "\\n\\n     It is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (2.1.7):\\n\\n\\npr�ye�a munayo r�jan\\n     niv�tt� vidhi-�edhata�\\nnairgu�ya-sth� ramante sma\\n     gu��nukathane hare�\\n\\n\\n     \"O King Par�k�it, mainly the topmost transcendentalists, who are above\\nthe regulative principles and restrictions, take pleasure in describing the\\nglories of the Lord.\"*\\n\\n     In these words the devotional service performed by the great souls is\\ndescribed. The position of the non-devotees is criticized in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (2.3.24):\\n\\n\\ntad a�ma-s�ra� h�daya� batedam\\n\\n\\n     \"Certainly that heart is steel-framed which, in spite of one's\\nchanting the holy name of the Lord with concentration, does not change when\\necstasy takes place, tears fill the eyes, and the hairs stand on end.\"*\\n\\n     The devotees are also praised in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(7.13.23):\\n\\n\\ntath�pi br�mahe pra�n��s\\n     tava r�jan yath�-�rutam\\nsambh��a��yo hi bhav�n\\n     �tmana� �uddhim icchat�\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear king, although you know everything, you have posed some\\nquestions, which I shall try to answer according to what I have learned by\\nhearing from authorities. I cannot remain silent in this regard, for a\\npersonality like you is just fit to be spoken to by one who desires self-\\npurification.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"�uddhim\" means \"the desire to attain pure\\ndveotional service\". This verse was spoken by Lord Datt�treya to �r�\\nPrahl�da.\\n\\n"
    },
    {
