      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 5035,
      "content": "\\n\\n     If a devotee prays for material things, he wishes them only to serve\\nthe Supreme Lord. The devotee does not wish them for his own personal sense\\ngratification. This is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.70.41):\\n\\n\\nyak�yati tv�� makhendre�a\\n     r�jas�yena p���ava�\\np�rame��hya-k�mo n�patis\\n     tad bhav�n anumodat�m\\n\\n\\n     \"First of all I may inform You that King Yudhi��hira has all material\\nopulences which are possible to achieve in the highets planetary system,\\nBrahmaloka. He has no material opulence for which to aspire, and yet he\\nwants to perform R�jas�ya sacrifices only to get Your association and to\\nplease You.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"p�rame��hya\" means {.sy 168}Lord K���a, the\\nking of Dv�rak�\".That the word {.sy 168}parame��h�\" refers to Lord K���a is\\nconfirmed by the following words describing Lord K���a's accepting the rice\\nbrought by Sud�m� Vipra (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.81.10):\\n\\n\\nt�vac chr�r jag�he hasta�\\n     tat-par� parame��hina�\\n\\n\\n     \"Rukmi��-dev�, who is the goddess of fortune herself, checked the Lord\\nby catching hold of His hand.\"*\\n\\n     Because \"paramesthi\" refers to Lord K���a, the word \"paramesthya\" may\\nbe taken to mean \"the opulence of Dvaraka City\". Interpreted in this way,\\nthe word {.sy 168}paramesthya-kamah\" means \"desiring opulence like that of\\nLord K���a Himself\". He desired opulence like that of Dvaraka so\\nIndraprastha would a be a fit place for Lord K���a to reside. He had no\\nother desire. This truth about Mah�r�ja Yudhi��hira is also described in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.12.6):\\n\\n\\nki� te k�m�� sura-sparh�\\n     mukunda-manaso dvij��\\nadhijahur muda� r�j�a�\\n     k�udhitasya yathetare\\n\\n\\n     \"O br�hma�as, the opulence of the king was so enchanting that the\\ndenizens of heaven aspired for it. But because he was absorbed in the\\nservice of the Lord, nothing could satisfy him except the Lord's service.\"*\\n\\n     By Lord K���a's mercy, Mah�r�ja Yudhi��hira attained this desire. This\\nis described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.75.34-35):\\n\\n\\nsabh�y�� maya-klpt�y��\\n     kv�pi dharma-suto 'dhir��\\nv�to 'nugair bandhubhi� ca\\n     k���en�pi sva-cak�u��\\n\\n�s�na� k��cane s�k��d\\n     �sane maghav�n iva\\np�rame��hya-�riy� ju��a�\\n     st�yam�na� ca vandibhi�\\n\\n\\n     \"Once upon a time king Yudhi��hira was sitting on the golden throne in\\nthe palace constructed by the demon Maya. His foru brothers and other\\nrelatives, as well as his great well-wisher, the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead, K���a, were present and the material opulence of King Yudhi��hira\\nseemed no less than that of Lord Brahm�.\"*\\n\\n     In these verses the word \"sva-caksusa\" (with his own eye) is an\\nadvjective referring to Lord K���a. A person who has eyes and can see has a\\npossession a bind man does not have. A blind man will certainly yearn to\\nattain such a possession, the possession of having eyes that can see. If a\\nperson loses his sight, then all his possessions become useless to him. For\\nthe devotees, devotees who consider Lord K���a their Lord and master, Lord\\nK���a's is their precious eyes.\\n\\n     Making reference to the P���avas, �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m� spoke these\\nwords to Mah�r�ja Par�k�it (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 1.16.20):\\n\\n\\nna v�. . .\\n . . .ye 'dhy�sana� r�ja-kir��a-ju��a�\\n     sadyo jahur bhagavat-p�r�va-k�m��\\n\\n\\n     \"O chief of all the saintly kings of the P���u dynasty who are\\nstrictly in the line of Lord �r� K���a! It is not at all astonishing that\\nyou give up your throne, which is decorated with the helmets of many kings,\\nto achieve eternal association with the Supreme Personality of Godhead.\"*\\n\\n     Speaking to Lord K���a, �r� N�rada said:\\n\\n\\ntad bhav�n anumodat�m\\n\\n\\n    \"May Your Lordhsip be pleased.\"\\n\\n     In this way it is seen that the Supreme Personality of Godhead is\\npleased with His pure devotees. The Supreme Personality of Godhead\\npersonally affirmed this truth by saying (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.60.50):\\n\\n\\ny�n y�n k�mayase k�m�n\\n     mayy ak�m�ya bh�mini\\nsanti hy ek�nta-bhakt�y�s\\n     tava kaly��i nityad�\\n\\n\\n     \"O most beautiful Rukmi��, You are My dearmost wife. I am greatly\\npleased to understand how much love you have for Me. Please take it for\\ngranted that no matter what ambition and desire you might have and no\\nmatter what you might expect from Me, I am always at your service.\"*\\n\\n     Here the word \"ak�ma�\" means \"devotional service where pure love for\\nLord K���a is present and where there is no other desire.\" In this way\\n\"ak�ma�\" means {.sy 168}the desire for devotional service nad nothing\\nelse\". The word \"ak�ma� is used in that way in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (2.3.10):\\n\\n\\nak�ma� sarva-k�mo v�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"A person who has broader intelligence, whether he be full of all\\nmaterial desire, without any material desire, or desiring liberation, must\\nby all means worship the Supreme whole, the Personality of Godhead.\"*\\n\\n     Devotional service with pure love for the Supreme Lord is also\\ndescribed by �r� Prahl�da in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (7.10.3):\\n\\n\\nbh�tya-lak�a�a-jij��su�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O my worshipable Lord, because the seed of lusty desires, which is\\nthe root cause of material existence, is within the core of everyone's\\nheart, You have sent me to this material world to exhibit the symptoms of a\\npure devotee.*\\n     \"Otherwise, O my Lord, O supreme instructor of the entire world, You\\nare so kind to Your devotee that You could not induce him to do something\\nunbeneficial for him. On the other hand, one who desires some material\\nbenefit in exchange for devotional service cannot be Your pure devotee.\\nIndeed, he is no better than a merchant who wants profit in exchange for\\nservice.\"*\\n\\n     Therefore, in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.60.50 the words {.sy 168}ak�m�ya\\ny�n y�n k�mayase k�m�n\" mean \"O Queen Rukmi��, whatever you desire to\\nattain the great treasure of loving devotional service to Me\". Because\\nRukmi�� is Lord K���a's eternal beloved, the eternal goddess Lak�m�\", she\\nis here addressed by the word \"santi\" (o eternal one).\\n\\nHere the word \"ek�nta-bhakt�y��\" means \"who do not desire anything for your\\nown benefit\", \"k�mini\" means \"O you who desire only to please Me\", and {.sy\\n168}kaly��i\" means \"O you who remove all obstacles to attaining the great\\ngood fortune that is devotional service\". The verse quoted in the beginning\\nof this anuccheda was spoken by Lord K���a to Queen Rukmi��.\\n\\n"
