      "content": "\\n\\n     That �r� R�dh� is more glorious than the 16,000 queens of Lord K���a,\\nthe king of the Yadus, is confirmed by the queens themselves, who say in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.83.41-43):\\n\\n\\nna vaya� s�dhvi samr�jya�\\n     svar�jya� bhaujyam apy uta\\nvair�jya� parame��hya� ca\\n     �nantya� v� hare� padam\\n\\nk�may�maha etasya\\n     �r�mat-p�da-raja� �riya�\\nkuca-ku�kuma-gandh��hya�\\n     m�rdhn� vo�hu� gad�-bh�ta�\\n\\n\\n     \"O saintly lady, we do not desire dominion over the earth, the\\nsovereignty of the King of heaven, unlimited facility for enjoyment, mystic\\npower, the position of Lord Brahm�, immortality, or even attainment of the\\nkingdom of God. We simply desire to carry on our heads the glorious dust of\\nLord K���a's feet, enriched by the freagrance of ku�kuma from His consort's\\nbosom.***\\n\\n\\nvraja-striyo yad v��chanti\\n     pulindyas t��a-v�rudha�\\ng�va� c�rayato gop��\\n     p�da-spar�a� mah�tmana�\\n\\n\\n     \"We desire the same contact with the Supreme Lord's feet that the\\nyoung women of Vraja, the cowherd boys, and even the aborigine Pulinda\\nwomen desire-the touch of the dust He leaves on the plants and grass as He\\ntends His cows.\"***\\n\\n     This verse means: \"O saintly lady, we do not desire dominion over the\\nearth, or any other material benefit.\" Here the word \"s�mr�jyam\" means \"a\\nkingdom on the earth\", \"sv�r�jyam\" means \"the kingdom of Indra\", {.sy\\n168}bhojyam\" means \"the pleasures available in these two kingdoms\",\\n\"vair�jyam\" means \"mystic powers\", \"and \"p�rame��hyam\" means \"the kingdom\\nof Lord Brahm�\". \"Anantyam\" here means \"the limitless happiness one attains\\nby realizing the Supreme Personality of Godhead\". This limitless happiness\\nis described in the Taittir�ya Upani�ad (2.8.2-9), where the different\\nlevels of human happiness, each level of happiness a hundred times greater\\nthan the one below is described. The highest of these levels of happiness\\nis found in realizing the Supreme Personality of Godhead. In Taittir�ya\\nUpani�ad (2.8.9) that limitless happiness is described. That is the meaning\\nhere. What more may be said? The queens say: \"We do not desire (na\\nk�may�mahe) to stay near Lord Hari (�r�-hare� padam).\" Someone may ask:\\n\"Then what do you desire to attain?\" To this question the queens answer:\\n\"We desire to carry (vo�hu�) on our heads (m�rdhn�) the dust of the\\nglorious feet (�r�mat-p�da-raja�) of our master, all-knowing Lord K���a\\n(gad�-bh�ta�). Fuerther, we desire to attain the great treasure present in\\nthat dust, the treasure that is the fragrance of ku�kuma from the goddess\\nof fortune's breast (�riya� kuca-ku�kuma-gandh��hya�).\"\\n     Here someone may protest: \"O queens, first you say you do not desire\\nto attain Lord Hari's feet, and then you say you want to hold the dust of\\nHis feet on your heads, dust rich with the fragrance of ku�kuma from the\\ngoddess of fortune's breast. Why do you contradict your own words? If\\nRukmi��, the goddess of fortune, desires, then you will certainly attain\\nyour desire. Then you will indeed attain the dust of the Lord's feet, dust\\nrich with ku�kuma from the most exalted goddess of fortune's breast. But\\nfirst please clearly tell us what it is that you desire.\"\\n     The answer to this protest is found in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.83.43,\\nwhere the feet of Lord K���a (not Lord N�r�ya�a) are specifically\\ndescribed. The dust of Lord K���a's feet are also described in these words\\nof �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.21.17):\\n\\n\\np�r��� pulindya urug�ya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The aborigine women of the V�nd�vana area become disturbed by lust\\nwhen they see the grass marked with reddish ku�kuma\\npowder. Endowed with the color of K���a's lotus feet, this powder\\noriginally decorated the breasts of His beloveds, and when the aborigine\\nwomen smear it on their faces and breasts, they give up all their\\nanxiety.\"***\\n\\n     In these quotes it is seen that the gop�s of Vraja yearned to attain\\n(yad v�nchanti) the dust of Lord K���a's feet. The present tense is used\\nhere (v�nchanti) on the assumption that even today the gop�s are not truly\\nseparated from Lord K���a. By mentioning the aborigine girls, the queens\\nhint (in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.83.43) that they themselves should also be\\nqualified to attain the dust of Lord K���a's feet. Here the words \"t��a-\\nvirudha�\" mean the d�rv� grass and other plants\". The fragranced of the\\nkunkuma from the goddess of foretune's breast is never absent from Lord\\nK���a's feet. That is the idea here. Here the word \"gavah\" means {.sy\\n168}cows\", \"carayata�\" means \"herding\", The cowherd boys (gop��) are only\\nbriefly mentioned here. Although many cowherd boys, such as the priya-\\nsakh�s and narma-sakh�s, are very close friends of Lord K���a, because they\\nare males an elaborate description of them here in the midts of a discusion\\nof the gop�s is not appropriate. Even famous Goddess Laksmi cannot attain\\nLord K���a's feet, although she aspires to. Thismis confirmed in the N�ga-\\npatn�s' prayers in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.16.36 and also in the words of\\nUddhava in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.62. Not even the famous Goddess Rukmi��\\ncan attain the same kind of association with Lord K���a, for her\\nassociation with Him is in a different time and place. Therefore Rukmi��\\ncannot hope to associate with Lord K���a as the gop�s of Vraja do. The\\nsuperior status of the gop�s is confirmed in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.60. Of\\nQueen Rukmi�� it is said the Matsya Pur��a:\\n\\n\\nrukmi�� dv�ravaty�� tu\\n     r�dh� v�nd�vane vane\\n\\n\\n     \"She who is Rukmi�� in Dv�rak� is also R�dh� in V�nd�vana Forest.\"\\n\\n     Ved�nta-s�tra (1.1.30) and the Ahangrahopasana-sastras declare that\\nthe demigod Indra and Goddess Durg� are in one sense not different from the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead. In the same way, in one sense �r� R�dh� is\\nnot different from Goddess Lak�m�. The difference is that �r� R�dh� is the\\noriginal and complete form of Goddess Lak�m�. This is confirmeed ion the\\nfollowing words of the B�had-Gautam�ya Tantra:\\n\\n\\ndev� k���amay� prokt�\\n     r�dhik�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The transcendental goddess �r�mat� R�dh�r��� is the direct\\ncounterpart of Lord �r� K���a. She is the central figure for all the\\ngoddesses of fortune. She possesses all the attractiveness to attract the\\nall-attractive Supreme Personality of Godhead. She is the primeval internal\\npotency of the Lord.\"*\\n\\n     In the Bahv-�k-pari�i��a it is said of �r� R�dh�:\\n\\n\\nr�dhay� m�dhavo devo\\n     m�dhavenaiva r�dhik�\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a always stays with �r� R�dh�. �r� R�dh� always stays with\\nLord K���a.\"\\n\\n     When she appears as �r� R�dh�, the goddess of fortune is most\\nglorious. That is confirmed by these words. It is also confirmed by the\\nfollowiong words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.28), which refer to �r� R�dh�:\\n\\n\\n\\nanay�r�dhito n�na�\\n     bhagavan. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Certainly this particular gop� has perfectly worshiped the all-\\npowerful Personality of Godhead, Govinda, since He was so pleased with Her\\nthat He abandoned the rest of us and brought Her to a secluded place.\"***\\n\\n     The following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.11) also refer to �r�\\nR�dh�:\\n\\n\\napy e�a-patny upagata�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O friend, wife of the deer, has Lord Acyuta been here with His\\nbeloved, bringing great joy to your eyes? Indeed, blowing this way is the\\nfragrance of His garland of kun�a flowers, which was smeared with the\\nku�kuma from the breasts of His girlfriend when He embraced Her.\"***\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.83.41-43 the Dvaraka queens say: \"As the gop�s\\nyearn to touch Lord K���a's feet, so do we.\" They pray: \"May the fragrance\\nof the ecstatic love the gop�s' feel be manifest in us.\" The Dv�rak� queens\\ndo not desire only to attain the dust of Lord K���a's feet. They desire to\\ntouch His feet also. Thus the words \"vaya� k�may�mahe\" may refer to the\\ndesire expressed in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.83.42 or the desire expressed in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.83.43. Thus the Dv�rak� queens' request may be either\\n{.sy 168}We desire to touch Lord K���a's feet.\" or \"We desire to touch the\\ndust of Lord K���a's feet.\" Either way, the same result is attained.\\nTherefore these two requests are not truly different. In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.83.42 the word {.sy 168}etasya\" refers to Lord K���a. What is Lord\\nK���a's nature? In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.83.43 it is said that Lord K���a is\\n{.sy 168}mah�tmana�\". In that word \"mah�\" means \"He who is naturally the\\ngreatest of all\", and \"�tmana�\" means \"He whose nature is filled with\\nhandsomeness and all other virtues\". Lord K���a's handsomeness and virtues\\nare seen in these words spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m� of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.33.6):\\n\\n\\ntatr�ti�u�ubhe t�bhir\\n     bhagav�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"In the midst of the dancing gop�s, Lord K���a appeared most\\nbrilliant, like an exquisite sapphire in the midst of golden ornaments.\"***\\n\\n     The �r�mad-Bh�gavatam verses 10.83.41-43 quoted in the beginning of\\nthis anucheda were spoken by the Dv�rak� queens to Draupad�.\\n\\n"
