      "content": "\\n\\n     The different levels of gop�s who love the Supreme Lord in the mood of\\nsweetness, levels that culimate in the love felt by �r� R�dh�, who has no\\nequal or superior, will be described in this anuccheda. Considering its\\ncauses and effects, scholars learned in ordinary material poetry call this\\nkind of love {.sy 168}sth�yi-bh�va\" (continuous ecstatic love). The causes\\nof sth�yi-bh�va are vibh�va, anubh�va, and vyabhic�ri-bh�va. These bh�vas\\nare all part of pr�ti (ecstatic love). Sth�yi-bh�va is described in these\\nwords of the rasa-��stra:\\n\\n\\nviruddhair aviruddhair v�\\n     bh�vair vicchidyate na ya�\\n�tma-bh�va� nayaty any�n\\n     sa sth�y� lava��kara�\\n\\n\\n     \"Ecstatic love that is not diminished by favorable or unfavorable\\nsituations is called sth�yi-bh�va, which is love deep like an ocean.\"\\n\\n     Vibh�va and the other parts of love will be discussed later in this\\nbook. When sth�yi-bh�va and its causes are manifest in the context of\\necstatic love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead (bhagavat-pr�ti), then\\nthey are called \"bhagavat-pr�timaya-rasa\", or \"bhakti-rasa\" (the mellows of\\ndevotional service). That the bh�vas thus become the rasas is described in\\nthe following words of the rasa-��stra:\\n\\n\\nbh�v� ev�bhisampann��\\n     pray�nti rasa-r�pat�m\\n\\n\\n     \"The bh�vas become the rasas.\"\\n\\n     Because they do not have the ingredients (s�magr�) of the true rasas,\\nthe poets who relish the material rasas (pr�k�ta-rasikas) do not desire to\\ntaste the rasas of devotional service (bhakti-rasa). Their sphere of\\ntasting the rasas is limited to the material demigods or other material\\npersons. The s�magr�s (igredients of the rasas are of three kinds: 1.\\nsvar�pa-yuogyat� s�magr�, 2. parikara-yogyat� s�magr�, and 3. puru�a-\\nyogyat� s�magr�. The svar�pa-yuogyat� s�magr�s are the ingredients of\\nsth�yi-bh�va, rati and other sentiemnts when they are manifest in the\\nmaterial rasas. These sentiments are based on the individual soul's desire\\nto attain happiness for the Supreme Personality of Godhead, that love\\nbrings happiness much greater than the waves of happiness attained by\\nrealizing the impersonal Brahman. Persons in material consciousness cannot\\nunderstand those sentiments, which are called yogyat� s�magr�. Only\\nspiritual poets and philosophers can understand those spiritual sentiments.\\nThese sentiments are very beautiful and wonderful. The spiritual sentiments\\nfelt by �r� Prahl�da and the other great devotees are called puru�a-yogyat�\\ns�magr�. Material poets and philosophers who are not qualified like the\\ngreat devotees cannot understand these sentiments. This is described in the\\nfollowing words of S�hitya-darpa�a (3.2):\\n\\n\\npu�yavanta� pama�vanti\\n     yogi-vad rasa-santatim\\n\\n\\n     \"Only saintly devotees can understand the spiritual rasas.\"\\n\\n     It is also said in S�hitya-darpa�a (3.8):\\n\\n\\nna j�yate tad-�sv�do\\n     vin� raty-�di-v�san�m\\n\\n\\n     \"Without rati (attraction) and the other sentiments, the taste of the\\nrasas is not manifest.\"\\n\\n     In the material rasas one tastes the pleasure of one's own happiness.\\nThis is described in the following words of S�hitya-darpa�a (3.2):\\n\\n\\nsattvodrek�d akha��a-sva-\\n     prak���nanda-cin-maya�\\nvedy�ntara-spar�a-��nyo\\n     brahm�sv�da-sahodara�\\n\\n\\n     \"When the mode of goodness becomes very strong, then limitless\\nspiritual bliss becomes manifest. When one tastes the bliss of the Supreme,\\nthen even the touch of knowledge of anything else becomes absent. Knowledge\\nof the Supreme and unawareness of anything else are like two brothers.\\n\\n\\nlokottara-camatk�ra-\\n     pr��a� kai�cit pram�t�bhi�\\ns�k�ravad abhinnatven-\\n     �yam �sv�dyate rasa�\\n\\n\\n     \"Some great souls know the spoiritual wonders beyond the reach of this\\nworld. They taste the rasas of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\\nwhose form is spiritual.\"\\n\\n     Pure spiritual goodness (apr�k�ta-vi�uddha-sattva) enables one to\\ntaste the spriitual rasas. This is described in the following words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (4.3.23):\\n\\n\\nsattva� vi�uddha� vasudeva-�abditam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"I am always engaged in offering obeisances to Lord V�sudeva in pure\\nK���a consciousness. K���a consciousness is always pure consciousness\\n(vi�uddha-sattva), in which the Supreme Personality of Godhead, known as\\nV�sudeva, is revealed without any covering.\"*\\n\\n     Spiritual pure goodness has already been described in the Bhagavat-\\nsandarbha. That the taste of rasas of love for the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead is sweeter than the taste of realizing the impersonal Brahman is\\nconfirmed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (4.9.10):\\n\\n\\ny� nirv�ttis tanu-bh�t�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My Lord, the transcendental bliss derived from meditating upon Your\\nlotus feet or hearing about Your glories from pure devotees is so unlimited\\nthat it is far beyond thwe stage of brahm�nanda, wherein one thinks oneself\\nmerged in the impersonal Brahman as one with the Supreme.\"*\\n\\n     Tbis truth is also confirmed by the following words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (3.15.48):\\n\\n\\nn�tyantika� viga�ayanty api te pras�dam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Persons who are very expert and most intelligent in understanding\\nthings as they are engage in hearing narrations of the auspicious\\nactivities and pastimes of the Lord, which are worth chanting and worth\\nhearing. Such persons do not care even for the highest material\\nbenediction, namely liberation, to say nothing of less important other\\nbenedictions like the material happiness of the heavenly kingdom.\"*\\n\\n     That the rasas of perceiving the Supreme Personality of Godhead are\\nvery wonderful is confirmed by these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (3.2.12):\\n\\n\\nvism�pana� svasya ca saubhagarddhe�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The Lord appeared in the mortal world by His internal potency,\\nyogam�y�. He came in His eternal form, which is just suitable for His\\npastimes. These pastimes were wonderful for everyone, even for thos proud\\nof their own opulence, including the Lord Himself in His form as the Lord\\nof Vaiku��ha. Thus His (�r� K���a's) transcendental body is the ornament of\\nall ornaments.\"*\\n\\n     The great authors of the past, who knew everything about the spiritual\\nand material rasas, have throughly explained ths topic of rasa. Rasa was\\nalso described in a general way by the author of �r� Bhagavan-n�ma-kaumud�.\\nThe five rasas were explicitly described by �r�la �r�dhara Sv�m� in his\\ncommentary on �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.43.17). There he said the word\\n\"str���m\" referred to ���g�ra-rasa, \"gop�n�m\" referred to the sakh�, priya-\\nsakh�, and narma-sakh� friends of thw Lord, among whom �r�d�m� and many\\nothers are prominent, \"pitro�\" referred to the kind devotees in v�tsalya-\\nrasa, \"yogin�m\" referred to the j��ni-bhaktas in ��nta-rasa, {.sy\\n168}v����n�m\" referred to the devoteed V���is, and \"n���m\" referred ot the\\npeople in general who loved the Lord. In this description all the rasas,\\nbeginning with ��nta-rasa, are wonderfully described. The rasas are also\\nexplained by Dharma-datta in these words of S�hitya-darpa�a (3.2):\\n\\n\\nrase s�ra� camatk�ra�\\n     sarvatr�py anubh�yate\\ntac-camatk�ra-s�ratve\\n     sarvatr�py adbhuto rasa�\\ntasm�d adbhutam ev�ha\\n     k�t� n�r�yano rasam\\n\\n\\n     \"Wonder is present everywhere in the rasas. The rasas are wonderful\\neverywhere. Therefore, saintly N�r�yua�a d�sa says: the rasas are\\nwonderful.\"\\n\\n     �r�la �r�dhara Svami does not show honor to the rasas like raudra-rasa\\n(anger) felt by the wrestlers and others, for the persons in these rasas\\nact like enemies of the Lord. The philosophers who know the truths of the\\nspiritual rasas agree with this view. Some philosopghers learned in the\\nmaterial rasas especially honor sakhya-rasa and vatsalya-rasa. Of this view\\nit is said:\\n\\n\\nsneha-sth�yi-bh�va� prey�n\\n\\n\\n     \"The ecstasy of sth�yi-bh�va is manifest in sakhya-rasa.\"\\n\\n     ���g�ra-rasa is described ion these words:\\n\\n\\nyad eva rocate mahya�\\n     tad eva kurute priy�\\niti vetti na j�n�ti\\n     tat priya� yat karoti s�\\n\\n\\n     \"Whatever pleases me, my beloved does. I may be aware of her actions\\nor not, but she always acts to please me.\"\\n\\n     Those words described the relationship of husband and wife. The\\nfollowing words describe sakhya-rasa:\\n\\n\\nsphu�a� camatk�ritatay�\\n     vatsala� ca rasa� vidu�\\nsth�y� vatsalat�syeha\\n     putr�dy-�lambana� matam\\n\\n\\n     \"The sages know that vatsala-rasa is wonderful. In vatsala-rasa sth�yi-\\nbh�va is manifest. Vatsala-rasa is the love one feels for children and\\nothers in a like position.\"\\n\\n     The sage Sudeva and others acknowledge vatsala-rasa as one of the\\nbhakti-rasas. Furthermore, material rati (attraction) and the material\\nrasas may bring pleasure in the beginning, but in the end they bring\\nsuffering. This the Supreme Personality of Godhead Himself confirmed in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (11.19.41):\\n\\n\\n     . . .sukha� du�kha-sukh�tyaya�\\ndu�kham k�ma-sukh�pek��\\n\\n\\n     \"Real happiness is to transcend material happiness and unhappiness,\\nand real misery is to be implicated in searching for sex pleasure.\"***\\n\\n    The Supreme (ord describes true peace in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (11.19.36):\\n\\n\\n�amo man-ni��hat� buddhe�\\n\\n\\n     \"True peace is attained when one faithfully rests his intelligence in\\nMe.\"\\n\\n     In these weords the Supreme Lord criticizes material pleasures, which\\nbring horrible results. Even the ordinary people of this world eventually\\ncome to hate material pleasures. Criticizing material so-called pleasures\\nand praising the rasas of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead, �r�\\nN�rada explains in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.5.10-11):\\n\\n\\nna yad-vaca� citra-pada� harer ya�o\\n     jagat-pavitra� prag���ta karhicit\\ntad v�yasa� t�rtham u�anti m�nas�\\n     na yatra ha�s� niramanty u�ik-k�ay��\\n\\n\\n     \"Those words which do not describe the glories of the Lord, who alone\\ncan sanctify the atmosphere of the whole universe, are considered by\\nsaintly persons to be like unto a pilgrimage for crows. Since the all-\\nperfect persons are inhabitants of the transcendental abode, they do not\\nderive any pleasure there.*\\n\\n\\ntad-v�g-visargo janat�gha-viplavo\\n     yasmin prati-�lokam abaddhavaty api\\nn�m�ny anantasya ya�o '�kit�ni yat\\n     ���vanti g�yanti g��anti s�dhava�\\n\\n\\n     \"On the other hand, that literature which is full of descriptions of\\nthe transcendental glories of the name, fame, forms, pastimes, etc. of the\\nunlimited Supreme Lord is a different creation, full of transcendental\\nwords directed toward bringing about a revolution in the impious lives of\\nthis world's misdirected civilization. Such transcendental literatures,\\neven though imperfectly composed, are heard, sung, and accepted by purified\\nmen who are thoroughly honest.\"*\\n\\n     The material rasas are also rejected by Queen rukmi��, who says in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.45):\\n\\n\\ntvak-�ma�ru-roma-nakha-ke�a-pinaddham antar\\n     m��s�sthi-rakta-k�imi-vi�-kapha-pitta-v�tam\\nj�vac-chava� bhajati k�nta-matir vim��h�\\n     y� te pad�bja-makarandam ajighrat� str�\\n\\n\\n     \"A woman who fails to relish the fragranc of the honey of Your lotus\\nfeet becomes totally befooled, and thus she accepts as her husband or lover\\na living corpse covered with skin, whiskers, nails, head-hair, and body\\nhair, and filled with flesh, bones, blood, parasites, feces, mucus, bile,\\nand air.\"*\\n\\n     Therefore one should not place his faith in the material rasas, for\\nthe material rasas always lead to a situation filled with horrors. The\\nrasas of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead, on the other hand,\\nlead to liberation from the material world. If the material reasas lead\\nonly to distress and the spiritual rasas lead to liberation, why then do\\nthe foolish people not relish the spiritual rasas? The answer is given in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.1.4):\\n\\n\\nniv�tta-tar�air upag�yam�n�t. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Glorification of the Supreme Personality of Godhead is performed in\\nthe parampara system. That is, it is conveyed from spiritual master ot\\ndisciple. Such gloreification is relished by those no longer interested in\\nthe false, temporary glorification of this cosmic manifestation.\\nDescriptions of the Lord are the right medicine for the conditioned soul\\nundergoing repeated birth and death. Therefore, who will cease hearing such\\nglorification of the Lord except a butcher or one who is killing his own\\nself?\"*\\n\\n     The sweetness of thew spiritual rasas is described in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.21.19):\\n\\n\\naspandana� gati-mat�� pulakas tar���m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When Lord K���a plays on His flute, the sweet music causes the moving\\nliving entities to become stunned and the nonmoving trees to tremble in\\necstasy. These things are certainly very wonderful.\"***\\n\\n     The spiritual rasas of association with Lord K���a brought the\\ndevotees back to life. This is described in the following words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.17.15):\\n\\n\\nk���am sametya labdheh�\\n     �san �u�k� nag� api\\n\\n\\n     \"Having regained their vital functions, Ya�od�, Rohi��, Nanda and all\\nthe other cowherd women and men went up to K��na. O descendant of Kuru,\\neven the dried-up trees came back to life.\"***\\n\\n     The Pur��a �r�mad-Bh�gavatam teaches the rasas of love for the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead. This is confirmed by the following words (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 1.1.3):\\n\\n\\nnigama-kalpa-taro�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O expert and thoughtful men, rlish �r�mad-Bh�gavatam, the mature\\nfruit of the desire tree of Vedic literatures. It emanated from the lips of\\n�r� �ukadeva Gosv�m�. Therefore this fruit has become even more tasteful,\\nalthough its nectarean juice weas already relishable for all, including\\nliberated souls.\"*\\n\\n     This verse means: \"O auspicious souls learned in the rasas of love for\\nthe Supreme Personality of Godhead, in your hearts please relish the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead, whioch is the sweet fruit of desire tree of\\nthe Vedas, a tree with many branches and sub-branches that bear all fruits,\\na tree that has come to this earth from the spiritual world of Vaiku��ha.\"\\nHere the word \"aho\" ismnused to hint that �r�mad-Bh�gavatam enables one to\\nattain what is otherwise unattainable. The scripture �r�mad-Bh�gavatam is\\ndesdcriubeed here with the w0ord \"rasa\" because it is sweet with the\\nspiritual rasas. It is called Bh�gavatam because it describes the rasas of\\nlove for Bhagav�n (the Supreme Personality of Godhead). That �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam is filled with the reasas of love for the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead is described in these words (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 1.7.7):\\n\\n\\nyasy�� vai �r�yam���y��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Simply by giving aural reception to this Vedic literature (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam), the feeling for loving devotional service to Lord K���a, the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead, sprouts up at once to extinguish the fire\\nof lamentation, illusion and fearfulness.\"*\\n\\n     The word \"rasa\" is used in relation to the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead in these words of Taittiriya Upanisad (2.7.1):\\n\\n\\nraso vai sa�. rasa� hy ev�ya� labdhv�nand�-bhavati.\\n\\n\\n     \"When one understands the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the\\nreservoir of pleasure (rasa), K���a, he actually becomes transcendentally\\nblissful.\"*\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 1.1.3 the word \"rasik��\" shows that the sages who\\nattained perfection in ancient times as well as the sages who attained\\nperfection in recent tiomes knew the truth of the rasas. The word \"galitam\"\\nshows that the fruit here is perfectly ripe and sweet. Here it is seen that\\nbecause it has come from the tree of the scriptures, this fruit is very\\nsweet and delicious. \"Rasam\" here shows that this fruit has neither skin\\nnor pit. It has no defect in any way. The words \"bh�gavatam nigamasya\" here\\nshow that �r�mad-Bh�gavatam is the best of all the sacred fruits of the\\ntree of the scriptures. This is so because it teaches the highest goal of\\nlife. Even though the nectar of the Supreme Personality of Godhead is\\nnaturally very excellent, it is made even more excellent in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam. This is shown here by the word \"�uka\". Although the fruit of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam is very wonderful because it has fallen from the kalpa-\\nv�k�a tree of the scriptures, it is made even more sweet and nectarean\\nbecause it is touched by ther mouth of �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�. When it\\ntouches �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�'s mouth, that fruit becoems sweeter. When\\nthey come from the mouth of a great devotee, descroptions of the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead become sweeter. How much more is this true when such\\ndescriptions come from the mouth of �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�, the king of all\\ngreat devotees? By tasting ther nectar of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam one will never\\nbecome satiated. His desire to taste it will never become diminished.\\nTherefore, even after attaining liberation (�layam), one should drink\\n(pibata) the nectar of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam. That even liberated souls are\\nattracted to �r�mad-Bh�gavatam is confirmed by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m� in\\nthe following words (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 2.1.9):\\n\\n\\nparini��hito 'pi. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O saintly king, I was perfectly situated in transcendence, yet I was\\nstill attracted by the delineation of the pastimes of the Lord, who is\\ndescribed by enlightened verses.\"*\\n\\n     The sweetness of other things fades in the course of time. The\\nsweetness of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam is not like that. It does not fade. The\\nreasas of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead are of two kinds: 1.\\npr�ty-upayukta and 2. pr�ti-pari��ma. These are described in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (12.3.14-15):\\n\\n\\nkath� im�s te kathit� mah�yas��\\n     vitatya loke�u ya�a� pareyu��m\\nvij��na-vair�gya-vivak�ay� vibho\\n     vaco-vibh�tir na tu p�ram�rthyam\\n\\n\\n     \"O mighty Pariksit, I have related to you the narrations of all these\\ngreat kings, who spread their fame throughout the world and then departed.\\nMy real purpose was to teach transcendental knowledge and renunciation.\\nStories of kings lend power and opulence to these narrations but do not in\\nthemselves constitute the ultimate aspect of knwoledge.***\\n\\n\\nyas t�ttama-�loka-gu��nuv�da�\\n     sa�g�yate 'bhik��am ama�gala-ghna�\\ntam eva nitya� ���uy�d abhik��a�\\n     k���e 'mal�� bhaktim abhipsam�na�\\n\\n\\n     \"The person who desires pure devotional service to Lord K���a should\\nhear the narrations of Lord Uttama�loka's glorious qualities, the constant\\nchanting of which destoys everything inauspicious. The devotee should\\nengage in such listening in regular daily assemblies and should also\\ncontinue his hearing throughout the day.\"***\\n\\n     After describing the rasas in a general way, �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�\\ndescribes thme in more detail in the word {.sy 168}am�ta\". \"Am�ta\" here\\nrefers to the nectar of the Lord's pastimes, nectar described in these\\nowrds of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (12.13.11):\\n\\n\\nhari-l�l�-kath�-vr�t�-\\n     m�t�nandita-sat-suram\\n\\n\\n     \"�r�mad-Bh�gavatam is full of nectarean accounts of Lord K���a's\\npastimes, which guve ecstasy ot the\\nsaintly devotees and demigods.\"\\n\\n     That narrations of Lord's pastimes are sweet is confirmed by these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (12.4.40):\\n\\n\\nl�l�-kath�-rasa-ni�eva�am. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Narrations of lord K���a's pastimes are sweet like nectar.\"\\n\\n     In the word \"sat-suram\" from �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 12.13.11, the word\\n\"sat\" refers to the �tm�r�ma transcendentalists situated in Brahman\\nrealization, who are referred to by thew word \"sat�m\" in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.12.11, and the word \"suram\" refers to the devotees who taste only the\\nnectar of devotional service ot the Lord. In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 1.1.3 the\\nword \"amrta-drava\" means {.sy 168}the sweetest nectar of the Lord's\\npastimes\". The following exp\"lanation may be given: Although the nectar of\\nlove (pritimaya-rasa) for the Supreme Personality of Godhead is the\\nsweetest of all nectars (sreyah), various levels of that nectar be\\ncertainly be distinguished. The taste of the nectar of the rasas, as\\ndescribed in the word \"pibata\" is of two kinds, the first of which is the\\nrasas tasted by the Lord's personal associates who personally participate\\nin the Supreme Lord's pastimes. This perception of the rasas is the best,\\nbecause it is direct and confidential (antara�ga). The other perception of\\nthe rasas is tasted by all others. It is indirect and not confidential\\n(bahira�ga). These two levels of the perception of the nectar of the rasas\\nare both described here by the single word \"pibata\". This nectar is like a\\nriver flowing from the mouth of �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�. That �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam describes the sweetest nectar of love for the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead is confirmed by these words (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n12.13.15):\\n\\n\\nsarva-ved�nta-s�ra� hi. . .\\n . . .tad-ras�m�ta-t�ptasya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"�r�mad-Bh�gavatam is declared to be the essence of all Ved�nta\\nphilosophy. One who has felt satisfaction from its nectarean mellow will\\nnever be attracted by any other literature.\"***\\n\\n     In His commentary �r�la �r�dhara Svami defines the word {.sy\\n168}bhavukah\" in this way:\\n\\n     \" `Bh�vuk��' here means `they who are expert at meditating on the\\nnectar rasas'.\"\\n\\n     This meditation is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(1.5.19):\\n\\n\\nsmaran mukund��ghry-upag�hana� punar\\n     vidh�tum icchen na rasa-graho jana�\\n\\n\\n     \"A person who has once relished the taste of the lotus feet of the\\nLord can do nothing but remember that ecstasy again and again.\"*\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 1.1.3 quoted in this anuccheda was spoken by �r�la\\nVedavy�sa.\\n\\n"
