      "content": "\\n\\n     By the Lord's inconceivable poetncy even mutually contradictory\\nqualities reside within Him. This is known on thw authority of scripture.\\nIn Vedanta-sutra (2.1.27) it is said:\\n\\n\\n�rutes tui �abda-m�latv�t\\n\\n\\n     \"The nature of the Supreme is known only by the revelation of\\nscripture.\"\\n\\n     Some of these mutually contradictory features of the Lord are seen in\\nthe words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.43.17):\\n\\n\\nmall�n�m a�ani�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The various groups of people in the arena ragarded K���a in different\\nways when He entered it with His elder brother. The wrestlers saw K���a as\\na lightning bolt, the men of Mathur� as the best of males, the women as\\nCupid in person, the cowherd men as their relative, the impious rulers as a\\nchastiser, His parents as their child, the King of thwe Bhojas as death,\\nthe unintelligent as the Supreme Lord's universal form, the yog�s as the\\nAbsolute Truth, and the V���is as their supreme worshipable Deity.\"***\\n\\n     The inconceivable power of thw Lord is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.7.7):\\n\\n\\n�i�or analpaka-prav�la-m�dv-a�ghri-hata� vyavartata. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord �r� K���a was lying down underneath the handcart in one corner\\nof the courtyard, and although His little legs were as soft as leaves, when\\nHe struck the cart with His legs, it turned over violently and collapsed.\\nThe wheels separated from the axle, the hubs and spokes fell apart, and the\\npole of the handcart broke. On the cart there were many little utensils\\nmade of various metals, and all of them scattered hither and thither.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's transcendental gentleness sis described in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.15.16):\\n\\n\\nkvacit pallava-talpe�u\\n     niyuddha-�rama-kar�ita�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Sometimes Lord K���a grew tired from fighting and lay down at the\\nbase of a tree, resting upon a bed made of soft twigs and buds and using\\nthe lap of a cowherd friend as His pillow.\"***\\n\\n     The Supreme Lord's gentleness is also seen in the pastime where\\nGoddess Lak�m� stopped Him from eating the inferior rice Sud�m� Vipra\\nbrought. Lord K���a's transcendental gentleness is also seen in these words\\nof �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.7):\\n\\n\\nv�la-vyajanam �d�ya\\n     ratna-da��a� sakh�-kar�t\\n\\n\\n     \"From her maidservant's hand Goddess Rukmi�� took a yak-hair fan with\\na jeweled handle, and then she began to worship her master by fanning\\nHim.\"***\\n\\n     The pastime where Goddess Lak�m� stopped Lord K���a from eating Sud�m�\\nVipra's rice is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.81.10):\\n\\n\\niti mu��i�. . . tat-par�\\n\\n\\n     \"After saying this, the Supreme Lord ate one plamful and was about to\\neat a second when the devoted goddess Rukmi�� took hold of His hand.\"***\\n\\n     In this pastime Lord K���a thought: \"By My mercy I have already given\\na little of My opulences to this devotee. Why should I continue to eat this\\ninferior rice?\" Even if He breaks the rules of religion, the Supreme Lord\\nis never sinful or impious. This is seen in the following words of\\nCh�ndogya Upani�ad (8.1.5):\\n\\n\\nayam �tm�pahata-p�pm�\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead is always free from sin.\"\\n\\n     This truth is also confirmed by the following words of K�rma Pur��a:\\n\\n\\nai�varya-yog�d bhagav�n\\n     viruddh�rtho 'bhidh�yate\\ntath�pi do��� parame\\n     naiv�h�ry�� samantata�\\n\\n\\n     \"Even if He breaks the rules of religion, the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead is always sinless, for He is the all-opulent supreme master.\"\\n\\n     The idea that among the Supreme lord's qualities there are some faults\\nis refuted in the following passage of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (8.8.18-19):\\n\\n\\ntatas tato n�pura-valgu-�i�jitair\\n     visarpat� hema-lateva s� babhau\\n\\n\\n     \"As she (the goddess of fortune) walked here and there, her ankle-\\nbells jingling softly, she appeared like a creeper of gold.*\\n\\n\\nvilokayant� niravadyam �tmana�\\n     pada� dhruvam c�vyabhic�ri-sad-gu�am\\ngandharva-siddh�sura-yak�a-c�ra�a-\\n     traipi��apey�di�u n�nvavindata\\n\\n\\n     \"While walking among the Gandharvas, Yak�as, asuras, Siddhas, C�ra�as,\\nand denizens of heaven, Laksmi-devi, the goddess of fortune, was\\nscrutinizingly examining them, but could not find anyone naturally endowed\\nwith all good qualities. None of them was devoid of faults, and therefore\\nshe could not take shelter of any of them.\"*\\n\\n     In this passage the word \"s�\" (she) refers to Goddess Lak�m�, \"padam\"\\nmeans \"shelter\", {.sy 168}dhruvam\" means \"eternal\", and \"avyabhic�ri-sad-\\ngu�am\" means \"the person who has eternal good qualities\".\\n\\n"
