      "sourceStartLine": 8577,
      "content": "\\n\\n     The passage continues in the next three verses of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(8.8.20-22):\\n\\n\\nn�na� tapo yasya na m�nyu-nirjayo\\n     j��na� kvacit tac ca na sa�ga-varjitam\\nka�cin mah��s tasya na k�ma-nirjaya�\\n     sa ��vara� ki� parato vyap��raya�\\n\\n\\n     \"The goddess of fortune, examining the assembly, thought in this way:\\nSomeone who has undergone great austerity has not yet conquered anger.\\nSomeone possesses knowledge, but he has not conquered material desires.\\nSomeone is a very great personality, but he cannot conquer lusty desires.\\nEven a great personality depends on something else. How, then, can he be\\nthe supreme controller?*\\n\\n\\ndharma� kvacit tatra na bh�ta-sauh�da�\\n     ty�ga� kvacit tatra na mukti-k�ra�am\\nv�rya� na pu�so 'sty aja-vega-ni�k�ta�\\n     na hi dvit�yo gu�a-sa�ga-varjita�\\n\\n\\n     \"Someone may possess full knowledge of religion but still not be kind\\nto all living entities. In someone, whether human of demigod, there may be\\nrenunciation, but that is not the cause of liberation. Someone may possess\\ngreat power and yet be unable to check the power of eternal time. Someone\\nelse may have renounced attachment to the material world, yet het cannot\\ncompare to the Supreme Personality of Godhead. TRherefore no one is\\ncompletely freed from the influence of the material modes of nature.*\\n\\n\\nkvacic cir�yur na hi ��la-ma�gala�\\n     kvacit tad apy asti na vedyam �yu�a�\\nyatrobhaya� kutra ca so 'py ama�gala�\\n     suma�gala� ka�ca na ka�k�ate hi m�m\\n\\n\\n     \"Someone may have longevity but not have auspiciousness or good\\nbehavior. Someone may have both auspiciousness and good behavior, but the\\nduration of his life is not fixed. Although such demigods as Lord �iva have\\neternal life, they have inauspicious habits like living in crematoriums.\\nAnd even if others are well qualified in all respects, they are not\\ndevotees of the Supreme Personality of Godhead.\"*\\n\\n     In this passage it is seen that no one possesses austerity or any\\nother virtue in the same degree as ther Supreme Personality of Godhead\\npossesses them. This is seen in the words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.16.30)\\nquoted above. The saintly devotees may possess austerity and the other\\nvirtues, but they possess them only imperfectly and in relatively small\\nquantities. Thus they are not equal to the Supreme Personality of Godhead.\\nIn �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 8.8.20-22 the word \"yasya\" refers to Durv�s� and the\\nsages, \"ka�cid\" refers to �ukra and his followers and also to Brahm�, Soma,\\nand their followers, \"parato vyapa�raya�\" refers to Indra and his\\nfollowers, \"sa kim ��vara� kvacit na bhuta-sauhrdam\" refers to Para�ur�ma\\nand other sages like Him, \"na mukti-karanam  ty�ga�\" refers to Mah�r�ja\\n�ibi and others like him, and \"pu�sa� v�ryam asti\" refers to K�rttav�rya\\nand others like him. The words \"aja-vega-ni�k�tam\" mean \"not free of the\\npower of time\", \"na hi dvitiyo guna-sanga-varjitah\" mean \"aside from Lord\\nK���a, no one, not even the four Kum�ras and the great �tm�r�ma sages, is\\ncompletely freed from the influence of the material modes of nature, for\\ntheir peacefulness, self-control, and other virtues are all made of the\\nillusory potency m�y�\". Even Lord �iva is not free of the material modes of\\nnature. This is described in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.88.3):\\n\\n\\n�iva� �akti-yuta� �a�vat\\n     tri-li�go gu�a-samv�ta�\\nvaik�rikas taijasa� ca\\n     tamasa� cety aha� tridh�\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord �iva is always united with his personal energy, the material\\nnature. Manifesting himself in three features in response the the\\nentreaties of nature's three modes, he thus embodies the threefold\\nprinciple of material ego in goodness, passion, and ignorance.\"***\\n\\n     Only Lord Vi��u is free of the material modes. This is described in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.88.5):\\n\\n\\nharir hi nirgu�a� s�k��t\\n     puru�a� prak�te� para�\\nsa sarva-d�g upadra���\\n     ta� bhajan nirgu�o bhavet\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord Hari, however, has no connection with the material modes. He is\\nthe Supreme Personality of Godhead, the all-seeing eternal witness, who is\\ntranscendental to material nature. One who worships Him becomes similarly\\nfree from the material modes.\"***\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 8.8.20-22 Godedess Laksmi rejects even Lord �iva.\\nThe words \"kvacin cir�yu�\" mean {.sy 168}M�rka��eya and other sages may\\nlive a long life\". The words \"��la-ma�gala\" are explained by �r�la �r�dhara\\nSv�m� in these words of his commentary: \"Good character means sense-\\ncontrol\". Hoewever, even if a person controls his senses, if he is not\\nauspicious (mangala) he is not honored in the world. Some persons like Maya\\nD�nava and others may live long lives, but they have neither good character\\nnor auspiciousness. This is because they are demons. Others, like Bali\\nMah�r�ja, may have both good character and auspiciousness, but they do not\\nhave long lives, for they are subject to death. Lord �iva is auspicious by\\nnature, and He also lives eterbnally. Still, His activities seem externally\\nto be inauspicious, for He lives in a cermatiroum and engages in seemingly\\ninauspicious activities. However, Lord Vi��u has limitless good qualities.\\nHe is very auspicious. Indeed,is form is a great treasure-house of all that\\nis auspicious. In this passage Goddess Lak�m� laments: \"Becuase His form is\\nfilled with all virtues and opulences, Lord Vi��u does not desire me, even\\nthough my form is filled with transcendental bliss and I am the giver of\\nall wealth and opuelnces. How can He not desire me, even though I love Him\\nso dearly? Even though He is supremely auspicious, He does not desire me.\"\\n\\n"
