      "content": "\\n\\n     Sometimes the Supreme Lord acts in a way contrary to the virtue of\\nbeing honest and straightforward. For example, bceomign allies with\\nHanum�n, Sugr�va, and others, three Lord was not straughtforward in His\\ndealings with V�li and others. In these situations the Lord's dishonesty is\\nall-auspicious. This is seen in the following example:\\n\\n\\nkrodho 'pi devasya vare�a tulya�\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Lord's anger is as auspicious as His blessing.\"\\n\\n     The Supreme Lord may sometimes seem to be lusty in His dealings with\\nHis consorts. However, what seems to be lust on His part is actually the\\npurest love. This is seen in the following explanation of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(1.11.35):\\n\\n\\nsa e�a nara-loke 'sminn\\n     avat�r�a� sva-m�yay�\\nreme str�-ratna-k�tastho\\n     bhagav�n pr�k�to yath�\\n\\n\\n     \"That Supreme Personality of Godhead �r� K���a, out of His causeless\\nmercy, appeared on this planet by His internal potency and enjoyed Himself\\namongst competent women as if He were engaging in mundane affairs.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"sva-m�yay�\" mean {.sy 168}with mercy to His\\ndevotees, mercy that means love, the love that makes Him wish to please\\nthem\", \"avat�r�a�\" means \"all the incarnations of the Lord descend to the\\nmaterial world because They are impelled by that mercy and love\", {.sy\\n168}str�-ratna-k�tastha�\" means \"women who with their great love bring the\\nLord under their control\", \"reme\" means \"because of that love the lord\\nenjoys pastimes with them. He does not enjoy pastimes with them because of\\nthe lust that is so well-known in the material world. Here the word {.sy\\n168}ratna\" indicates that these women are very qualified to associate with\\nthe Lord. It indicates that they have great love for Him. Although the\\nLord's pastimes with these women is very different from anything in the\\nmaterial world, these pastimes superifically seem to be like the activities\\nof the material world. This is described here by the words \"pr�k�to yath�\".\\nThus, although the Lord's pastimes may seem to be like ordinary activities\\nof the material world, the idea that He is impelled by lust is here\\nrefuted.\\n\\n"
