      "sourceLabel": "Anuccheda 150",
      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 10570,
      "content": "\\n\\n     In different situations the Lord manifests different featires of His\\ncharacter. He has four primary features, beginning with Dh�rod�tta.\\nDh�rod�tta is described in the following words of Bhakti-ras�m�ta-sindhu\\n(2.1.226):\\n\\n\\ngambh�ro vinay� k�ant�\\n     karu�a� sud��ha-vrata�\\nakatthano g��ha-garvo\\n     dh�rodatta� su-sattva-bh�t\\n\\n\\n     \"A Dhirod�tta is a person who is naturally very grave, gentle,\\nforgiving, merciful, determined, humble, highly qualified, chivalrous, and\\nphysically attractive.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a manifested these qualities whne He lifted Govardhana Hilla,\\nconversed with Indra, and enjoyed other like pastimes.\\n\\n     The Lord's feature of dh�ra-lalita is described in the following words\\nof Bhakti-ras�m�ta-sindhu (2.1.226):\\n\\n\\nvidagdho nava-t�ru�ya�\\n     parih�sa-vi��rada�\\nni�cinto dh�ra-lalita�\\n     sy�t pr�ya� preyas�-va�a�\\n\\n\\n     \"A person is called dh.ira-lalita id he is naturally very funny,\\nalways in full youthfulness, expert in joking and free from all anxieties.\\nSuch a dh�ra-lalita personality is generally found to be domesticated and\\nvery submissive to his lover.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a manifested these qualities when He enjoyed pastimes with\\nthe goddesslike vraja-gop�s.\\n\\n     The Lord's feature of dh�ra-pra��nta is described in the following\\nwords of Bhakti-ras�m�ta-sindhu (2.1.226):\\n\\n\\n�ama-prak�tika� kle�a-\\n     sahana� ca vivecaka�\\nvinay�di-gu�opeto\\n     dh�ra-��nta ud�ryate\\n\\n\\n     \"A person who is very peaceful, forbearing, considerate, and obliging\\nis called dh�ra-pra�a�ta.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a manifested these qualities when He enjoyed pastimes of\\nassociating with and protecting Mah�r�ja Yudhi��hira and other devotees.\\n\\n     The Lord's feature of dh�roddhata is described in the following words\\nof Bhakti-ras�m�ta-sindhu (2.1.226):\\n\\n\\nm�tsaryav�n aha�k�r�\\n     m�y�v� ro�a�a� cala�\\nvikatthana� ca vidvadbhir\\n     dh�roddhata ud�h�ta�\\n\\n\\n     \"A person who is very envious, prud, easily angered, restless, and\\ncomplacent is called dh�roddhata by learned schoalrs.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a manifested these qualities when He approached the demons.\\nThese qualities are manifest to punish the demons. All these different\\nfeatures are couinted among the udd�panas (secondary causes of ecstatic\\nlove).\\n\\n     Lord K���a has two castes: 1. gopa, and 2. k�atriya. His complexion is\\ndark. He is youthful. He has a host of transcendental qualities poets may\\ndescribe in many metaphors and similies. His associates are gopas and\\nmembers of other castes also. His pastimes are also counted among the\\nudd�panas (causes of ecstatic love). His pastimes are of two kinds: 1. the\\npastimes of creation, maintenance, and destruction of the material worlds,\\npastimes manifested by the m�y� potency, and 2. His pastimes that are the\\nactivities He performs with His own graceful transcendental form, pastimes\\nlike smiling, playing, dancing, and fighting, pastimes manifest by His\\ninternal potency (svar�pa-�akti). His internal potency is so called because\\nit is manifested from the bliss of His own transcendental form. The Lord's\\ninternal potency is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (3.9.23):\\n\\n\\nramay�tma-�akty� yad yat kari�yati\\n\\n\\n    \"The Supreme Lord's activities are always enacted through His internal\\npotency, Ram�, or the goddess of fortune.\"*\\n\\n     Because He is the supreme controller, the Lord may act in any way He\\npleases. His pastimes are described in these words of Ved�nta-s�tra\\n(2.1.33):\\n\\n\\nlokavat tu l�l�-kaivalyam\\n\\n\\n     \"Although they seem to be the actions of an ordinary person in the\\nmaterial world, the Lord's pastimes are all transcendental.\"\\n\\n     To enjoy pastimes the lord sometimes acts as if He were not all-\\npowerful. This is seen in the following owrds of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(8.6.17):\\n\\n\\neka eve�varas tasmin\\n     sura-k�rye sure�vara�\\nvihartu-k�mas t�n �ha\\n     samudronmathan�dibhi�\\n\\n\\n    \"Although the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the master of the\\ndemigods, was capable of performing the activities of the dmmigods by\\nHimself, He wanted to enjoy pastimes in churning the ocean. Therefore He\\nspoke as follows.\"*\\n\\n     �r�la �r�dhara Sv�m� comments:\\n\\n     \"Here the words `eka eve�vara�' mean `although He was capable of\\nperforming these activities by Himself'. \"\\n\\n     The Lord's. different castes and pastimes are described in these words\\nof Vi��u-dharmottaraPur��a:\\n\\n\\nyasy�� yasy�� yad� yonau\\n     pr�durbhavati k�ra��t\\ntad-yoni-sad��a� vatsa\\n     tad� loke vice��ate\\n\\n\\n     \"O child, in whatever species of life He incarnates, the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead acts as a member of that species would act.\\n\\n\\nsa�hartu� jagad-���na�\\n     samartho 'pi tad� n�pa\\ntad-yoni-sad��op�yair\\n     vadhy�n hi�sati y�dava\\n\\n\\n     \"O king, O descendent of Mah�r�ja Yadu, although He is always the\\nmaster of all the worlds, and although He has the power to destroy all the\\nworlds, He acts as a member of that species would act. In those\\nincarnations the Lord acts to kill the demons, demons that deserve death.\"\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 8.6.17, quoted in this anuccheda, was spoken by\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
