      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 10725,
      "content": "\\n\\n     The activities the Supreme Personality of Godhead performs in His\\ntranscendental form are of two kinds: 1. activities of power and opulence\\n(ai�varya-ce���), and 2. activities of charming sweetness (m�dhurya-ce���).\\nBecause His devotees love Him so much, the Lord manifests His great\\nsweetness before them. Filled with wonder and bliss, �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�\\nexplains in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.15.19):\\n\\n\\neva� nig��h�tma-gati� sva-m�yay�\\n     gop�tmajatva� caritair vi�ambayan\\nreme ram�-l�lita-p�da-pallavo\\n     gr�myai� sama� gr�mya-vad ��a-ce�tita�\\n\\n\\n     \"In this way the Supreme Lord, whose soft lotus feet are personally\\nattended by the goddess of fortune, concealed His transcendental opulences\\nby His internal potency and acted like the son of a cowherd. Yet even while\\nenjoying like a village boy in the company of other village residents, He\\noften exhibited feats only God could perform.\"*\\n\\n     This verse means: \"The Supreme Lord, whose soft lotus feet are\\npersonally attended by the goddess of fortune when He manifests His form as\\nLord N�r�ya�a, stayed amongst the people of Vraja.\"\\n\\n     It is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.18.27):\\n\\nnir�k�ya tad-vapur alam ambare carat. . .haladhara ��ad atrasat\\n\\n     \"When Lord Balar�ma, who carries the plow weapon, saw the gigantic\\nbody of the demon as he moved swiftly in the sky-with his blazing eyes,\\nfiery hair, terrible teeth reaching toward his scowling brows, and an\\namazing effulgence generated by his armlets, crown and earrings-the Lord\\nseemed to become a little frightened.\"***\\n\\n     In this pastime the Lord's sweetness is manifest because He pretends\\nto be an ordinary human beings. In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.15.19 the word\\n\"m�yay�\" means \"by My mercy\". The Lord acts like an ordinary human beings\\nso the people of Vraja will think He is one of them. The Lord acts in this\\nway because He loves His devotees, as He has declared in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(9.4.68):\\n\\n\\ns�dhavo h�daya� mahyam. . .\\n\\n\\n    \"The pure devotee is always within the core of My heart, and I am\\nalways in the heart of the pure devotee. My devotees do not know anything\\nelse but Me, and I do not know anyone else but them.\"*\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.15.19 the word {.sy 168}nig��h�tma-gati�\"\\nmeans \"concealing His supreme opulence and acting like an ordinary human\\nbeing of this world\", \"gop�tmajatvam\" means \"like the son of an ordinary\\ncowherd man\", \"caritair vi�ambayan reme\" means \"imitating an ordinary\\ngopa's activities, He enjoyed pastimes\". The word \"ram�-l�lita-p�da-\\npallava�\" indicates that the Lord does not desire to enjoy the pastime\\nwhere the goddess of fortune serves His feet as much as He desires to enjoy\\nHis pastimes with the people of Vraja. The pastimes with Vraja's peoples\\nare described here in the words \"gr�myai� sama�\" which mean \"pastimes with\\nvillage boys\". The Lord's pastimes with the people of Vraja are His most\\nimportant pastimes. The pastimes where He displays His power and opulence\\nare not His most important pastimes. An example of these pastimes is seen\\nin the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.9.6):\\n\\n\\nsa�j�ta-kopa� sphurit�run�dhara�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Being very angry and biting His reddish lips with His teeth, K���a,\\nwith false tears in His eyes, broke the container of yogurt with a piece of\\nstone. Then He entered a room and began to eat the freshly churned butter\\nin a solitary place.\"*\\n\\n     Even though He was in a solitary place, Lord K���a still acted like an\\nordinary child. Another example of Lord K���a acting as if He were an\\nordinary human beings is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.12.27):\\n\\n\\nt�n v�k�ya k���a�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"K���a saw that all the cowherd boys, who did not know anyone but Him\\nas their Lord, had now gone out of His hand and were helpless, having\\nentered like straws into the fire of the abdomen of Agh�sura, who was death\\npersonified. It was intolerable for K���a to be separated from His friends\\nthe cowherd boys. Therefore, as if seeing that this had been arranged by\\nHis internal potency, K���a was momentarily struck with wonder and unsure\\nof what to do.\"*\\n\\n     In this pastime Lord K���a became distressed, thinking that the\\ncowherd boys were now under the control of fate. In this pastime the Lord\\nthought ans acted as if He were an ordinary human being. When they describe\\nthese activities of the Lord, the great sages become filled with wonder. In\\ndescribing the battle with Jar�sandha, �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m� says in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.50.29):\\n\\n\\nsthity-udbhav�nta� bhuvana-trayasya ya�\\n     sam�hite 'nanta-gu�a� sva-l�lay�\\nna tasya citra� para-pak�a-nigrahas\\n     tath�pi marty�nuvidhasya var�yate\\n\\n\\n    \"For Him who orchestrates the creation, maintenace, and destruction of\\nthe three worlds, and who posseses unlimited spiritual qualities, it is\\nhardly amazing that He subdues an opposing party. Still, when the Lord does\\nso, imitating human behavior, sages glorify His acts.\"***\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.15.19 quoted above, the word {.sy 168}��a\"\\nmeans \"the Lord's pastime potency, which with its own perfect power\\nestablishes the extraordinary transcendental pastimes of the Lord, who\\nyearns to taste the nectar of pastimes\", and the word \"ce��itam\" means \"the\\nLord's pastimes, which sometimes seem to be oprdinary activities and\\nsometimes are wonderful activities impossible for others to perform\". An\\nexample of the extraordinary and ordinary nature of the Lord's pastimes is\\nseen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.69.37):\\n\\n\\nathov�ca h���ke�a�\\n     n�rada� prahasann iva\\nyoga-m�yodaya� v�k�ya\\n     m�nu��m �yu�o gatim\\n\\n\\n    \"Thus having seen this display (where Lord K���a was present in 16,000\\npalaces simultaneously) of the Lord's Yogam�y�, N�rada mildly laughed and\\nthen addressed Lord H���ke�a, who was adopting the bahvior of a human\\nbeings.\"***\\n\\n     The extraordinary and ordinary nature of the Lord's pastimes is also\\nseen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.36-37):\\n\\n\\nyady eva� tarhi vyadeh�ty\\n     ukta� sa bhagav�n hari�\\nvyadatt�vyahatai�varya�\\n     kr���-m�nuja-b�laka�\\n\\ns� tatra dad��e vi�va�\\n\\n\\n     \"Mother Ya�od� challenged K���a, `If You have not eaten earth, then\\nopen Your mouth wide.' When challenged by His mother in this way, K���a,\\nthe son of Nanda Mah�r�ja and Ya�od�, to exhibit pastimes like a human\\nchild, opened His mouth. Although the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\\nK���a, who is full of all opulences, did not disturb His mother's parental\\naffection, His opulence was automatically displayed, for K���a's opulence\\nis never lost at any stage, but is manifest at the proper time.*\\n     \"When K���a opened His mouth wide by the order of Mother Ya�od�, she\\nsaw within His mouth all moving and nonmoving entities, outer space, and\\nall directions, along with mountains, islands, oceans, the surface of the\\nearth, the blowing wind, fire, the moon, and stars. She saw the planetary\\nsystems, water, light, air, sky, and creation by transformation of\\naka�k�ra. She also saw the senses, the mind, sense perception, and the\\nthree qualities goodness, passion, and ignorance. She saw the time allotted\\nfor the living entities, she saw natural instinct and the reactions of\\nkarma, and she saw desires and different varieties of bodies, moving and\\nnonmoving. Seeing all these aspects of the cosmic manifestation, along with\\nherself and V�nd�vana-dh�ma, she became doubtful and fearful of her son's\\nnature.\"*\\n\\n     The Supreme Lord's sweetness, as was previously described in these\\npages, is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.35):\\n\\n\\nn�ha� bhak�itav�n amba\\n     sarve mithy�bhi�a�sina�\\nyadi satya-giras tarhi\\n     samak�a� pa�ya me mukham\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord �r� K���a replied: My dear mother, I have never eaten dirt. All\\nMy friends complaining against Me ar liars. If You think they are being\\ntruthful, you can directly look in My mouth and examine it.\"*\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.8.36 quoted above the word {.sy\\n168}avyahatai�varya�\" shows that the Supreme Lord's pastimes are arranged\\nby His pastime potency (l�l�-�akti). It is His pastime potency that\\nincreases Yasaoda's motherly affection for K���a and fills her with awe and\\nwonder. It is the lord's pastime potency that turns the Lord's anxious lie\\n\"I have never eaten dirt\" into the truth. In His D�modara pastime as long\\nas the Lord to not agree to be bound with rope, the rope to bind Him was\\nalways too short by the measurement of two fingers. This is described in\\nthe following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.9.15):\\n\\n\\ntad d�ma. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When mother Ya�od� was trying to bind the offending child, she saw\\nthat the binding rope was short by a distance the width of two fingers.\\nThus she brought another rope to join to it.\"*\\n\\n     However, when Mother Ya�od� became exhausted, Lord K���a agrred to be\\nbound. Then the rope was not longer two fingers short. This is described in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.9.18):\\n\\n\\nsva-m�tu� svinna-g�tr�y�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Because of mother Ya�od�'s hard labor, her whole body became covered\\nwith perspiration, and the flowers and comb were falling from her hair.\\nWhen child K���a saw His mother thus fatigued, He became merciful to her\\nand agreed to be bound.\"*\\n\\n     In the same way with His merciful glance Lord K���a revived His gopa\\nfriends after they fainted by drinking the poisoned water of K�liya Lake,\\nand in the same way He swalloed a forest fire and performed many other\\nextraordinary activities. The words \"kr���-m�nuja-b�laka�\" (who enjoyed\\npastimes as if He were a human child) is echoed in these owrds of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.16.68):\\n\\n\\nkr�d�-m�nu�a-r�pi�a�\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead was manifesting a humanlike form\\nto enjoy His pastimes.\"***\\n\\n     It is also said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.16.60):\\n\\n\\nk�rya-m�nu�a�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead was acting the role of a human\\nbeing.\"***\\n\\n     In this way �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.15.19 is explained. That verse was\\nspoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
