      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 11218,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Sometimes great obstacles also become udd�panas. The following\\nwexample is seen in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.53.20-21):\\n\\n\\n�rutvaitad bhagav�n r�mo\\n     vipak��ya-n�podyamam\\nk���a� caika� gata� hartu�\\n     kany�� kalaha-�a�kita�\\n\\nbalena mahat� s�rdha�\\n     bhr�t�-sneha-paripluta�\\n\\n\\n    \"When Lord Balar�ma heard about these preparations of the inimical\\nkings and how Lord K���a had set off alone to steal the bride, He feared\\nthat a fight would ensue. Immersed in affection for His brother, He hurried\\nto Ku��ina with a mighty army.\"***\\n\\n     The Lord's body being anointed with dust and mud in His pastimes act\\nas udd�panas in the same way for the devotees in v�tsalya-rasa. The\\nobstacles presented by elder relatives and others also act as udd�panas for\\nthe devotees in ���g�ra-rasa. In the same way various kinds of obstacles\\nact as udd�panas in the five primary and seven secondary rasas. The\\nvyabhic�r�s (disturbing symptoms of ecstatic love) are also manifest in the\\nvarious rasas, as described in the following words of Bhakti-ras�m�ta-\\nsindhu (4.7.14):\\n\\n\\nam� pa�caiva ��nt�dy�\\n     harer bhakti-ras� mat��\\ne�u h�s�daya� pr�yo\\n     bibhrati vyabhac�rit�m\\n\\n\\n     \"The five primary bhakti-rasas beginning with ��nta-rasa, and the\\nseven secondary bhakti-rasas beginning with h�sa-rasa all manifest the\\nvyabhic�r�s (disturbing symptoms of ecstatic love).\"\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.53.20-21) quoted in this anuccheda was spoken by\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
