      "content": "\\n\\n     In this way the udd�panas were described. Among the udd�panas those\\nmanifested in V�nd�vana are the most exalted. This is so because in\\nV�nd�vana Lord K���a is the sole object of the highest spiritual love. This\\nis described in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.11.36):\\n\\n\\nv�nd�vana� govardhanam. . .\\n\\n\\n    \"O King Par�k�it, when R�ma and K���a saw V�nd�vana, Govardhana, and\\nthe banks of the River Yamun�, They both enjoyed great pleasure.\"*\\n\\n     Speaking to Lord Balar�ma, Lord K���a described the glories of\\nV�nd�vana in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.15.5):\\n\\n\\naho am� deva-var�mar�rcita�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O greatest of Lords, just see how these trees are bowing their heads\\nat Your lotus feet, which are worshipable by the immortal demigods. lhe\\ntrees are offering You their fruits and flowers to eradicate the dark\\nignorance that has caused their birth as trees.\"***\\n\\n     The great devotees who reside in V�nd�vana are praised in these words\\nof �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.14.34):\\n\\n\\ntad bh�ri-bh�gyam iha janma. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"I am most humbly praying at Your lotus feet for You to please give me\\nany sort of birth within this V�nd�vana forest so that I may be able to be\\nfavored by the dust of the feet of some of the devotees of V�nd�vana. Even\\nif I am given the chance to grow just as the humble grass in this land,\\nthat will be a glorious birth for me. But if I am not so fortunate to take\\nbirth within the forest of V�nd�vana, I beg to be allowed to take birth\\noutside the immediate area of V�nd�vana so that when the devotees go out\\nthey will walk over me. Even that would be a great fortune for me. I am\\njust aspiring for a birth in which I will be smeared by the dust of Your\\ndevotees' feet. I can see that everyone here is simply full of K���a\\nconsciousness. They do not know anything but Mukunda. All the Vedas are\\nindeed searching after the lotus feet of K���a.\"*\\n\\n     The great devotees who reside in V�nd�vana are also praised in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.61):\\n\\n\\n�s�m aho cara�a-re�u-ju��m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The gop�s of V�nd�vana have given up the association of their\\nhusbands, sons, and other family members, who are very difficult to give\\nup, andf they have forsaken the path of chastity to take shelter of the\\nlotus feet of Mukunda, K���a, which one should search for by Vedic\\nknowledge. Oh, let me be fortunate enough to be one of the bushes,\\ncreepers, or herbs in V�nd�vana, because the gop�s trample them and bless\\nthem with the dust of their feet.\"*\\n\\n     The great devotees who reside in V�nd�vana are also praised in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.21.10):\\n\\n\\nvrnd�vana� sakhi bhuvo vitanoti k�rti�. . .\\n\\n\\n      \"O friend, V�nd�vana is spreading the glory of the earth, having\\nobtained the treasure of the lotus feet of K���a, the son of Devak�. The\\npeacocks dance madly when they hear Govinda's flute, and when other\\ncreatures see them from the hilltops, they all become stunned.\"***\\n\\n     The pastimes Lord K���a enjoys in V�nd�vana are the best of all His\\npastimes. This is described in the following words of the Trailokya-\\nsammohana Tantra, �r�mad-a���da��k�ara-prast�va:\\n\\n\\nsanti tasya mah�-bh�ga\\n     avat�r�� sahasra�a�\\nte��� madhye 'vat�r����\\n     b�latvam ati-durlabham\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead has thousands and thousands of\\nglorious incarnations. Amongst them all His incarnation as a child is the\\nmost glorious and the most rare.\"\\n\\n     Here the word \"b�la\" (child) means \"until the age of sixteen\". In the\\nHari-l�l�-��k�-sm�ti it is said:\\n\\n\\ngarbhastha-sad��o j�eya\\n     ���am�d vatasr�c chi�u�\\nb�la� c��o�a��d var��t\\n     pauga��a� ceti procyate\\n\\n\\n     \"From the time of living in the womb until the age of age a child is\\ncalled `�i�u'. Up to thw age of sixteen a child is called `b�la' or\\n`pauga��a'.\"\\n\\n\\n     The childhood of the Supreme Personality of Godhead is glorified in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.46-47):\\n\\n\\nnanda� kim akarod brahman\\n     �reya eva� mahodayam\\nya�od� ca mah�-bh�g�\\n     p�pau y�sy�� stana� hari�\\n\\n\\n     \"Having heard of the great fortune of Mother Ya�od�, Par�k�it Mah�r�ja\\ninquired from �ukadeva Gosv�m�: O learned br�hma�a, mother Ya�od�'s breast\\nmilk was sucked by the Supreme Personality of Godhead. What past auspicious\\nactivities did she and Nanda Mah�r�ja perform to achieve such perfection in\\necstatic love?*\\n\\n\\npitarau n�nvavindeta�\\n     k���od�r�rbhakehitam\\ng�yanty ady�pi kavayo\\n     yal loka-�amal�paham\\n\\n\\n     \"Although K���a was so pleased with Vasudeva and Devak� that He\\ndescended as their son, they could not enjoy K���a's magnanimous childhood\\npastimes, which are so great that simply chanting about them vanquishes the\\ncontamination of the material world. Nanda Mah�r�ja and Ya�od�, however,\\nenjoyed these pastimes fully, and therefore their position is always better\\nthan that of Vasudeva and Devak�.\"*\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam's Eleventh Canto, after describign Lord K���a's\\npastimes in a general way, and after explaining that Lord K���a's pastimes\\narouse devotion (bhakty-udd�pana) in the hearers, it is said that Lord\\nK���a's childhood pastimes are the best of His pastimes. There it is said\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 11.31.28):\\n\\n\\nittha� harer bhagavato rucir�vat�ra-\\n     v�ry��i b�la-carit�ni ca �antam�ni\\nanyatra ceha ca �rut�ni g��an manu�yo\\n     bhakti� par�� paramaha�sa-gatau labheta\\n\\n\\n    \"The all-auspoicious exploits of the all-attractive incarnations of\\nLord �r� K���a, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, and also the pastimes\\nHe performed as a child, are described in this �r�mad-Bh�gavatam and in\\nother scriptures. Anyone who clearly chants these descriptions of His\\npastimes will attain transcendental loving service unto Lord K���a, who is\\nthe goal of all perfect sages.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a's exalted V�nd�vana pastimes are of many kinds. For\\nexample, there are the pastimes where He reveals His power and opulence\\n(ai�varya), such as the pastime where He shows that many millions of\\nuniverses are present in His eternal spiritual form full of knowledge and\\nlimitless bliss. There are also his pastimes of compassion (k�ru�ya), such\\nas He pastime of showing compassion to P�tan� and allowing her to become\\nlike His own mother. Then there are also His pastimes of sweetness\\n(m�dhurya), which are described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.8.22):\\n\\n\\nt�v a�ghri-yugmam anuk��ya sar�s�pantau. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When K���a and Balar�ma, with the strength of Their legs, crawled in\\nthe muddy places created in Vraja by cow dung and cow urines, Their\\ncrawling resembled the crawling of serpents, and the sound of Their ankle\\nbells was very charming. Very much pleased by the spound of other people's\\nankle bells, They used to follow these people as if going to Their mothers,\\nbut when They saw that these were other people, They became afraid and\\nreturned to Their real mothers, Ya�od� and Rohi��.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.8.29):\\n\\n\\nvats�n mu�can kvacid asamaye. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Our dear friend Ya�od�, your son sometimes comes to our houses before\\nthe milking of the cows and releases the calves, and when the master of the\\nhouse becomes angry, your son merely smiles. Sometimes He devises some\\nprocess by which He steals palatavble curd, butter, and milk, which He then\\neats and drinks. When the monkeys assemble, He divides it with them, and\\nwhen the monkeys have their bellies so full that they won't take any more,\\nHe breaks the pots. Sometimes, if He gets no opportunity to stral butter\\nand milk from a house, He will be angry at the householders, and for His\\nrevenge He will agitate the small children by pinching them. Then, when\\nthey begin crying, K���a will go away.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.11.7):\\n\\n\\ngop�bhi� stobhito 'n�tyat. . .\\n\\n\\n    \"The gop�s would say: `If You dance, my dear K���a, then I shall give\\nYou half a sweetmeat.' By saying these words or by clapping their hands,\\nall the gop�s encouraged K���a in different ways. At such times, although\\nHe was the supremely powerful Personality of Godhead, He would smile and\\ndance according to their desire, as if He were a wooden doll in their\\nhands. Sometimes He would sing very loudly, at their bidding. In this way,\\nK���a came completely undere the control of the gop�s.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.11.39-40):\\n\\n\\nkvacid v�dayato ve�um. . .\\n\\n\\n    \"Sometimes K���a and Balar�ma would play on Their flutes, sometimes\\nThey would throw ropes and stones devised for getting fruits from the\\ntrees, sometimes They would throw only stones, and sometimes, Their ankle-\\nbells tinkling, They would play football with fruits like bael and amalaki.\\nSometimes They would cover Themselves with blankets and imitate cows and\\nbulls and fight with one another, oraring loudly, and sometimes They would\\nimitae the voices of the animals. In this way They enjoyed sporting,\\nexactly like two ordinary human children.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.12.1):\\n\\n\\nkvacid van���ya mano dadhad vraj�t. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O King, one day K���a decided to take His breakfast as a picnic in\\nthe forest. Having risen early in the morning, He blew His bugle made of\\nhorn and woke all the cowherd boys and calves with its beautiful sound.\\nThen K���a and the boys, keeping their respective groups of calves before\\nthem, proceeded from Vrajabh�mi to the forest.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.15.10-12):\\n\\n\\nkvacid g�yati g�yatsu. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Sometimes the honeybees in V�nd�vana became so mad with ecstasy that\\nthey closed their eyes and began to sing. Lord K���a, moving along the\\nforest Path with His cowherd boyfriends and Baladeva, would then respond to\\nthe bees by imitating their singing while His friends sang about His\\npastimes. Sometimes Lord K���a would imitate the chattering of a parrot,\\nsometimes, with a sweet voice, the call of a cuckoo, and sometimes the\\ncooing of swans. Sometimes He vigorously imitated the dancing of a peacock,\\nmaking His cowherd boyfriends laugh. Sometimes, with a voice as deep as the\\nrumbling of clouds, He would call out with great affection the names of the\\nanimals who had wandered far from the herd, thus enchanting the cows and\\nthe cowherd boys.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.15.42):\\n\\n\\nta� gorajas-churita-kuntala-baddha-barha. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a's hair, powdered with the dust raised by the cows, was\\ndecorated with a peacock feather and forest flowers. The Lord glanced\\ncharmingly and smiled beautifully, playing upon His flute while His\\ncompanions chanted His glories. The gop�s, all together, came forward to\\nmeet Him, their eyes very eager to see Him.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.18.10):\\n\\n\\nkrs�asya n�tyata� kecit. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"As K���a danced, some of the boys accompanied Him by singing, and\\nothers by playing flutes, hand cymbals and buffalo horns, while still\\nothers praised His dancing.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.20.26):\\n\\n\\ndhenavo manda-g�minya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The cows had to move slowly because of their weighty milk bags, but\\nthey quickly ran to the Supreme Personality of Godhead as soon as He called\\nthem, their affection for Him causing their udders to become wet.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.21.7):\\n\\n\\nak�a�vat�� phalam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The cowherd girls said: O friends, those eyes that see the beautiful\\nfaces of the sons of Mah�r�ja Nanda are certainly fortunate. As these two\\nsons enter the forest, surrounded by Their friends, driving the cows before\\nThem, They hold Their flutes to Their mouths and glance lovingly upon the\\nresidents of V�nd�vana. For those who have eyes, we think there is no\\ngreater object of vision.\"*\\n\\n.mt 3\\n.mb 5\\n.rm 6.5 in Set Right Margin to 6.5 in.\\n.rm 65\\n..\\n      Select font 1\\n.fa times to Times Roman type style.\\n.lh 1.25 em\\n.ch 12 pt\\n\\n.fa timesitl\\n.lh 1.25 em\\n.ch 12 pt\\n..\\n\\n.fa clarendo\\n.ch 12 pt\\n..\\n\\n.fa helvetic\\n.ch 16 pt\\n..\\n\\n.fa clarendo\\n.ch 16.2 pt\\n..\\n\\n.fa clarendo\\n.ch 23 pt\\n..\\n\\n.. Lower case Italicized\\n.de  {.px -1.2 en}{.py +0.23 em}.{.py -.23 em}\\n.. ^D - . below letters n - h     -    normal - italic\\n.de  {.px -1. en}{.py +.23 em}.{.py -.23 em}\\n...de  {.px -1 en}{.py -.3 em}'{.fn}{.px +.1 en}{.py +.3 em}\\n.. ^T - ' on S\\n.de [pic] {.ru .28 em, .055 em, -.6 en, -1.38 en}\\n.. ^A - - above long 'a' - 'u' sound  'i non-italic\\n.de  {.ru .28 em, .055 em, -.40 en, -1.40 en}\\n..^E - - above long 'i'\\n.de  {.px -.6 em}{.py -.65 em}.{.py +.65 em}\\n.. ^V - . above 'm'\\n..\\n.de  {.px -.5 en}{.py -.7 en}{.fa clarendo}{.ch .5 em}{.sy\\n180}{.ch}{.fa}{.py +.7 en}\\n..\\n.. Upper case Italicized\\n.de  {.px -.8 en}{.py -.73 en}{.fa clarendo}{.sy 180}{.fa}{.px +.2 en}{.py\\n+.73 en}\\n..de  {.ru .06 em, .13 em, -.25 em, -2.05 en}\\n.. ^T - ' on S\\n..\\n.. Lower case non-Italics\\n.. ^A - - over long 'i'\\n.de  {.ru .28 em, .055 em, -1 en, -1.40 en}\\n.. ^Q - - above long 'a' - 'u' sound\\n.de  {.lf .7 em}{.up .65 em}.{.dn}\\n.. ^P - . above 'm'\\n..\\n.. Upper case non-Italicized\\n.de  {.px -.8 en}{.py -.7 en}{.fa clarendo}{.sy 180}{.fa}{.px +.1 en}{.py\\n+.7 en}\\n..{.ru .06 em, .13 em, -.35 em, -1.95 en}\\n.. ^U - ' on S\\n..\\n.de - -{.lf .15 em}-{.lf .15 em}-\\n.. long '-'\\n..\\n.hy\\n.lh 1.25 em\\n.bd off\\n.pn 278\\n.op\\n.oj on\\n\\n.hee #                           Srila Jiva Gosvami's\\n.heo                  Sri Priti-sandarbha                       #\\n\\n     \"The cowherd girls said: O friends, those eyes that see the beautiful\\nfaces of the sons of Mah�r�ja Nanda are certainly fortunate. As these two\\nsons enter the forest, surrounded by Their friends, driving the cows before\\nThem, They hold Their flutes to Their mouths and glance lovingly upon the\\nresidents of V�nd�vana. For those who have eyes, we think there is no\\ngreater object of vision.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.23.22):\\n\\n\\n�y�ma� hira�ya-paridhim. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"His complexion was dark blue and His garment golden. Wearing a\\npeacock feather, colored minerals, sprigs of flower buds, and a garland of\\nforest flowers and leaves, He was dressed just like a dramatic dancer. He\\nrested one hand upon the shoulder of a friend and with the other twirled a\\nlotus. Lilies graced His ears, His hair hung down over His cheeks, and His\\nlotuslike face was smiling.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.29.1):\\n\\n\\nbhagav�n api t� r�tr��\\n\\n\\n     \"Sr� Badaraya�i said: Sr� K���a is the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\\nfull in all opulences, yet upon seeing those autumn nights scented with\\nblossoming jasmine flowers, He turned His mind toward loving affairs. To\\nfulfill His purposes He employed His internal potency.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a's sweetness is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.35.2):\\n\\n\\nv�ma-b�hu-k�ta-v�ma-kapola�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When Mukunda vibrates the flute He has placed to His lips, stopping\\nits holes with His tender fingers, He rests His left cheek on His left arm\\nand makes His eyebrows dance. At that time the demigoddesses traveling in\\nthe sky with their husbands, the Siddhas, become amazed. As those ladies\\nlisten, they are embarrassed to find their minds yeilding to the pursuit of\\nlusty desires, and in their distress they are unaware that the belts of\\ntheir garments are loosening.\"***\\n\\n     What more need be said? Devotees who in their hearts love the Lord\\nwill easily understand all these descriptions.\\n\\n     Amongst the bh�vas manifest in the heart are the anubh�vas, which will\\nnow be discussedf. The anubh�vas are of two kinds: 1. udbh�svara-anubh�vaa,\\nand 2. s�ttvika-anubh�vas. The udbh�svaras are anubh�vas manifest as\\nexternal activities. They are described in these words of Bhakti-ras�m�ta-\\nsindhu (2.2.2):\\n\\n\\nn�tya� vilu�hita� g�na�\\n     kro�anam anumo�anam\\nhu�k�ro j�mbhana� �v�sa-\\n     bh�m� lok�nupek�it�\\nl�l�-sravo '��a-h�sa� ca\\n     gh�r�a-hikk�dayo 'pi ca\\n\\n\\n     \"The udbh�svaras are dancing, rolling on the ground, singing very\\nloudly, stretching the body, crying loudly, yawning, breathing very\\nheavily, negvlecting the presence of others, drooling, laughing like a\\nmadman, wheeling the head, and belching.\"*\\n\\n     The sattvika-anubhavas are ecstatic symptoms that come from the heart.\\nThey are described in these words of Bhakti-ras�m�ta-sindhu (2.3.16):\\n\\n\\nte stambha-sveda-rom��c��\\n     svara-bhedo 'tha vepathu�\\nvaivar�yam a�ru pralaya\\n     ity a��au s�ttvik�� sm�t��\\n\\n\\n     \"The eight s�ttvika-bh�vas are becoming stunned, perspiring, standing\\nof the hairs on the body, faltering of the voice, trembling of the body,\\nchanging of bodily colors, shedding of tears, and devastation.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"pralaya\" (devastation) means \"when all\\nactivities are lost\". This means \"when, for the sake of love for the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead, all external material activities are lost\".\\nIn this state internal spiritual activities for the sake of the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead are not lost. These internal devotional activities\\nare described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (3.2.4-6):\\n\\n\\nsa muh�rtam abh�t t�����\\n     k�����ghri-sudhay� bh��am\\nt�vre�a bhakti-yogena\\n     nimagna� s�dhu nirv�ta�\\n\\n�anakair bhagaval-lok�n\\n     n�loka� punar �gata�\\n\\n\\n     \"For a moment he remained dead silent, and his body did not move. He\\nbecame absorbed in the nectar of remembering the Lord's lotus feet in\\ndevotional ecstasy, and he appeared to be going increasingly deeper into\\nthat ecstasy.*\\n     \"It was so observed by Vidura that Uddhava had all the transcendental\\nbodily changes due to total ecstasy, and he was trying to wipe away tears\\nof separation from his eyes. Thus Vidura could understand that Uddhava had\\ncompletely assimilated extensive love for the Lord.*\\n     \"The great devotee Uddhava soon came back from the abode of the Lord\\nto the human plane.\"*\\n\\n     These internal devotional activities are also described in these words\\nof the Garu�a Pur��a:\\n\\n\\nj�grat-svapna-su�upte�u\\n     yoga-sthasya ca yogina�\\ny� k�cin manaso v�tti�\\n     s� bhaved acyut��ray�\\n\\n\\n     \"During wakefulness, dream, and dreamless sleep a yog� situated in\\nyoga keeps the activities of his mind fixed always on the infallible\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead.\"\\n\\n     In that condition of rapt meditation on the Lord one can understand\\nthe different tastes of the different rasas.\\n\\n     Now the sa�c�ri-bh�vas, which are also called vyabhic�ri-bh�vas will\\nbe discussed. The 33 vyabhic�r�s are described in these words of Bhakti-\\nras�m�ta-sindhu (2.4.1-2, and 4-6):\\n\\n\\nsanc�rayanti bh�vasya gatim. . .\\n . . .vi�e�e��bhimukhyena\\n     caranti sth�yinam prati. . .\\n\\nnirvedo 'tha vi��do\\n     dainya� gl�ni-�ramo ca mada-garvau\\n�a�k�-tr�s�veg�\\n     unm�d�pasm�t� tatha vy�dhi�\\n\\nmoho m�tir �lasya�\\n     j��ya� vr���vahitth� ca\\nsm�tir atha vitarka-cint�-\\n     mati-dh�tayo ha�sa utsukatva� ca\\n\\naugry�mar��s�ya�\\n     c�palya� caiva nidr� ca\\nsuptir bodha it�me\\n     bh�v� vyabhic�ri�a� sam�khy�t��\\n\\n\\n     \"There are some bodily symptoms which express overwhelming ecstatic\\nlove (vyabhic�r�-bh�va). They are counted at 33 as follows: disappointment,\\nlamentation, humility, guilt, fatigue, intoxication, pride, doubt,\\napprehension, intense emotion, madness, forgetfulness, disease, confusion,\\ndeath, laziness, inertness, bashfulness, concealment, remembrance\\nargumentativeness, anxiety, thoughtfulness, endurance, happiness,\\neagerness, violence, haughtiness, envy, impudence, dizziness, sleepiness,\\nand alertness.\"*\\n\\n     To understand the characteristics of all these one may look in the\\nUjjvala-n�lama�i. Here the word \"tr�sa\" (apprehension) means \"when the\\ndevotees in v�tsalya-rasa and the other rasas become apprehensive that\\ntheir association with K���a will become broken\". \"Nidr�\" (sleepiness) here\\nmeans \"with a heart free of cares, in a dream the devotee meets K���a\".\\n\"�rama\" (fatigue) here means {.sy 168}fatigue attained by engaging in\\nblissful activities for K���a's sake\". \"Alasyam\" (laziness) here means {.sy\\n168}unwillingness to perform actions that have no relation to Lord K���a\".\\n\"Bodha\" (alertness) here means \"alert to see Lord K���a\". These 33\\nvyabhicaris are not material, for they are beyond the modes of material\\nnature. This is so because the bring love for the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead (bhagavat-pr�ti). Together these anubh�vas are part of the rasas of\\nlove for the Supreme Personality of Godhead. They are described in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (11.3.31-32):\\n\\n\\nsmaranta� sm�rayanta� ca\\n     mitho 'ghaugha-hara� harim\\nbhakty� sa�j�tay� bhakty�\\n     bibhraty utpulak�� tanum\\n\\n\\n     \"The devotees of the Lord constantly discuss the glories of the\\nPersonality of Godhead among themselves. Thus they constantly remember the\\nLord and remind one another of His qualities and pastimes. In this way, by\\ntheir devotion to the principles of bhakti-yoga, the devotees please the\\nPersonality of Godhead, who takes away from them everything inauspicious.\\nBeing purified of all impediments, the devotees awaken to pure love of\\nGodhead, and thus, even within this world, their spiritualized bodies\\nexhibit symptoms of transcendental ecstasy, such as standing of the bodily\\nhairs on end.***\\n\\n\\nkvacid rudanty acyuta-cintay� kvacid\\n     dhasanti nandanti vadanty alaukik��\\nn�tyanti g�yanty anu��layanty aja�\\n     bhavanti t����� par�m etya nirv�t��\\n\\n\\n     \"Having achieved love of Godhead, the devotees sometimes cry out loud,\\nabsorbed in thought of the infallible Lord. Sometimes they laugh, feel\\ngreat pleasure, speak out loud to the Lord, dance, or sing. Such devotees,\\nhavign transcended material, conditional life, sometimes imitate the unborn\\nSupreme by acting out His pastimes. And sometimes, achieving His personal\\naudience, they remain peaceful and silent.\"***\\n\\n     In these two verses Lord Hari is the �lambana (primary impetus for\\necstatic love, remembering Lord Hari is the udd�pana (secondary impetus for\\necstatic love), reminding one another of Lord Hari and other like\\ndevotional activities are the udbh�svaras (external activities of ecstatic\\nlove), the standing up of the body's hairs is one of the s�ttvika-bh�vas,\\nand the activities beginning with being rapt in thought of the Lord are the\\nsancari-bhavas. When devotion is awakened in this way sthayi-bhava\\n(continuous ecstasy) is manifest. The words \"And sometimes, achieving His\\npersonal audience, they (the devotees) remain peaceful and silent\" describe\\nsamvalana (the situation when all the elements of rasa are joined\\ntogether). Here the word \"param\" means \"the supreme object of rasa\". The\\nrasas of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead are five. These five\\nare ��nta-rasa (neutrality), d�sya-rasa (servitorship), sakhya-rasa\\n(friendship), v�tsalya-rasa (paternal love), and ���g�ra-rasa (conjugal\\nlove). Because they manifest sth�yi-bh�va and because they are eternal and\\nunchanging, always manifest, these five rasas are said to be the primary\\nrasas. Sometimes amnifest among these fiver primary rasas are other rasas,\\nrasas that begin with adbhuta (wonder). Because they are not always\\nmanifest, these rasas are said to be secondary rasas. The primary rasas are\\ndescribed in these words:\\n\\n\\nmadhure�a sam�payet\\n\\n\\n     \"The five rasas culminating in ���g�ra-rasa are the primary rasas.\"\\n\\n     Certain combinations of the secondary rasas are considered ras�bh�sa\\n(incompatible mixtures of rasa).\\n\\n     Now the secondary rasas will be discussed. The secondary rasasa, which\\nbegin with adbhuta (wonder) increase the devotees' love for the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead. They are described in these words:\\n\\n\\nadbhuto h�sya-v�rau ca\\n     raudro bh��a�a ity api\\nb�bhatsa� karu�a� ceti\\n     gau��� sapta ras�� sm�t��\\n\\n\\n     \"The seven kinds of indirect ecstasies of devotional service are known\\nas laughing, astonishment, chivalry, compassion, anger, dread, and\\nghastilness.\"*\\n\\n     The adbhuta-rasa (wonder) here is wonder that is full of love for the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead. Here the �lambanas are the extraordinary\\nand unexpected activities of the Lord, the visaya (object of perception) is\\nLord K���a, �dh�ra (person who loves) is the devotee, the udd�panas\\n(secondary impetuses for ecstatic love) are the Lord's activities, the\\nanubh�vas (bodily manifestations of rasa) are glances and other like\\nthings, the vyabhic�r�s (external manifestations of ecstatic love) are\\nagitation, joy, becomign stunned, and other like ecstatic symptoms, the\\nsthayi-bh�va (continuous ecstasy) is the rasa of wonder filled with love\\nfor the Supreme Personality of Godhead. The rasa of wonder is described in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.69.2):\\n\\n\\ncitra� bataitad ekena\\n     vapu�� yugapat p�thak\\ng�he�u dvy-a��a-s�hasra�\\n     striya eka ud�vahat\\n\\n\\n     \"It is astounding that Lord �r� K���a, who is one without a second,\\nexpanded Himself in sixteen-thousand similar forms to marry sixteen-\\nthousand queens in their respective homes.\"*\\n\\n     Now the rasa of laughter and comedy (h�sya-rasa) filled with love for\\nthe Supreme Personality of Godhead willbe discussed. In h�sya-rasa the\\n�lambanas are various activities, garments, and joking words, the vi�aya\\n(source of comedy) is Lord K���a, and the �dh�ra is the devotee. If\\npleasant and unpleasant things bring the rasa of comedy then that rasa\\nincreases the devotee's love for Lord K���a. This rasa is also manifest in\\nd�na-rasa (the rasa of giving charity), yuddha-rasa (the rasa of conflict),\\nand v�ra-rasa (the rasa of chivalry). The udd�panas here are the actions,\\nwords, garments, and other things that create laughter. The anubh�vas are\\nthe trembling of the nostrils and lips and other like symptoms. The\\nvyabhic�r�s are joy, laziness, dissimulation, and other like things. The\\nsty�yi-bh�va is comedy based on love for the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead. The jokes in this rasa bring pleasure to the heart. An example of\\nthis rasa of joking and laughter is given in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.8.29):\\n\\n\\nvats�n mu�can kvacid asamaye kro�a-sa�j�ta-h�sa�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Our dear friend Ya�od�, your son sometimes comes to our houses before\\nthe milking of the cows and releases the calves, and when the master of the\\nhouse becomes angry, your son merely smiles. Sometimes He devises some\\nprocess by which He steals palatavble curd, butter, and milk, which He then\\neats and drinks. When the monkeys assemble, He divides it with them, and\\nwhen the monkeys have their bellies so full that they won't take any more,\\nHe breaks the pots. Sometimes, if He gets no opportunity to stral butter\\nand milk from a house, He will be angry at the householders, and for His\\nrevenge He will agitate the small children by pinching them. Then, when\\nthey begin crying, K���a will go away.\"*\\n\\n     Another example is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.30):\\n\\n\\n\\nhast�gr�hye racayati vidhim. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When the milk and curd are kept hig� on a swing hanging from a\\nceiling and K���a and Balar�ma cannot reach it, They arrange to reach it by\\npiling up various planks and turning upside down the mortar for grinding\\nspices. Being quite aware of the contents of a pot, They pick holes in it.\\nWhile the elderly gop�s go abou their affairs, K���a ande Balar�ma\\nsometimes go into a dark room, brightening the place with the valuable\\njewels and ornaments on Their bodies and taking advantage of this light by\\nstealing.\"*\\n\\n     Another example is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.31):\\n\\n\\n\\neva� dh�r��y�ny u�ati kurute. . .\\n . . .ittha� str�bhi� sa-bhaya-nayana-�r�-mukh�lokin�bhir\\n     vy�khy�t�rth� prahasita-mukh� na hy upalabdhum aicchat\\n\\n\\n     \"When K���a is caught in His naughty activities, the master of the\\nhouse will say to Him: `Oh, You arew a thief,' and artifically express\\nanger at K���a. K���a will then reply, `I am not a thief. You are a thief.'\\nSometimes, being angry, K���a passes urine and stoll in a neat, clean place\\nin our houses. But now, our dear friend Ya�od�, this expert thief is\\nsitting before you like a very good boy..' Sometimes all tyhe gop�s would\\nlook at K���a sitting there, His eyes fearful so His mother would not\\nchastise Him, and when they saw K���a's beautiful face, instead of\\nchastising Him they would simply look upon His face and enjoy\\ntranscendental bliss. Mother Ya�od� wouls mildly smile at all this fun, and\\nshe would not want to chastise her blessed transcendental child.\"*\\n\\n     In tyhis verse the word \"vy�khy�t�rth��\" means \"while complaining\\nabout K���a's mischievous activities\".\\n\\n"
