      "sourceStartLine": 12039,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Now the rasa of chivalry (v�ra-rasa) filled with love for the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead will be described. V�ra-rasa is of four kinds: 1.\\ndharma-v�ra (munificent in executing religious rites), 2. day�-v�ra\\n(showing extraordinary mercy), 3. d�na-v�ra (charitable activities), and 4.\\nyuddha-v�ra (chivalrous activities in fighting). Now dharma-v�ra will be\\nconsidered. In that rasa the �lambana is the intense desire to perform\\nreligious rites. The vi�aya (object of loev) is Lord K���a. The �dh�ra\\n(lover) is the devotee. The udd�panas are the instructions of the\\nscriptures and other like things. The anubh�vas are humility, faith, and\\nother like virtues. The vyabhic�r�s are thoughtfulness, memory, and other\\nlike things. The sth�yi-bh�va is the eagerness to perform religious rites,\\nan eagerness born of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead. An\\nexample of dharma-v�ra is seen in these owrds spoken by Mah�r�ja\\nYudhi��hira in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.72.3):\\n\\n\\nkratu-r�jena gpvinda\\n     r�jas�yena p�van��\\nyak�ye vibh�t�r bhavatas\\n     tat samp�daya na� prabho\\n\\n\\n     \"O Govinda, I desire to worship Your auspicious, opulent expansions by\\nthe R�jas�ya sacrifice, the king of Vedic ceremonies. Please make our\\nendeavor a success, my Lord.\"***\\n\\n     Now the day�-v�ra rasa of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead\\nwill be considered. In this rasa the �lambana is overwhelming compassion\\nfor all living entities, seeing everyone as originally devotees of the\\nSupreme Lord. In this way the devotee satisfied Lord K���a, who is seen as\\nthe vi�aya (object of love. The �dh�ra (lover) here is the devotee. This\\nrasa is also seen in Lord K���a's parents and many others who show\\ncompassion to Lord K���a Himself. The udd�panas here are the perceptin of\\nothers' sufferings. The anubh�vas are speaking words of consolation and\\nother like activities. The vyabhic�r�s are eagerness, thoughtfulness, joy,\\nand other like emotions. The sthayi-bhava is eagerness to be compassionate.\\nAn example of day�-rasa is given in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(9.21.5-15):\\n\\n\\nk�cchra-pr�pta-ku�umbasya\\n     k�ut-t��bhy�� j�ta-vepatho�\\natithir br�hma�a� k�le\\n     bhoktu-k�masya c�gamat\\n\\ntasmai samvyabhajat so 'nnam\\n     �d�tya �raddhay�nvita�\\nhari� sarvatra sampa�yan. . .\\n\\n . . .iti prabh��ya p�n�ya�\\n     mriyam�na� pip�say�\\npulkas�y�dadad dh�ro\\n     nisarga-karu�o n�pa�\\n\\ntasya tribhuvan�dh��a�\\n     phalada� phalam icchat�m\\n�tm�na� dar�ay�� cakrur\\n     m�y� vi��u-vinirmit��\\n\\n\\n     \"Rantideva never endeavored to earn anything. He would enjoy whatever\\nhe got by the arrangement of Providence, but when guests came he would give\\nthem everything. Thus he underwent considerable suffering, along with the\\nmembers of his family. Indeed, he and his family members shivered for want\\nof food and water. Yet Rantideva always remained sober. Once, after fasting\\nfor forty-eight days, in the morning Rantideva recieved some water and some\\nfoodstuffs made with milk and ghee, but when he and his family were about\\nto eat, a br�hma�a guest arrived.*\\n     \"Because Rantideva percieved the presence of the Supreme Godhead\\neverywhere and in every living entity, he recieved the guest with faith and\\nrespect and gave him a share of the food. The br�hma�a guest ate his share\\nand then went away.*\\n     \"Thereafter, having divided the remaining food with hsi relatives,\\nRantideva was just about to eat his own share when a ��dra guest arrived.\\nSeeing the ��dra in relationship with the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\\nKing Rantideva gave him also a share of the food.*\\n     \"When the ��dra went away, another guest arrived, surrounded by dogs,\\nand said: `O king, I and my company of dogs are very hungry. Please give us\\nsomething to eat.'*\\n     \"With great respect Kign Rantideva offered the balance of food to the\\ndogs and the master of the dogs, who had come as guests. The king offered\\nthem all respects and obeisances.*\\n     \"Thereafter only the drinking water remained, and there was only\\nenough to satisfy one person, but when the king was just about to drink it\\na c����la appeared and said: `O king, although I am lowborn, kindly give me\\nsome drinking water.*\\n     \"Aggrieved at hearing the pitiable words of the poor aggrieved\\nc����la, Mah�r�ja Rantideva spoke the following nectarean words.*\\n     \"I do nto pray to the Supreme Personality of Godhead for the eight\\nperfections of mystic yoga, nor for salvation from repeated birth and\\ndeath. I want only to stay among all the living entities and suffer all\\ndistresses on their behalf, so that they may be freed from all suffering.*\\n     \"By offering my water ot maintain the life of this poor c����la, who\\nis struggling to live, I have been freed from all hunger, thirst, fatigue,\\ntrembling of the body, moroseness, distress, lamentation, and illusion.*\\n     \"Having spoken thus, King Rantideva, although on the verge of death\\nbecause of thirst, gave his own portion of water to the candala without\\nhesitation, for the king was naturally very kind and sober.*\\n     \"Demigods like Lord Brahm� and Lord �iva, who can satisfy all\\nmaterially ambitious men by giving them the rewards they desire, then\\nmanifested their own identities before King Rantideva, for it was they who\\nhad presented themselves as the br�hma�a, ��dra, c����la, and so on.\"*\\n\\n     The meaning og these verses is clear. These verses were spoken by\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
