      "sourceStartLine": 12650,
      "content": "\\n\\n     It is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (4.20.27-28):\\n\\n\\nath�bhaje tv�khila-p�ru�ottama�\\n     gu��laya� padma-kare�a l�lasa�\\napy �vayor eka-pati-sp�dho� kalir\\n     na sy�t k�ta-tvac-cara�aika-t�nayo�\\n\\n\\n     \"Now I wish to engage in the service of the lotus feet of the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead and serve just like the goddess of fortune, who\\ncarries a lotus flower in her hand, because His Lordship, the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead, is the reservoir of all transcendental qualities. I\\nam afraid that the goddess of fortune and I would quarrel because both of\\nus would be attentively engaged in the same service.*\\n\\n\\njagaj-janany�� jagad-��a vai�asa� sy�t\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear Lord of the universe, the goddess of fortune, Lak�m�, is the\\nmother of the universe, and yet I think she may be angry with me because of\\nmy intruding upon her service and acting on the very platform to which she\\nis so much attached. Yet I am hopeful that even though there is some\\nmisunderstanding, You will take my part, for You are very much inclined to\\nthe poor and You always magnify even insignificant service unto You.\\nTherefore even though she becomes angry, I think there is no harm for You,\\nbecause You are so self-sufficient that You can do without her.\"*\\n\\n     Here it is seen that Mah�r�ja P�thu's true rasa is d�sya-rasa. Because\\nhis rasa is clearly not ���g�ra-rasa, these words seem to be ras�bh�sa.\\nMah�r�ja P�thu's rasa is d�sya-rasa and Goddess Lak�m�'s (padma-kerva\\nl�lasa�) rasa is ���g�ra-rasa. Here is the explanation: Mah�r�ja P�thu's\\ndesire is not at all based on srngara-rasa. It is based only on d�sya-rasa.\\nThe examples he gives are examples only of devotional service in dasya-\\nrasa. His perception of rivalry with Goddess Lak�m� are not inappropriate\\nfor a devotee situated in v�ra-d�sya-rasa (heroic servitorship), a devotee\\nwho has attained great mercy from the Supreme Personality of Godhead.\\nOthers may say Mah�r�ja Prtbu'd words show only his great love for the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead, who is so merciful to the poor and fallen,\\nand these words do not at all show any true rivalry with goddess Lak�m�.\\nThis kind of apreciation for the Lord's greeat mercy, thinking the Lord is\\nmore merciful to the devotee than He is even to Goddess Lak�m�, is seen in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (8.23.6), where Prahl�da Mah�r�ja\\ndescribes Lord V�mana's pastime of stepping on Bali Mah�r�ja's head:\\n\\n\\nnema� viri�co labhate pras�dam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O Supreme Personality of Godhead, You are universally worshiped. Even\\nLord Brahma and Lord Siva worship Your lotus feet. Yet although You are\\nsuch a great personality, You have kindly promised to protect us, the\\ndemons. I think that such kindness never has been achieved even by Lord\\nBrahma, Lord Siva, or the Goddess of fortune, Laksmi, what to speak of\\nother dmeigods or common people.\"*\\n\\n     That the Supreme Personality of Godhead is more merciful to the\\ndevotee than He is even to Goddess Lak�m� is seen in this prayer, where\\nPrahl�da Mah�r�ja glorifies Lord N�si�hadeva's mercy to him (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 7.9.26):\\n\\n\\nkv�ha� raja�-prabhava ��a tamo 'dhike 'smin\\n     j�ta� suretara-kule kva tav�nukamp�\\nna brahma�o na tu bhavasya na vai ram�y�\\n     yan me 'rpita� �irasi padma-kara� pras�da�\\n\\n\\n     \"O my Lord, O Supreme, because I was born in a family full of the\\nhellish material qualities of passion and ignorance, what is my position?\\nAnd what is to bge said of your causeless mercy, which was never offered\\neven to Lord Brahm�, Lord �iva, or the goddess of fortune, Lak�m�? You\\nnever put Your lotus hand upon their heads, but You have put it upon\\nmine.\"*\\n\\n     Here Prahl�da Mah�r�ja says: \"In the presence of Brahm� and the\\ndemigods You placed Your hand on my head\". He says: \"Never was such\\nmercyever given in any of Your other avat�ras.\" �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 4.20.27-\\n28 quoted in this anuccheda was spoken by Mah�r�ja P�thu to Lord �r� Vi��u.\\n\\n"
