      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 12832,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Even the vraja-gop�s, who think only of Lord K���a's sweetness, are\\nsometimes aware of His power and opulence. This is seen in the following\\nwords spoken by the gop�ws in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.31.4):\\n\\n\\nna khalu gopik�-nandano bhav�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"You are not actually the son of the gop� Ya�od�, O friend, but rather\\nthe indwelling witness in the hearts of all embodied souls. Because Lord\\nBrahm� prayed for Yon to come and protect the universe, You have now\\nappeared in the S�tvata dynasty.\"***\\n\\n     What here seems to be ���g�ra-rasa mixed with ��nta-rtasa is in truth\\nonly joking words spoken by the gop�s. This will be explained later in this\\nbook. Therefore everything in this verse is proper and there is no\\nras�bh�sa. It is also said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.23):\\n\\n\\nbaddh�nyay� sraj� k�cit. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"One gop� tied up her slender companion with a flower garland and\\nsaid, ``Now I will bind this boy who has broken the butter pots and stolen\\nthe butter.'' The second gop� then covered her face and beautiful eyes,\\npretending to be afraid.\"***\\n\\n     These words seem to be a mixture of v�tsalya-rasa and ���g�ra-rasa .\\nIn truth there is no mixture, nor any ras�bh�sa in these words. This will\\nbe explained later. An example of natural ���g�ra-rasa is seen in these\\nwords describing the r�sa-dance pastime (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.10):\\n\\n\\ndu�saha-pre��ha-viraha. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"For those gop�s who could not go to see K���a, intolerable separation\\nfrom their beloved caused an intense agony that burned away all impious\\nkarma. By meditating upon Him they realized His embrace, and the ecstasy\\nthey then felt exhausted their material piety. Although Lord K���a is the\\nSupreme Soul, these girls simply thought of Him as their male lover and\\nassociated with Him in that intimate mood. Thus their karmic bondage was\\nnullified and they abandoned their gross material bodies.\"***\\n\\n     These words were spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�, the king of sages.\\nThen it is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.12):\\n\\n\\nk���am vidu� param kantam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O sage, the gop�s knew K���a only as their lover, not as the Supreme\\nAbsolute Truth. So how could these girls, their minds caught up in the\\nwaves of the modes of nature, free themselves from material attachment?\"***\\n\\n     These words are a question posed by Mah�r�ja Par�k�it. In �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m�'s answer to this question may seem to be based on a\\ndescription of liberation, a description of santa-rasa, a description that\\nmay seem to be rasabhasa in the context of srngara-rasa. Actually there is\\nno rasabhasa here. This is explained in K���a-sandarbha. What is seen as\\nliberation (mok�a) here is in truth merely the gop�s' overcoming the\\nobstacles that prevent them from meeting Lord K���a. This is not a\\ndescription of anything else. It is not a description of impersonal\\nliberation (mok�a). It is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.32.8):\\n\\nta� k�cin netra-randhre�a. . .yog�v�nanda-samplut�\\n\\n     \"One gop� took the Lord through the aperture of her eyes and placed\\nHim within her heart. Then, with her eyes closed and her bodily hairs\\nstanding on end, she continuously embraced Him within. Thus immersed in\\ntranscendental ecstasy, she resembled a yog� meditating upon the Lord.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"yogi\" is in the neuter gender. Although they\\nshyly embrace Lord K���a in their hearts, the gop�s in truth embraced Him\\nvery earnestly. In this way they attained His association (yogi). Thus\\nthere is no ras�bh�sa here. In whatever passaghes of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\nthere may seem to be ras�bh�sa, there is no ras�bh�sa in truth. Everything\\nin �r�mad-Bh�gavatam is proper and right.\\n\\n     Now seeming contardictions in relation to Lord Balar�ma and others\\nwill be considered. Many contradictions may seem to be manifest in Lord\\nK���a's many blissful pastimes with His devotees. None of these are\\ncontradictions in truth. This is so because of the action of the Lord's\\ninconceivable spiritual potency (acintya-�akti). This is truew for all the\\ndevotees qualifiedto participate in the Lord's pastimes. Because He is\\nLored K���a's elder brother, Lord Balar�ma is situated in v�tsalya-rasa.\\nBecause He is identical with Lord K���a and beacuse from childhood He\\nenjoys pastimes with Lord K���a, Lord\\nBalar�ma is situated in sakhya-rasa. Because He is aware of Lord K���a's\\nsupreme power and opulence (ai�varya), Lord Balar�ma is a devotee of Lored\\nK���a. All these features are manifest at the time of Lord K���a's pastimes\\nwith Lord Balar�ma. The two of Them acting together, K���a and Balar�ma\\nattacked �a�khac��a, enjoyed the holi-l�l�, sang, and enjoyed many\\npastimes. From Dv�rak� Lord Balar�ma carried Lord K���a's message to the\\ngop�s. There is nothing wrong in any of these activities. This truth is\\nconfirmed by the words of �r�m�n Uddhava and many others also.\\n\\n     Now will be considered the ras�bh�sa present when a primary rasa is\\nincompatible with a secondary rasa. It is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.44.51):\\n\\n\\ndevak� vasudeva� ca\\n     vij��ya jagad-��varau\\nk�ta-samvandanau putrau\\n     sasvaj�te na �a�kitau\\n\\n\\n     \"Devak� and Vasudeva, now knowing K���a and Balar�ma to be the Lords\\nof the universe, simply stood with joined palms. Being apprehensive, they\\ndid not embrace their sons.\"***\\n\\n     Here someone may saty that the combination of vatsalya-rasa and\\nbhayanaka-rasa (fear) in this verse is ras�bh�sa. The previous explanations\\nhave already shown that in this situation there is no ras�bh�sa.\\n\\n     Now will be considered the ras�bh�sa present when two secondary rasas\\nare incompatible. In the pastime when Lord K���a entered K�liya Lake it is\\nsaid in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.16.16):\\n\\n\\nt��s tath� k�tar�n v�k�ya\\n     bhagav�n m�dhavo balah\\nprahasya ki�cin nov�ca\\n     prabh�va j�o 'nujasya sa�\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Lord Balar�ma, the master of all transcendental\\nknowledge, smiled and said nothing when He saw the residents of V�nd�vana\\nin such distress, since He understood the extraordinary power of His\\nyounger brother.\"***\\n\\n     Here Lord Balar�ma is fully aware of Lord K���a's power and opulence,\\nand the people of Vraja are plunged in karuna-rasa (grief). In this\\nsituation grief is appropriate, but someone may say that Lord Balar�ma's\\nmanifestation of h�sya-rasa (smiling) is not appropriate, and therefore\\nras�bh�sa is present in this verse. The truth is that Lord Balar�ma\\nmanifests many different natures according to His different pastimes. This\\nhas already been explained. In this way He enjoys different pastimes. Here\\nthe cause of His smiling is His knowledge of Lord K���a's supreme power.\\nThe people of Vraja, eager to protect Lord K���a, are situated in a\\ndifferent mood in this situation. If they had known the secret truth of\\ntheir dear K���a, they would have smiled also. They would not have worried\\nor lamented. An activity of Lord Balar�ma performed to benefit the people\\nof Vraja is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.16.22):\\n\\n\\nk���a-pr���n nirvisato\\n     nand�d�n v�k�ya ta� hradam\\npratya�edhat sa bhagav�n\\n     rama� k����nubh�va-vit\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord Balar�ma then saw that Nanda Mah�r�ja and the other cowherd men,\\nwho had dedicated their very lives to K���a, were beginning to enter the\\nserpent's lake. As the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Lord Balar�ma fully\\nknew Lord K���a's actual power, and therefore He restrained them.\"***\\n\\n     At the end of this pastime the people of Vraja regained their Lord\\nK���a. This is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.17.16):\\n\\n\\nr�ma� c�Cyutam �li�gya\\n     jah�s�sy�nubh�va-vit\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord Balar�ma embraced His infallible brother and laughed, knowing\\nwell the extent of His potency. Out of great feelings of love, He lifted\\nHim up on His lap and repeatedly looked at Him. The cows, bulls and young\\nfemale calves also achieved the highest pleasure.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse Lord Balar�ma smiled because he regained Lord K���a. In\\nthe pastime of the kidnapping of Rukimini and in manyother pastimes also,\\nLord Balar�ma's nature of being flooded with love for His brother K���a is\\ndescribed. In all these pastimes nothing is inappropriate. Therefore Lord\\nBalar�ma's smiling here is not ras�bh�sa. �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.16.16 quoted\\nhere was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
