      "sourceStartLine": 13463,
      "content": "\\n\\n     It is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.55.40):\\n\\n\\nya� vai muhu�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"It is not astonishing that the palace women, who should have felt\\nmaternal affection for Pradyumna, privately felt ecstatic attraction for\\nhim as if he were their own lord. After all, the son exactly resembled his\\nfather. Indeed, Pradyumna was a perfect reflection of the beauty of Lord\\nK���a, the shelter of the goddess of fortune, and appeared before their\\neyes as Cupid himself. Since even those on the level of his mother felt\\nconjugal attraction for him, then what to speak of how other women felt\\nwhen they saw him?\"***\\n\\n     Here the truth that the palace women were agitated by feelings of\\namorous love for Pradyumna seems to be inappropriate. What seems here to be\\ninappropriate is actually appropriate, as has already been explained in a\\nprevious passage of this book.\\n     Someone may say that innapropriate �lambanas or inappro{.inappropriate\\nlovers (prity-�dh�ra) bring rasabhasa situations in the descriptions of the\\nyajna--patnis, pulinda girls, does, and others in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam. This\\nassertion will be refuted later in this book.\\n     Someone may claim that an inappropriateness in relation to the object\\nof love (pr�ti-vi�aya) is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.21.7):\\n\\n\\nak�a�vat�m. . .vaktra� vraje�a-sutayo�\\n\\n\\n     \"The cowherd girls said: O friends, those eyes that see the beautiful\\nfaces of the sons of Mah�r�ja Nanda are certainly fortunate. As these two\\nsons enter the forest, surrounded by Their friends, driving the cows before\\nThem, They hold Their flutes to Their mouths and glance lovingly upon the\\nresidents of V�nd�vana. For those who have eyes, we think there is no\\ngreater object of vision.\"***\\n\\n     Here someone may say that although Lord K���a and Lord Balar�ma ,\\nstill the form of Lord Balar�ma is not the same as the form of Lord K���a,\\nand therefore it is not appropriate for Lord %'s girlfriends to have\\nfeelings of amorous love for Lord Balar�ma, and therefore this verse has a\\nras�bh�sa of ���g�ra-rasa. The truth, however, is that in this verse the\\ngop�s are hiding their amorous feelings for Lord K���a. They are pretending\\nthat they have no amorous feelings for Lord K���a. Therefore their praising\\nLord K���a and Lord Balar�ma equally is in now way a statement of their\\namorous feelings for Lord Balar�ma. Therefore there is no ras�bh�sa in this\\nverse. In his commentary on �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.65.17) �r�la �r�dhara\\nSv�m� states: \"The gop�s who enjoyed amorous pastimes with Lord Balar�ma\\nwere different from the gop�s who enjoyed pastimes with Lord K���a.\" �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.21.7 quoted in this anuccheda was spoken by the goddesslike\\ngop�s of Vraja.\\n\\n"
