    {
      "number": 203,
      "sub": null,
      "sourceLabel": "Anuccheda 203",
      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 14062,
      "content": "\\n\\n     In this way the rasas of love for the Supreme Personality of Godhead\\nare described. The rasa of devotional service mixed with knowledge (j��na-\\nbhaktimaya-rasa) is also called by the name ��nta-rasa. In this rasa of\\ndevotion mixed with knowledge the �lambana (prime impetus of ecstatic love)\\nand the vi�aya (object of love) are both the transcendental form of the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead (bhagav�n) manifest as the Supreme Brahman\\n(para-brahma). Here the �dh�ra (lovers) are the great j��ni-bhaktas who\\npersonally participate in the Supreme Lord's pastimes. The Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead as He manifests in this rasa is described in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (3.15.37):\\n\\n\\neva� tadaiva bhagav�n aravinda-n�bha�\\n\\n\\n     \"At that very moment, the Lord, who is called Padman�bha because of\\nthe lotus grown from His navel and who is the delight of the righteous,\\nlearned about the insult offered by His own servants to the saints.\\nAccompanied by His spouse, the goddess of fortune, He went to the spot on\\nthose very feet sought for by recluses and great sages.\"*\\n\\n     When they arrived in Vaiku��ha, the four Kum�ras saw this form of the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead. The j��ni-bhaktas are described in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.7.10):\\n\\n\\n�tm�r�m�� ca munayo\\n\\n\\n     \"All different varieties of �tm�r�mas [those who take pleasure in\\n�tm�, or spirit self], especially those established on the path of seff-\\nrealization, though freed from all kinds of material bondage, desire to\\nrender unalloyed devotional service unto the Personality of Godhead. This\\nmeans that the Lord possesses transcendental qualities and therefore can\\nattract everyone, including liberated souls.\"*\\n\\n     The four Kum�ras and many others are �tm�r�mas are described in this\\nverse. �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�, who was attracted by the nectar sweetness of\\nthe rasas in the Supreme Lord's pastimes and who was rapt in reciting\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam, is also described in that verse.\\n     In ��nta-rasa the udd�panas (secondary impetuses for ecstatic love)\\nare primarily the Supreme Lord's qualities, activities, and paraphernalia.\\nHere the Lord's qualities include His eternal form of knowledge and bliss,\\nHis form always being manifest, His being the master of all opulences, His\\nbeing the all-pervading Supersoul, His being the master of the knoledge-\\npotency, His being all-powerful, His giving liberation to the enemies He\\nkills, His being dear to the devotees in ��nta-rasa, His being equal to\\nall, His being self-controlled, His being peaceful, His purity, and His\\nmanifesting wonderful forms. The Lord's activities being with His acting to\\nprotect His devotees and include a host of other activities also. His\\nparaphenralia include His great Upani�ads, the dust of the j��ni-bhaktas'\\nfeet, His Tulas�-dev�, His transcendental abodes, and many other things\\nalso.\\n     Now the anubh�vas (activities of ecstatic love) will be described.\\nHere the anubh�vas include: praising the Supreme Lord's transcendental\\nqualities and other features, chanting the holy names of the Lord in His\\nforms as Brahman, Param�tm�, and other forms, tasting the bliss of seeing\\nthe Lord in His Brahman feature, and yearning to see the Lord in His\\noriginal feature as Bhagav�n (the Supreme Personality of Godhead). Here the\\ns�ttvika-bh�vas include: staring at the tip of the nose, performing the\\nactions of an avadh�ta, assuming the jnana-mudra posture, yawning,\\nobserving silence, bowing down before the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\\nand offering prayers to the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Most of these\\nactivities are material in nature. In this rasa the sa�c�ri-bh�vas\\n(symptoms of ecstatic love) include: being detached from material things,\\nsteadiness, joyfulness, thoughtfulness, meditation, gravity, eagerness,\\nearnestness, and the philosophical search for the truth.\\n     The sth�yi-bh�va (continuous ecstasy) of the j��ni-bhaktas is\\ndescribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (3.15.46):\\n\\n\\nyo 'ntarhito h�di gato 'pi dur�tman�� tva�\\n     so 'dyaiva no nayana-m�lam ananta r�ddhah\\n\\n\\n     \"Our dear Lord, You are not manifested to rascals, even though You are\\nseated within the heart of everyone. But as far as we are concerned, we see\\nYou face to face, although You are unlimited. The statements we have heard\\nabout You from our father, Brahm�, through the ears have now been actually\\nrealized by Your kind appearance.\"*\\n\\n     The attainment of ��nta-rasa is described in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (3.15.43):\\n\\n\\ntasy�ravinda-nayanasya pad�ravinda-\\n     ki�jalka-mi�ra-tulas�-makaranda-v�yuh\\nantar-gata� sva-vivare�a cak�ra tes��\\n     sa�k�obham ak�ara ju��m api citta-tanvo�\\n\\n\\n     \"When the breeze carrying the aroma of tulas� leaves from the toes of\\nthe lotus feet of the Personality of Godhead entered the nostrils of those\\nsages, they experienced a change both in body and in mind, even though they\\nwere attached to the impersonal Brahman understanding.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the lotus-eyed Lord (aravinda-nayanasya) is the\\n�lambana, the breeze is the udd�pana, the change in body is the udbh�svara,\\ns�ttvika-bh�va, and anubh�va, and the change in mind is the sa�c�ri-bh�va,\\nwhich consists of spiritual happiness. Even though they were situated in\\nimpersonal Brahman understanding, the sages attained devotional service\\nmixed with knowledge (j��na-bhakti). That kind of devotional service was\\nthe sth�yi-bh�va (continuous ecstasy) they felt. All these together\\nculiminate in j��na-bhakti (devotional service mixed with knowledge). That\\nwas the rasa they experienced.\\n     Now we will discuss the bhakti-rasa known as ��raya-rasa (the rasa of\\ntaking shelter of Lord K���a). In this rasa Lord K���a is the object of\\nlove and certain devotees who participate in Lord K���a's pastimes are the\\nlovers. In different situations sometimes Lord K���a manifests His\\nhumanlike form and sometimes He manifests His form as the supreme\\ncontroller. In the presence of the residents of Vraja He manifests His\\nsupremely sweet and glorious humanlike form.\\n     The devotees are of two kinds: 1. the external devotees who serve the\\nLord by performing specific duties in the material world, and 2. the\\nconfidential devotees for whom the shade of Lord K���a's feet is their very\\nlife. In the first group the demigods Brahm�, �iva, and some others are\\nconsidered confidential devotees because of their great devotion to the\\nLord. The second group is divided into three parts: 1. the devotees in\\ngeneral, 2. the residents of the cities of the Yadus (like Mathur� and\\nDv�rak�), and 3. the residents of Vraja. In the first of these groups are\\nincluded the kings imprisoned by Jar�sandha, the great sages, and many\\nothers, and in the second and third groups are included Janaka Mah�r�ja\\n(the king of Mithil�) and many other devotees.\\n     Included among the udd�panas in this rasa are Lord K���a's qualities.\\nIncluded among His qualities are the following: 1. being the master of all\\nopulences (bhagavattvam) in His relationship with the devotees who take\\nshelter of Him as the supreme controller, 2. being the seed of all avat�ras\\n(avat�r�val�-b�jatvam), 3. being the attractor of the self-satisfied sages\\n(�tm�r�m�kar�itvam), 4. being the giver of ecstatic devotional service to\\nP�tan� and others who only pretend to engage in devotional service, 5.\\nmanifesting numberless material universes from the pores of His skin, 6.\\nbeing an ocean of mercy to the devotees who take shelter of Him as He is\\nmanifest in His humanlike form, 7. being the protector of anyone who takes\\nshelter of Him, 8. possessing inconceivable potencies, 9. being the supreme\\nobject of worship, 10. being all-knowing, 11. being firm in vow, 12.\\npossessing all wealth and all good fortune, 13. being naturally patient and\\ntolerant, 14. being righteous, 15. being truthful, 16. being expert, 17.\\nbeing all-auspicious, 18. being supremely powerful and glorious, 19. being\\nthe best follower of religion, 20. seeing through the eyes of the\\nscriptures, 21. being the devotee's friend and well-wisher, 22. generosity,\\n23. power 24. fame, 25. strength, 26. tolerance, 27. ability, 28. being\\ncontrolled by love, and many other transcendental qualities also.\\n     Now we will consider Lord K���a's castes. The first group of devotees\\n(the devotees in general) accept that Lord K���a is a cowherd boy or a\\nmember of another caste (as a ksatirya in Dvaraka). This acceptance of\\ncaste by the Lord is only an imitation. Lord K���a's dark complexion and\\nmany other things remind (sm�raka) the devotees of Him. The devotees in the\\nsecond group (the residents of Vraja, Mathura, and Dvaraka) thinkt hat Lord\\nK���a is the best of all gopas or the best of all ksatriyas. Now Lord\\nK���a's activities will be considered. For the first group of devotees (the\\ndevotees in general) Lord K���a's creation and maintenance of the material\\nworlds and His revelation of His universal form are prominent. For the\\nsecond group of devotees (the residents of Vraja, Mathura, and Dvaraka)\\nLord K���a's killing the dfemons, protecting His devotees, and casting His\\nglance of mercy are prominent. Now Lord K���a's paraphernalia will be\\nconsidered. Lord K���a's weapons, musical instruments, ornaments, abodes,\\nfootprints, and devotees are prominent among His paraphernalia. For the\\nfirst group of devotees these are all clearly transcendental. For the\\nsecond group of devotees these seem to be material even though they\\ncertainly are transcendental in fact. Now the time sequence of Lord K���a\\nwill be considered. For both the first and second groups of devotees these\\nbegin with Lord K���a's birth in this world and end with His departure for\\nthe spiritual world.\\n     Now the anubh�vas in this rasa will be considered. The anubh�vas\\ninclude residing in places sacred to Lord K���a, chanting the glories of\\nLord K���a's holy names and qualities, and performing other like devotional\\nactivities.\\n     Now the sa�c�ri-bh�vas in this rasa will be discussed. When the\\ndevotees associate with Lord K���a the sa�c�ri-bh�vas are joy, pride, and\\nsteadiness. When the devotees are separated from Lord K���a the sancari-\\nbhavas are sickness and exhaustion. In both association with and separation\\nfrom the Lord are manifest the sa�c�ri-bh�vas detachment, anxiety,\\nmoroseness, humbleness, worry, meditation, embarassment, thoughtfulness,\\nand death. An example of meditation on the Lord even when the Lord is\\npresent before the devotee is seen in this description of Bh��madeva's\\nactions in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.9.31):\\n\\n\\nvi�uddhay� dh�ra�ay�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"By pure meditation, looking at Lord �r� K���a, he at once was freed\\nfrom all material inauspiciousness and was relieved of all bodily pains\\ncaused by the arrow wounds. Thus all the external activities of his senses\\nat once stopped, and he prayed transcendentally to the controller of all\\nliving beings while quitting his material body.\"*\\n\\n     Unhappy that he may have offended Lord K���a by attacking Him in\\nbattle, Bh��madeva prays in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.9.34):\\n\\n\\nyudhi turaga-raja�. . .mama ni�ita-�arair vibhidyam�na-tvaci\\n\\n\\n     \"On the battlefield [where �r� K���a attended Arjuna out of\\nfriendship], the flowing hair of Lord K���a turned ashen due to the dust\\nraised by the hoofs of the horses. And because of His labor, beads of sweat\\nwetted His face. All these decorations, intensified by the wounds dealt by\\nmy sharp arrows, were enjoyed by Him. Let my mind thus go unto �r� K���a.\"*\\n\\n     Bh��madeva again prays in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.9.38):\\n\\n\\n�ita-vi�ikha-hata�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"May He, Lord Sr� K���a, the Personality of Godhead, who awards\\nsalvation, be my ultimate destination. On the battlefield He charged me, as\\nif angry because of the wounds dealt by my sharp arrows. His shield was\\nscattered, and His body was smeared with blood due to the wounds.\"*\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 1.9.31 quoted here was spoken by �r�la S�ta Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
