      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 14401,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Separation when the Lord is in a distant place is described in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.11.9):\\n\\n\\nyarhy ambuj�k��pasas�ra. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O lotus-eyed Lord, whenever You go away to Mathur�-V�nd�vana or\\nHastin�pura to meet Your friends and relatives, every moment of Your\\nabsence seems like a million years. O infallible one, at that time our eyes\\nbecome useless, as if bereft of sun.\"*\\n\\n     This verse describes the separation felt by the citizens of Dv�rak�.\\nThe separation felt by the people of Vraja is described in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.35.25):\\n\\n\\nyadu-patir dvirada-r�ja-vih�ra�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"With His cheerful face resembling the moon, lord of the night, the\\nLord of the Yadus moves with the grace of a regal elephant. Thus He returns\\nin the evening, delivering the cows of Vraja from the heat of the day.\"***\\n\\n     Here it is said that Lord K���a deliughted the young and old cows. How\\nmuch more did He give delight to the human beings?  The \"tu��i\" kind of\\nassociation with the Lord is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(1.11.1):\\n\\n\\n�nart�n sa upavrajya\\n     svrddh�� jana-pad�n svak�n\\ndadhmau daravara� tes��\\n     vi��da� �amayann iva\\n\\n\\n     \"Upon reaching the border of His most prosperous metropolis, known as\\nthe country of the Anartas [Dv�rak�], the Lord sounded His auspicious\\nconchshell, heralding His arrival and apparently pacifying the dejection of\\nthe inhabitants.\"*\\n\\n     The word \"iva\" (as if) used here is a rhetorical ornament. This verse\\nwas spoken by �r�la S�ta Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
