      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 15298,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Pradyumna's glory is also described in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.55.40):\\n\\n\\nya� vai muhu� pit�-svar�pa-nije�a-bh�v�s\\n     tan-m�taro yad abhajan raha-r��ha-bh�v��\\ncitra� na tat khalu ram�spada-bimba-bimbe\\n     k�me smare 'k�a-vi�aye kim ut�nya-n�rya�\\n\\n\\n     \"It is not astonishing that the palace women, who should have felt\\nmaternal affection for Pradyumna, privately felt ecstatic attraction for\\nHim as if He were their own Lord. After all, the son exactly resembled His\\nfather. Indeed, Pradyumna was a perfect reflection of the beauty of Lord\\nK���a, the shelter of the goddess of fortune, and appeared before their\\neyes as Cupid himself. Since even those on the level of His mother felt\\nconjugal attraction for Him, then what to speak of how other women felt\\nwhen they saw Him?\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"yam\" refers to Pradyumna. The words \"tan-\\nm�taro yad abhajan raha�\" mean {.sy 168}His mothers came to see Him. Shyly\\ntaking Him to a secluded place, they worshiped Him.\" The words \"tat khalu\\nram�spada-bimba-bimbe\" mean \"He was like a picture of Lord K���a, the place\\nwhere the goddess of fortune enjoys pastimes\". \"Na citram\" means \"It was\\nnot surprising that the mothers felt maternal love for Him, for He was\\nexactly like His father.\" The words \"pit�-svar�pa-nije�a-bh�v��\" mean \"This\\nwas also not surprising because Pradyumna's body closely resembled that of\\nHis father, and therefore the queens thought Pradyumna was their master,\\nLord K���a.\" Still, they did not think of Him as their husband, and\\ntherefore it was proper for them to take Him to a secluded place.  The same\\nexplanation was already given previously in that chapter where it was said\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.55.27-28):\\n\\n\\nta� d���v� jalada-�y�mam. . .\\n\\n . . .k���a� matv� striyo hr�t�\\n     nililyus tatra tatra ha\\n\\n\\n     \"The women of the palace thought he was Lord K���a when they saw His\\ndark-blue complexion the color of a rain-cloud, His yellow silk garments,\\nhis long arms, and red-tinged eyes, his charming lotus face adorned with a\\npleasing smile, His fine ornaments, and His thick, curly black hair. Thus\\nthe women became bashful and hid themselves here and there.\"***\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.55.40 the words \"r��ha-bh�v��\" indicate that\\nthe queens thought Pradyumna was Lord K���a, who is very dear to them.\\nBecause He very closely resembled His father, they thought He was their\\nmaster, Lord K���a. Still, they did not think of Him as their husband. �r�\\nRukmini thought of HIm as her son. She did not think He was her master,\\nLord K���a. Because He so closely resembled Lord K���a, Rukmi�� thought He\\nmust be her own son. This is seen in the following words spoken by �r�\\nRukmi�� in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.55.33-34):\\n\\n\\nkatha� tv anena sampr�ptam. . .\\n\\n . . .sa eva va bhaven n�na�\\n     yo me garbhe dh�to 'rbhaka�\\namusmin pr�tir adhik�\\n     v�ma� sphurati me bhuja�\\n\\n\\n     \"But how is it that this young man so much resembles my own Lord,\\nK���a, the wielder of ��r�ga, in his bodily form and His limbs, in His gait\\nand the tone of His voice, and in His smiling glance?***\\n     \"Yes, He must be the same child I bore in my womb, sicne I feel great\\naffection for Him and my left arm is quivering.\"***\\n\\n          In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.55.40 the words {.sy 168}ram�spada-bimba-\\nbimbe k�me smare\" mean the queens were bewildered into thinking Pradyumna\\nwas the spiritual K�madeva who had now incarnated in the material world.\\nThe words {.sy 168}ak�a-vi�aye kim ut�nya-n�rya�\" mean, if the queens saw\\nHim in that way, then how enchanted must other women have become by seeing\\nHim?\" �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.55.40 quoted in this anuccheda was spoken by\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
