      "sourceStartLine": 15380,
      "content": "\\n\\n     In pra�raya-rasa the udd�panas are Lord K���a's transcendental\\nqualities, beginning with His smiling glance filled with paternal love.\\nAlso included among the uddipanas are the glories of Lord K���a's fame,\\nintelligence, strength, and other attributes. Also included are Lord\\nK���a's noble birth, activities, and many other like things.\\n     The anubh�vas are as follows. In childhood the anubh�vas include: 1.\\nagain and again asking quaestions in a sweet voice, 2. grasping rthe arm\\nand the fingers, 3. sitting on the lap, and 4. accepting the betelnuts\\nchewed by the Lord. After childhood the anubh�vas include: 1. following the\\ncommands, 2. imitating the activities, and 3. renouncing all independence.\\nIn both childhood and after the sons are followers of their father. All the\\ns�ttvika-bh�vas are manifest here. The vyabhic�r�s are also as before\\ndescribed. The sth�yi-bh�va is pra�raya-rasa. In childhood hugging and\\ndealing with great affection are the seed of pra�raya-rasa. Those are the\\nactivities in childhood. The activities after childhood are described in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.11.17-18):\\n\\n\\nni�amya pre�tham �y�ntam. . .\\n\\n . . .pradyumna� c�rude�na� ca\\n     s�mbo j�mbavatt-suta�\\nprahar�a-vegoccha�ita-\\n     �ayan�sana-bhojan��\\n\\n\\n     \"On hearing that the most dear K���a was approaching Dv�rak�-dh�ma,\\nmagnanimous Vasudeva, Akr�ra, Ugrasena, Balar�ma (the superhumanly\\npowerful), Pradyumna, C�rude��a and S�mba the son of J�mbavat�, all\\nextremely happy, abandoned resting, sitting and dining.*\\n\\n\\nv�ra�endra� purask�tya\\n     br�hma�ai� sasuma�galaih\\n�a�kha-t�rya-nin�dena\\n     brahma-gho�e�a c�drt�h\\npratyujjagm� rathair h��t��\\n     pra�ay�gata-s�dhvas��\\n\\n\\n     \"They hastened toward the Lord on chariots with br�hma�as bearing\\nflowers. Before them were elephants, emblems of good fortune. Conchshells\\nand bugles were sounded, and Vedic hymns were chanted. Thus they offered\\ntheir respects, which were saturated with affection.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"pra�aya�\" means {.sy 168}great devotion\". This\\nverse was spoken by �r�la S�ta Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
