      "sourceStartLine": 16089,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Seeing a display of Lord K���a's extraordinary power, a devotee in\\nv�tsalya-rasa may think the power came not from K���a, but from some other\\nsource instead. An example of this is seen in the following words spoken by\\nLord K���a's parents in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.7.31):\\n\\n\\naho bat�ty-adbhutam e�a rak�as�\\n     b�lo niv�tti� gamito 'bhyag�t puna�\\nhi�sra� sva-p�pena vihi�sita� khala�\\n     s�dhu� samatvena bhay�d vimucyate\\n\\n\\n     \"It is most astonishing that although this innocent child was taken\\naway by the R�k�asa to be eaten, He has returned without having been killed\\nor even injured. Because this demon was envious, cruel, and sinful, he has\\nbeen killed for his own sinful activities. This is the law of nature. An\\ninnocent devotee is always protecvted by the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead, and a sinful person is always vanquished for his sinful life.\"*\\n\\n     Lord K���a's prents thought in this way. Lord K���a's mother spoke\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.40):\\n\\n\\nki� svapna. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"(Mother Ya�od� began to argue with herself:) Is this a dream, or is\\nit an illusory creation by the external energy? Has this been manifested by\\nmy own intelligence, or is it some mystic power of my child?\"*\\n\\n     After seeing Lord K���a manifest the entire material universe within\\nHis abdomen, Mother Ya�od� could not believe that this was a display of her\\nson's great power. She assumed that He had no power to manifest this\\nvision. (She assumed that the vision of the universal form was caused by\\nthe Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is different from her son.) She\\nthought in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.41):\\n\\n\\natho yath�van na vitarka-gocaram. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Therefore let me surrender to the Supreme Personality of Godhead and\\noffer my obeisances unto Him, who is beyond the conception of human\\nspeculation, the mind, activities, words, and arguments, who is the\\noriginal cause of this cosmic manifestation, by whom the entire cosmos is\\nmaintained, and by whom we can conceive of its existence. Let me simply\\noffer my obeisances, for He is beyond my contemplation, speculation, and\\nmeditation. He is beyond all of my material activities.\"*\\n\\n     Here Mother Ya�od� assumes that this vision of the universal form was\\ncaused by the Supreme Personality of Godhead (who is different from her\\nson). To become free from seeing this fearful vision, she takes shelter of\\nthe Supreme Lord's lotus feet. Then she thought (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.8.42):\\n\\n\\naha� mam�sau. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"It is by the influence of the Supreme Lord's m�y� that I am wrongly\\nthinking that Nanda Mah�r�ja is my husband, that K���a is my son, and that\\nbecause I am the queen of Nanda Mah�r�ja, all the wealth of cows and calves\\nare my possessions and all the cowherd men and their wives are my subjects.\\nActually, I also am eternally subordinate to the Supreme Lord. He is my\\nultimate shelter.\"*\\n\\n     With these words Mother Yasoda firmlytakes shelter of the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead. Speaking the words \"Nanda Mah�r�ja is my husband,\\nthat K���a is my son,\" she refers to Lord K���a, who was then before her\\neyes. Then she says: \"I am bewildered (kumati�) by these many\\nmanifestations of the universal form (yan-m�yay�).\" Then she says: \"the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead (sa�) is the goal of my life (gati�). There\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m� explained (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.8.43):\\n\\n\\nittha� vidita-tattv�yam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Mother Ya�od�, by the grace of the Lord, could understand the real\\ntruth. But then again, the supreme master, by the influence of the internal\\npotency, Yogam�y�, inspired her to become absorbed in intense maternal\\naffection for her son.\"*\\n\\n     Here the word \"tattva\" reefers to her son. Here the words \"sa ��vara�\"\\nrefer to Lord K���a's feature as the supreme controller.In �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.8.42 the words \"pranatasmi tat-padam\" mean \"Let me simply\\noffer obeisances, for He is beyond my contemplation\". In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.8.43 the words \"vai��v�� m�y�m\" may mean \"the Supreme Lord's internal\\npotency\", or they may also mean \"the Supreme Lord's mercy\". The it is\\nexplained in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.45):\\n\\n\\ntrayy� copani�adbhi� ca. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The glories of the Supreme Personality of Godhead are studied through\\nthe three Vedas, the Upani�ads, the literature of sa�khya-yoga, and other\\nVai��ava literature, yet mother Ya�od� considered the Supreme Person her\\nordinary child.\"*\\n\\n     It is also said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.9.21):\\n\\n\\nn�ya� sukh�po bhagav�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead, K���a, the son of mother Ya�od�,\\nis accessible to devotees engaged in spontaneous loving service, but He is\\nnot as easily accessible to mental speculators, to those striving for self-\\nrealization by severe austerities and penances, or to those who consider\\nthe body the same as the self.\"*\\n\\n     It these words the �r�mad-Bh�gavatam sings thre glories of Mother\\nYa�od�. It is also said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.46.18-19):\\n\\n\\napi smarati na� k���a�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Does K���a remember us? Does He remember His mother and His friends\\nand well-wishers? Does He remember the cowherds and their village of Vraja,\\nof which He is thr master? Does He remember the cows, V�nd�vana forest, and\\nGovardhana Hill?***\\n\\n\\napy �ya�yati govinda�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Will Govinda return even once to see His family? If He ever does, we\\nmay then glance upon His beautiful face, with its beautiful eyes, nose, and\\nsmile.\"***\\n\\n     After Nanda Mah�r�ja had spoken these words, Uddhava, following the\\nway of this world, to console him spoke these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.46.30):\\n\\n\\nyuv�� �l�ghyatamau n�nam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O respectful Nanda, certainly you and mother Ya�od� are the most\\npraiseworthy persons in the entire world, since you have developed such a\\nloving attitude toward Lord N�r�ya�a, the spiritual master of all living\\nbeings.\"***\\n\\n     Even as Uddhava spoke these words of praise, Nanda's remained\\nconvinced that K���a was his son and not the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead. He could nto be moved from that idea. Then it is said in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.46.44):\\n\\n\\neva� ni�� sa bhruvator vyat�t�\\n     nandasya k����nucarasya r�jan. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"While K���a's messenger continued speaking with Nanda, the night\\nended, O king. The women of the cowherd village rose from bed and, lighting\\nlamps, worshiped their household deities. Then they began churning yogurt\\ninto butter.\"***\\n\\n     In this way Uddhava spoke many words to console Nanda Maharaja, who\\nwas griving in separation from K���a. No matter what Uddhava said, Nanda\\nremained always convinced that K���a was his son and not the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead. The truth of Uddhava's words, that Lord K���a is\\nindeed the original form of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, is\\nelaboratedly explained in the K���a-sandarbha. Even at Kuruksetra, when a\\ngreat host of saintly devotees affirmed that Lord K���a is indeed the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead, Nanda and Ya�od� remained unconvinced. They\\ncontinued to believe that K���a was only their son. This is seen in the\\nfollowing words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.35):\\n\\n\\nt�v �tm�sanam �ropya\\n     b�hubhy�� parirabhya ca\\nya�od� ca mah�-bh�g�\\n     sutau vijahatu� �uca�\\n\\n\\n     \"Raising their two sons onto their laps and holding Them in their\\narms, Nanda and saintly mother Ya�od� forgot their sorrow.\"***\\n\\n     It is also seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.66):\\n\\n\\nmanaso v�ttayo na� syu�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"May our thoughts always take shelter of K���a's lotus feet, may our\\nwords always chant His names, and may our bodies always bow down to Him and\\nserve Him.\"***\\n\\n     Even though the truth that �r� K���a is the all-powerful Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead was again and again proved to him, Nanda Mah�r�ja\\ncontinued to believe K���a was only his son and nothing more. These words\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.66) Nanda spoke to Uddhava mean: \"We pray that\\nbirth after birth we may love K���a.\" This prayer of love spoken by Nanda\\nis filled with anur�ga love. Loev is not absent from it. Then it is said in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.65):\\n\\n\\nta� nirgata� sam�s�dya\\n     n�nop�yana-p�naya�\\nnand�dayo 'nur�ge�a\\n     pr�vocann a�ru-locan��\\n\\n\\n     \"As Uddhava was about to leave, Nanda and the others approached him\\nbearing various items of worship. With tears in their eyes they addressed\\nhim as follows.\"***\\n\\n     In these verses it is seen that Nanda and Ya�od� are filled with\\nanur�ga ecstatic love for Lord K���a. Their devotion to the Lord is not in\\nthe mood of awe and veneration, the mood of appreciating Lord K���a's\\nsupreme power and opulence. Here Uddhava thinks: \"Although I am far from\\nattaining this kind of good fortune, I still pray that someday I will\\nattainj this kind of ecstatic love. I pray this love will nto be denied to\\nme.\" With a voice choked with emotion Uddhava prays in this way. Praying\\nthat in some other lifetime he may some day attain this kind of anur�ga\\necstatic love, Uddhava says (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.67):\\n\\n\\nkarmabhir bhramyam�n�n�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Wherever we are made to wander about this world by the Supreme Lord's\\nwill, in accordance to the reactions to our fruitive work, may our good\\nworks and charity always grant us love for Lord K���a.\"***\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.66 may be interpreted in two ways. It may be\\ninterpreted to refer to v�tsalya-rasa alone, or it may also be interpreted\\nto refer to the other rasas also. Here the phrase beginning with the word\\n\"p�da\" (taking shelter of K���a's feet) is considered appropriate in\\nv�tsalya-rasa because Nanda Mah�r�ja feels great anguish because he cannto\\nassociate with K���a who has gone to a faraway country. A similar situation\\nin karu�a-rasa is seen in the story of Mah�r�ja Citraketu. Here the word\\n\"prahvana\" means \"bowing down\". In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.67 the word\\n\"��vare\" is used, as it was used before, as an affectionate term used to\\naddress a child. It is sometimes used in that way in this world. These are\\nincluded amongst the udbh�svaras. Now the s�ttvika-bh�vas will be\\nconsidered. Of these there are eight, as were described before. For Lord\\nK���a's mother there is a ninth s�ttvika-bh�va, which is milk flowing from\\nher breasts. Now the sa�c�ri-bh�vas will be considered. These are: 1. those\\nborn from the pastimes Lord K���a directly performs, and 2. those born from\\nthe pastimes of power and opulence manifest with thr aid of Lord K���a's\\npastime potency. The first of these is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.8.34):\\n\\n\\nkasm�n m�dam �dant�tman. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear K���a, why are You so restless that You have eaten dirt in a\\nsolitary place? This complaint has been lodged against You by all Your\\nplaymates, including Your elder brother, Balar�ma. How is this?\"***\\n\\n     This is an example of Lord K���a's restlessness. The second of these\\nsa�c�ri-bh�vas (those pastimes of power and opulence manifest with the aid\\nof Lord K���a's pastime potency) is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.8.37):\\n\\n\\ns� tatra dad��e vi�vam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When K���a opened His mouth wide by the order of Mother Ya�od�, she\\nsaw within His mouth all moving and nonmoving entities, outer space, and\\nall directions, along with mountains, islands, oceans, the surface of the\\nearth, the blowing wind, fire, the moon, and stars. She saw the planetary\\nsystems, water, light, air, sky, and creation by transformation of\\naka�k�ra. She also saw the senses, the mind, sense perception, and the\\nthree qualities goodness, passion, and ignorance. She saw the time allotted\\nfor the living entities, she saw natural instinct and the reactions of\\nkarma, and she saw desires and different varieties of bodies, moving and\\nnonmoving. Seeing all these aspects of the cosmic manifestation, along with\\nherself and V�nd�vana-dh�ma, she became doubtful and fearful of her son's\\nnature.\"*\\n\\n     In this passage is seen Mother Ya�od�'s wonder and doubt. Here the\\nsth�yi-bh�va is v�tsalya-rasa. This sth�yi-bh�va is seen in these owrds of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.8.23):\\n\\n\\ntan-m�tarau nija-sutau gh��ay� snuvantyau\\n     pa�k��ga-r�ga-rucir�v upag�hya dorbhy�m\\ndattv� stana� prapibato� sma mukha� nir�k�ya\\n     mugdha-smit�lpa-da�ana� yayatu� pramodam\\n\\n\\n     \"Dressed with muddy earth muxed with cow dung and cow urine, the\\nbabies looked very beautiful, and when They went to Their mothers, both\\nYa�od� and Rohi�� picked Them up with great affection, embraced Them, and\\nallowed Them to suck the milk flowing from their breasts. While sucking the\\nbreast, the babies smiled, and Their small teeth were visible. Their\\nmothers, upon seeing those beautiful teeth, enjoyed great transcendental\\nbliss.\"*\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"tan-m�tarau\" means {.sy 168}the mothers of\\nK���a and Balar�ma\", and \"gh��ay�\" means \"with compassion\". �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.8.23 was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
