      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 16563,
      "content": "\\n\\n     It was after these pastimes that the citizens of Dv�rak� spoke these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.11.9):\\n\\n\\nyarhy ambuj�k��pasas�ra bho bhav�n\\n     kur�n madh�n v�tha suh�d-did�k�ay�\\n\\n\\n     \"O lotus-eyed Lord, whenever You go away to Mathur�-V�nd�vana or\\nHastin�pura to meet Your friends and relatives, every moment of Your\\nabsence seems like a million years. O infallible one, at that time our eyes\\nbecome useless, as if bereft of sun.\"*\\n\\n     In �r� K���a-sandarbha (Anuccheda 174) it was explained that Lord\\nK���a resides in Vraja eternally in His apraka�a (unmanifest) form, and\\ntherefore they people of vraja are never truly separated from Him. Their\\ndesire to associate with Him is always satisfied. In this way v�tsalya-rasa\\nhas been described.\\n     Now sakhya-rasa will be described. In this rasa the �lambana is Lord\\nmanifest as a friend. The vi�aya is Lord K���a. The �sraya is the friends\\nwho aprticipate in Lord K���a's glorous pastimes. In this rasa the Lord\\nsometimes manifests a form-armed form and other times manifests a humanlike\\nform. The four-armed form is described by Arjuna in these words of Bhagavad-\\ng�t� (11.46):\\n\\n\\ntenaiva r�pe�a catur-bhujena\\n     sahasra-b�ho bhava vi�va-m�rte\\n\\n\\n     \"O universal form, O thousand-armed Lord, I wish to see You in Your\\nfour-armed form.\"*\\n\\n     Afterward, when Lord K���a's humanlike form was manifest, Arjuna said\\nin Bhagavad-g�t� (11.51):\\n\\n\\nd���veda� m�nu�a� r�pa�\\n     tava saumya� jan�rdana\\nid�n�m asmi samv�tta�\\n     sa-ceta� prak�ti� gata�\\n\\n\\n     \"When Arjuna saw K���a in His origina form, he said: O Jan�rdana,\\nseeing this humanlike form, so very beautiful, I am now composed in mind,\\nand I am restored to my original nature.\"*\\n\\n     Amongst the devotees in sakhya-rasa are the mitras, suh�ts, and\\nsakh�s. Among the suh�ts, �r� Bhimasena and �r� Draupad� are prominent.\\nAmong the sakh�s, �r�m�n Arjuna and �r�d�m� Vipra are prominent. In Gokula\\nthe gopa �r�d�m� is prominent. These great devotees are glorified in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam and other scriptures. In the Agama-sastras, Vasud�m�, Ki�kini,\\nand other devotees in sakhya-rasa are described. In the Bhavi�ya Pur��a's\\ndescription of pastimes of wrestling, the names of some devotees in sakhya-\\nrasa are given in these words:\\n\\n\\nsubhadra-ma��al�bhadra-\\n     bhadravardhana-gobha���\\nyak�endrabha�a�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Subhadra, Ma��al�bhadra, Bhadravardhana, Gobha�a, and Yak�endrabha�a\\nparticipated in these pastimes.\"\\n\\n     The gopas in sakhya-rasa are also described in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.12.2):\\n\\n\\ntenaiva s�ka� p�thuk�� sahasra�a�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"At that time, hundreds and thousands of cowherd boys came out of\\ntheir respective homes in Vrajabh�mi and joined K���a, keeping before them\\ntheir hundreds and thousands of groups of calves. The boys were very\\nbeautiful, and they were equipped with lunch bags, bugles, flutes, and\\nsticks for controlling the calves.\"*\\n\\n     These gopas were all Lord K���a's equals. It is said in the\\nscriptures:\\n\\n\\ngopai� sam�na-gu�a-��la-vayo-vil�sa-va�ai� ca\\n\\n\\n     \"In their qualities, character, age, pastimes, and garments, the gopa\\nboys were like K���a.\"\\n\\n     Of the gopas it is also said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.18.11):\\n\\n\\ngopa j�ti-praticchann�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O King, demigods disguised themselves as members of the cowherd\\ncommunity and, just as dramatic dancers praise another dancer, worshiped\\nK���a and Balar�ma, who were also appearing as cowherd boys.\"***\\n\\n     This verse has been explained in �r� K���a-sandarbha, Anuccheda 117.\\nThe gopas' great learning is seen in these words they spoke in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.23.8):\\n\\n\\nd�ks�y�� pa�u-sa�sth�y��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Except during the interval between the initiation of the performer of\\na sacrifice and the actual sacrifice of the animal, O most pure br�hma�as,\\nit is not contaminating for even the initiated to partake of food, at least\\nin sacrifices other than the Sautr�ma�i.\"***\\n\\n     The gopas' expert artistic skill is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.18.13):\\n\\n\\nkvacin n�tyatsu c�nye�u. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"While the other boys were dancing, O King, K���a and Balar�ma would\\nsometimes accompany them with song and instrumental music, and sometimes\\nthe two Lords would praise the boys, saying, ``Very good! Very good!' \"***\\n\\n     In these words Lord K���a Himself praised the gopas' glorious\\nqualities. The gopas are of three kinds: 1. sakh� (ordinary friends), 2.\\npriya-sakh� (more confidential friends), and 3. priya-narma-sakh� (intimate\\nfriends). The gopa �r�d�m�a and the other gopas enjoy very sweeet pastimes\\nwith Lord K���a. They are described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.12.11):\\n\\n\\nittha� sat��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"In this way, all the cowherd boys used to play with K���a, who is the\\nsource of the Brahman effulgence for j��n�s desiring to merge into that\\neffulgence, who is the Supreme Personality of Godhead for devotees who have\\naccepted eternal servitorship, and who for ordinary persons is but another\\nordinary child. The cowherd boys, having accumulated the results of pious\\nactivities for many lives, were able to associate in this way with the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead. How can one explain their great fortune?\"*\\n\\n     In this rasa lord K���a is the �lambana. Lord K���a as the �lambana is\\ndescribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.21.5):\\n\\n\\nbarh�p��a� na�a-vara-vapu�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Wearing a peacock-feather ornament upon His head, blue karnik�ra\\nflowers on His ears, a yellow garment as brilliant as gold, and the\\nVaijayant� garland, Lord K���a exhibited His transcendental form as the\\ngreatest of dancers as He entered the forest of V�nd�vana, beautifying it\\nwith the marks of His footprints. He filled the holes of His flute with the\\nnectar of His lips, and the cowherd boys sang His glories.\"***\\n\\n     Mow the udd�panas in this rasa will be considered. Amongst these\\nudd�panas are included the following transcendental qualities: loving\\nfriendship, straightforwardness, gratitude, intelligence, learning, glory,\\nexpert skill, heroism, strength, tolerance, mercy, and giving delight to\\nthe people. Also included amongst these udd�panas are youthful\\nhandsomeness, all-auspicious bodily features, and other qualities. For the\\nsuh�t friends the qualities beginning with straightforwardness are\\nprominent. For the sakh� friends the qualities beginning with handsomeness\\nand skill are prominent. For the mitra friends the qualities beginning with\\nstraightforwardness, handsomeness, and skill are prominent. The suh�t,\\nsakh�, and mitra kinds of friendship are mentioned by lamenting Arjuna in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.15.4):\\n\\n\\nsakhya� maitr�� sauh�da� ca. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Remembering Lord K���a and His well wishes, benefactions, intimate\\nfamilial relations and His chariot driving, Arjuna, overwhelmed and\\nbreathing very heavily, began to speak.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a's friendly affection for the gopas is seen in these words\\nof �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.13.13 and 16):\\n\\n\\nt�n d�s�v� bhaya-santrast�n\\n     �ce k���o'sya bh�-bhayam\\nmitr��y ���n m� viramate-\\n     h�ne�ye vatsak�n aham\\n\\n\\n     \"When K���a saw that His friends the cowherd boys were frightened, He,\\nthe fierce controller even of fear itself, said, just to mitigate their\\nfear, `My dear friends, do not stop eating. I shall bring your calves back\\nto this spot by personally going after them Myself.' \"***\\n\\n\\ntato vats�n ad���vaitya\\n     puline 'pi ca vatsap�n\\nubh�v api vane k���o\\n     vicik�ya samantata�\\n\\n\\n     \"Thereafter, when K���a was unable to find the calves, He returned to\\nthe bank of the river, but there He was also unable to see the cowherd\\nboys. Thus He began to search for both the calves and the boys, as if He\\ncould not understand what had happened.\"*\\n\\n     The meaning of these verses (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.13.13 and 16) is\\nclear. These verses were spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
