      "content": "\\n\\n     Now other features of sakhya-rasa will be considered. Amongst the\\nanubhavas are the udbh�svaras. Amongst the suhrt friends the udbh�svaras\\ninclude attempts to bring limitless good fortune to K���a, discussion of\\nwhat is proper and improper, smiling conversations, and other like\\nactivities. Amongst the sakh� friends the udbh�svaras include activities of\\nunwavering affection, activities like various playful games, singing,\\nartistic skill, eating together, sitting down together, taking naps, and\\nother like pastimes. Telling jokes, hearing descriptions of confidential\\npastimes, and other like activities are also included here. These friends\\nare glorified in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.12.11):\\n\\n\\nittha� sat��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"In this way, all the cowherd boys used to play with K���a, who is the\\nsource of the Brahman effulgence for j��n�s desiring to merge into that\\neffulgence, who is the Supreme Personality of Godhead for devotees who have\\naccepted eternal servitorship, and who for ordinary persons is but another\\nordinary child. The cowherd boys, having accumulated the results of pious\\nactivities for many lives, were able to associate in this way with the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead. How can one explain their great fortune?\"*\\n\\n     The sakh� friends are also described in these owrds of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.18.9-14):\\n\\n\\nprav�la-barha-stabaka-\\n     srag-dh�tu-k�ta-bh��an�h\\nr�ma-k����dayo gop�\\n     nan�tur yuyudhur jaguh\\n\\n\\n     \"Decorating themselves with newly grown leaves, along with peacock\\nfeathers, garlands, clusters of flower buds, and colored minerals,\\nBalar�ma, K���a and Their cowherd friends danced, wrestled and sang.\"***\\n\\n\\nk���asya n�tyata� kecij\\n     jagu� kecid av�dayan\\nve�u-p��italai� ���gaih\\n     pra�a�a�sur ath�pare\\n\\n\\n     \"As K���a danced, some of the boys accompanied Him by singing, and\\nothers by playing flutes, hand cy