      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 17924,
      "content": "\\n\\n     In this way the great glory of the gop�s' love for Lord K���a is seen.\\nTheir love is gloriosu because it is forbidden, because it is love for a\\nparamour (parak�ya). Even in ordinary affairs of the materuial world, this\\nkind of forbidden love is understood to be very intense. The author Bharata\\nexplains:\\n\\n\\nbahu v�ryate yata� khalu\\n     yatra pracchanna-k�mukatva� ca\\ny� ca mitho durlabhat�\\n     s� param� manmathasya rati�\\n\\n\\n     \"The highest amorous pleasure is attained when love is forbidden, when\\nthe lovers must conceal their love, when the lovers rarely meet.\"\\n\\n     The author Rudra explains:\\n\\n\\nv�mat� durlabhatva� ca\\n     str���� ya ca niv�ra��\\ntad eva pa�ca-b��asya\\n     manye paramam �yudham\\n\\n\\n     \"Contrariness, difficulty of attainment, and rejection by women I\\nconsider K�madeva's greatest weapons.\"\\n\\n     The author Vi��u-gupta explains:\\n\\n\\nyatra ni�edha-vi�e�a�\\n     su-durlabhatva� ca yan m�g�k����m\\ntatraiva n�gar����\\n     nirbharam �sajjate h�dayam\\n\\n\\n     \"When love is forbidden and the doe-eyed beloved is difficult to\\nattain, then the passionate lover's heart becomes very attached to his\\nbeloved.\"\\n\\n     Some gop� girls prayed to Goddess Katyayani that they might attain\\nLord K���a as their husband. Their desire to attain Him was very strong.\\nBecause K���a was unattainable for them, their love for Him was very great.\\nThe greatness oftheir love is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.83.43):\\n\\n\\nvraja-striyo yad v��chanti. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"We desire the same contact with the Supreme Lord's feet that thr\\nyoung women of Vraja, the cowherd boys, and even the aborigine Pulinda\\nwomen desire-the touch of the dust He leaves on the plants and grass as He\\ntends His cows.\"***\\n\\n     Their love is also described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.47.58):\\n\\n\\nv��chanti yad bhava-bhiya�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Among all persons on earth, these cowherd women alone have actually\\nperfected their embodied lives, for they have achieved the perfection of\\nunalloyed love for Lord Govinda. Their pure love is hankered after by those\\nwho fear material existence, by great sages, and by ourselves as well. For\\none who has tasted the narrations of the infinite Lord, what is the use of\\ntaking birth as a high-class br�hma�a, or even as Lord Barhm� himself?\"***\\n\\n     Still, the gs' intesne desire to attain Lord K���a was not born merely\\nbecause they were forbidden to associate with Him. They certrainly did not\\nwish to be forbidden or to have so many obstacles placed befope them.\\nRather they loved Him in spite of being so strongly forbidden, This is\\ndescribed in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.61):\\n\\n\\ny� dustyajam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The gop�s of V�nd�vana have given up the association of their\\nhusbands, sons, and other family members, who are very difficult to give\\nup, andf they have forsaken the path of chastity to take shelter of tyhe\\nlotus feet of Mukunda, K���a, which one should search for by Vedic\\nknowledge. Oh, let me be fortunate enough to be one of the bushes,\\ncreepers, or herbs in V�nd�vana, because the gop�s trample them and bless\\nthem with the dust of their feet.\"*\\n\\n     As a wild elephant cannot be stopped, so the gop�s' passionate love\\nfor K���a could not be stopped. Although the gop�s were all forbidden in\\nthe smae way to associate with Lord K���a, still they each had a different\\nintensity of love for Him. Of them all R�dh� had the greatest love. She had\\nthe greatest power to bring Lord K���a under Her control. The supremely\\nexalted position of �r� R�dh�'s love is indirectly described in these words\\nof �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.28):\\n\\n\\nanay�r�dhito n�nam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Certainly this particular gop� has perfectly worshiped the all-\\npowerful Personality of Godhead, Govinda, since He was so pleased with Her\\nthat He abandoned the rest of us and brought Her to a secluded place.\"***\\n\\n     In tyhis way the gop�s' love for Lord K���a blossomed. It was as if\\nthey had been bitten by the black snake of love for K���a. They did not\\nassociate with K���a as a wife associates with her husband. Rather they\\npassionately loved K���a as a girl loves her paramour. This kind of love is\\ncriticized in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.7-8):\\n\\n\\nni�sva� tyajanti ga�ik�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Prostitutes abandon a penniless man.\"***\\n\\n\\nj�r� bhuktv� rat�� striyam\\n\\n\\n     \"A lover abandons the woman he has enjoyed, even though she remains\\nattached to him.\"***\\n\\n     It is said that girls who love someone other than their husbands\\n(parak�ya) are fallen and worthy of contempt. This is true for girls who\\ntake shelter of individual souls who live in the material world. The\\nsituation of such girls is abominable. However these defects asnd\\ncriticisms do not apply to the gop�s. This truth is seen in the following\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.33.35):\\n\\ngop�n�� tat-pat�n�� ca. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"He who lives as the overseeing witness within the gop�s and their\\nhusbands, and indeed within all embodied living beings, assumes forms in\\nthis world to enjoy transcendental pastimes.\"***\\n\\n     From the material point of view it seemed that the gop�s had husbands\\nother than K���a. However, from the spiritual point of view these so-called\\nhusbands did not exist. In the K���a-sandarbha it has already been shown\\nthat the gop�s' so-called husbands were in truth only a display manifested\\nfrom Lord K���a's internal potency. Therefore it is Lord K���a Himself who\\nis ultimately the gs only true husband. The gop�s' exalted position, which\\nhas so equal or superior, is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.47.58):\\n\\n\\net�� para� tanu-bh�ta�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Among all persons on earth, these cowherd women alone have actually\\nperfected their embodied lives, for they have achieved the perfection of\\nunalloyed love for Lord Govinda. Their pure love is hankered after by those\\nwho fear material existence, by great sages, and by ourselves as well. For\\none who has tasted the narrations of the infinite Lord, what is the use of\\ntaking birth as a high-class br�hma�a, or even as Lord Barhm� himself?\"***\\n\\n     The gop�s' glories are also described in these words of Ujjvala-\\nn�lama�i:\\n\\n\\nne��� yad a�gini rase kavibhi� paro�h�\\n     tad gokul�mbuja-d���� kulam antare�a\\n��a�say� rasa-vidher avat�rit�n��\\n     ka�s�ri�� rasika-ma��ala-�ekhare�a\\n\\n\\n     \"Philosophers do not praise adultery. However, they do praise the\\npastimes Lord K���a, the crown of all rasikas, enjoys with Gokula's lotus-\\neyed girls, girls who descended to this world to enjoy nectar pastimes with\\nHim.\"\\n\\n     The gop�s did not have even a dim reflection of contact with their so-\\ncalled husbands. This is seen in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.33.37):\\n\\n\\nn�s�yan khalu k����ya\\n     mohit�s tasya m�yay�\\nmanyam�n�� sva-p�r�va-sth�n\\n     sv�n sv�n d�r�n vrajaukasa�\\n\\n\\n     \"The cowherd men, bewildered by K���a's illusory potency, thought\\ntheir wives had remained home at their sides. Thus they did not harbor any\\njealous feelings against Him.\"***\\n\\n     The gop�s are eternally dear to Lord K���a. They are His consorts\\neternally. This verse explains: \"The cowherd men, bewildered by K���a's\\nillusory potency, thought their wives had remained home at their sides.\\nThus they did not harbor any jealous feelings against Him.\"*** This verse\\nwas spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
