      "sourceLabel": "Anuccheda 284",
      "headingType": "anuccheda",
      "sourceStartLine": 18229,
      "content": "\\n\\n     The gop�s' songs are also described in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.33.9):\\n\\n\\nk�cit sama� mukundena\\n     svara j�t�r ami�rit��\\nunninye p�jit� tena\\n     pr�yat� s�dhu s�dhv iti\\ntad eva dhruvam unninye\\n     tasyai m�na� ca bahv ad�t\\n\\n\\n     \"One gop�, joining Lord Mukunda in His singing, sang pure melodious\\ntones that rose harmoniously above His. K���a was pleased and showed great\\nappreciation for her performance, saying `Excellent! Excellent!' Then\\nanother gop� repeated the same melody, but in a special metrical pattern,\\nand K���a praised her also.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"svar��\" means the scale of seven notes\\nbeginning with the sixth note\", and {.sy 168}j�taya�\" means \"the r�ga that\\ncomes from that scale\". Here \"amisritah\" means that because K���a and the\\ngop�s were very expert, the melodies they sang did not become mixed. The\\nmelodies remained pure, and \"unninye\" means \"the melodies were gloriously\\nsung\". The gop�s sang together with Lord K���a, K���a whose melodies even\\nBrahm�, �iva, Indra and all the demigods have no power to understand. The\\nsinging of K���a and the gop�s was equal. The singing of one did not\\ndominate the singing of the other. The words \"p�jit� tena\" mean \"with their\\ntreble voices the gop�s worshiped Lord K���a\". Here the word \"dhruvam\"\\nmeans \"a gop� sang a melody in the dhruva rhythm, and \"tasyai m�nam ad�t\"\\nmeans \"Lord K���a praised her\". This verse was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva\\nGosv�m�.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 285 and 286\\n\\n\\n     Among the ordinary (s�m�nya) conjugal lovers of Lord K���a, Kubj� is\\nthe most exalted. Among the queens married (svak�ya) to Lord K���a, �r�\\nRukmi�� and �r� Satyabh�m� are mosty exalted. They are described in the\\nfollowing words of Hari-va��a:\\n\\n\\nku�umbasye�var� c�s�d\\n     rukmi�� bh��mak�tmaj�\\nsatyabh�mottam� str����\\n     saubh�gye c�dhik�bhavat\\n\\n\\n     \"Bh��maka's daughter, Rukmi��, is the controller of Lord K���a's\\nhousehold. Satyabh�m� is the most fortunate of Lord K���a's wives.\"\\n\\n     The most exalted of the goddesslike gop�s of Vraja are described in\\nthese words of the Bhavi�ya Pur��a, Uttara-kha��a:\\n\\n\\ngop�l� p�lik� dhany�\\n     vi��kh� dhy�na-ni��hik�\\nr�dh�nur�dh� som�bh�\\n     t�rak� da�am� tath�\\n\\n\\n     \"The ten most prominent gop�s are Gop�l�, P�lik�, Dhany�, Vi��kh�,\\nDhy�na-ni��hik�, R�dh�, Anur�dh�, Som�bh�, and T�rak�.\"\\n\\n     Here the words \"t�rak� da�am�\" mean \"the tenth gop� is named T�rak�\".\\nIn ther Skanda Purana, Prahlada-samhita the four gop�s Lalit�, �aiby�,\\nPadm�, and Bhadr� are included amongst the most prominent gop�s. In other\\nplaces in the scriptures Candr�val� is also included amongst the prominent\\ngop�s. The Som�bh� mentioned in the previous quote from the Bhavi�ya Pur��a\\nis the same as Candr�val�. In the Agama-��stras it is said:\\n\\n\\npramad�-�ata-ko�ibhir �kulita�\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a is surrounded by hundreds and millions of beautiful\\ngop�s.\"\\n\\n     Amongst all the gop�s, R�dh� is the most important. It is because of\\nher great love that Lord K���a disappeared from the r�sa dance. This has\\nalready been described in the �r� K���a-sandarbha (Anuccheda 189). R�dh� is\\ncelebrated in all the scriptures. In the Gop�la-t�pan� Upani�ad she is\\nknown by the name G�ndharvik�.\\n\\n     Lord K���a's beloveds are of three kinds: 1. mugdh� (meek), 2. madhy�\\n(niteermediate), and 3. pragalbh� (arrogant).  They are also of three ages:\\n1. nava-yauavana (new youth), 2. spa��a (clearly manifested youth), and 3.\\n(samyag-yauvana (fully blossomed youth). According to these different ages\\ndifferent kinds of activities are manifested. The age of samyag-yuavana is\\nsixteen years. It is not older than that. This is seeen in the following\\nwords of the Gautam�ya Tantra:\\n\\nkany�bhir dvy-a��a-var��bhi�\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a is surrounded by sixteen-year-old gop�s.\"\\n\\n     The termperments of the Lord's beloveds are of three kinds: 1. dh�r�\\n(peaceful), 2. adh�r� (agitated), and 3. mi�ra-gu�� (a mixture of peace and\\nagitation). Their love for Lord K���a they are also of three kinds: 1.\\n�re��h� (best), 2. sam� (intermediate), and 3. laghu (least).\\n     In their pastimes with Lord K���a, His beloveds are in the following\\neight situations: 1. abhis�rik� (meeting K���a ina  rendezvous), 2. v�saka-\\nsajj� (dressing in fine garments and ornaments to prepare for a rendezvous\\nwith K���a), 3. utka��hit� (yearning to meet K���a at the rendezvous), 4.\\nkha��it� (jilted by K���a), 5. vipralabdh� (separated from K���a), 6.\\nkalah�ntarit� (feuding with K���a), 7. pro�ita-preyas� (separated from\\nK���a because He has gone to a faraway place), and 8. sv�dh�na-bhart�k�\\n(dominating K���a). In their realtions with each other, Lord K���a's\\nbeloveds have four kinds of natures: 1. s�d��ya (alike), ki�cit-s�d��ya\\n(somewhat alike), 3. aspa��a-s�d��ya (not clearly alike), and 4. virodhi\\n(opposed). These four natures are manifest as the following four kinds of\\nrelationships: 1. sakh� (friend), 2. suh�t (well-wisher), 3. ta�asth�\\n(neutral person), and pr�tipak�ik� (rival). In this way the Lord's beloveds\\nhave different natures. A sakh� spoke these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.30.11):\\n\\n\\napy e�a-patn�. . .tanvan d���� sakhi su-nirv�tim\\n\\n\\n     \"O friend, wife of the deer, has Lord Acyuta been here with His\\nbeloved, bringing great joy to your eyes? Indeed, blowing this way is the\\nfragrance of His garland of kun�a flowers, which was smeared with the\\nku�kuma from the breasts of His girlfriend when He embraced Her.\"***\\n\\n     Here the words \"tanvan d���� sakhi su-nirv�tim\" (O friend, has Lord\\nAcyuta been here with His beloved, bringing great joy to your eyes?) were\\nspoken by the gop�s because they yearned to see Lord K���a. Here the word\\n\"sakhi\" (O friend) shows that the gop�s imagined the deer to be like them,\\ndelighted by the sight of Lord K���a. In this way they imagined that the\\ndeer were their friends. Here the word \"k�nt�\" means \"the great good\\nfortune of Lord K���a's girlfriend\", \"kula-pate�\" means \"of Lord K���a\",\\nand \"k�nt��ga-sa�ge\" means \"delighted by the fragrance of the two lovers'\\nembraces\". The pleasures R�dh� and K���a enjoy in Their pastimes are\\ndescribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.12):\\n\\n\\nb�hu� priy��sa. . .\\n\\n\\n       \"O trees, we see that you are bowing down. When the younger brother\\nof R�ma walked by here, followed by intoxicated bees swarming around the\\ntulas� ma�jar�s decorating His garland, did He acknowledge your obeisances\\nwith His affectionate glances? He must have been resting His arm on the\\nshoulder of His beloved and carrying a lotus flower in His free hand.\"***\\n\\n     A suh�t (well-wisher) spoke these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.30.28):\\n\\n\\nanay�r�dhito n�na�\\n     bhagavan harir isvara�\\nyan no vih�ya govinda�\\n     pr�to y�m amayad raha�\\n\\n\\n     \"Certainly this particular gop� has perfectly worshiped the all-\\npowerful Personality of Godhead, Govinda, since He was so pleased with Her\\nthat He abandoned the rest of us and brought Her to a secluded place.\"***\\n\\n     Because she praises �r� R�dh�'s good fortune, the gop� who speak this\\nverse is a suh�t. After hearing a sakh� speak �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.11, a\\nThe ta�asth� (neutral person) spoke these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.30.13):\\n\\n\\np�cchatem� lat� b�h�n\\n     apy ��li��� vanaspate�\\nn�na� tat-karaja-sp����\\n     bibhraty utpulak�ny aho\\n\\n\\n     \"Let us ask these creepers about K���a. Even though they are embracing\\nthe arms of their husband, this tree, they certainly must have been touched\\nby K���a's fingernails, since out of joy they are manifesting eruptions on\\ntheir skin.\"***\\n\\n     After hearing a suh�t speak �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.28, a ta�asth�\\nspoke these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.29):\\n\\n\\ndhany� aho am� alya�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O girls! The dust of Govinda's lotus feet is so sacred that even\\nBrahm�, �iva and the goddess Ram� take that dust upon their heads to dispel\\nsinful reactions.\"***\\n\\n"
