      "sourceStartLine": 18526,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Here someone may protest: \"It is not possible that the devotees of the\\nLord can be rivals of each other. That is a horrible idea. It is said in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.48):\\n\\n\\nt�s�� tat-saubhaga-madam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \" `Lord Ke�ava, seeing the gop�s too proud of their good fortune,\\nwanted to relieve them of this pride and show them further mercy. Thus He\\nimmediately disappeared.'***\\n\\n     \"In this way it is seen that the Supreme Personality of Godhead wishes\\nto cast far away all envy, lust, and pride. �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m� here\\nuses the word `daur�tmya' (wickedness) to describe those vices.\"\\n\\n     To this protest I reply: If sometimes the Lord's devotees are rivals\\nof each other that is only to nourish their love for the Lord and to\\nnourish the Lord's pastimes. The Lord reveals His pastimes so the souls may\\nattain love for Him. This is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.33.36):\\n\\n\\nbhajate t�d���� kr���\\n     y�� �rutva tat-paro bhavet\\n\\n\\n     \"When the Lord assumes a humanlike body to show mercy to His devotees,\\nHe engages in such pastimes as will attract those who hear about them to\\nbecome dedicated to Him.\"***\\n\\n     Indeed, in ���g�ra-rasa (the mellow of conjugal love), the rasa is\\nnoursihed by a wonderful display of spiritual envy, lust, and pride. This\\ntruth is confirmed by the great philosophers. The Supreme Lord personally\\naccepts it in His pastimes. As the hero loved by His beloveds, the Supreme\\nLord manifests four features: 1. dak�i�a (pleasing), 2. anuk�la (a faithful\\nlover), 3. �a�ha (a cheat), and 4. dh���a (bold and dominating). The Lord's\\npastime potency (l�l�-�akti) manifests these various features in the\\npresence of the Lord's beloveds. In this way these features are seen.\\nHowever, when they are separated from the Lord, the Lord's beloveds feel\\ngreat distress. In that state of calamity they all feel friendship for each\\nother. This is seen in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.40):\\n\\n\\n\\nanvicchantyo bhagavato\\n     m�rga� gopyo 'vid�rita�\\ndad��u� priya-vi�le�an\\n     mohit�� du�khit�� sakh�m\\n\\n\\n     \"WhiIe continuing to search out K���a's path, the gop�s discovered\\ntheir unhappy friend close by. She was bewildered by separation from Her\\nlover.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the gop�s' feelingsof friendship for each other is\\nmanifested. Lord K���a manifests this pastime of being separated from Him\\nin order that the gop�s' thirst to attain Him may very quickly increase. In\\nthis way the gop�s' love for Lord K���a, the crest jewel of amorous heroes,\\nincreases. That this is the true reason Lord K���a arranges that His\\ndevotees be separated from Him is confirmed by Lord K���a Himself in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.32.20):\\n\\n\\nn�ha� tu sakhyo bhajato 'pi jant�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"But the reason I do not immediately reciprocate the affection of\\nliving beings even when they worship Me, O gop�s, is that I want to\\nintensify their loving devotion. They then become like a poor man who has\\ngained some wealth and then lost it, and who thus becomes so anxious about\\nit that he can think of nothing else.\"***\\n\\n     These pastimes are the Supreme Lord's attempt to make the gop�s free\\noffalse pride. This is seen in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.48, which was quoted\\nabove. In that verse the word {.sy 168}prasamaya\" means \"to make them free\\nof false pride\", and the word \"prasadaya\" means \"to give them mercy, mercy\\nthat took the form of making them thirst after attaining Him\". In this way\\nthe gop�s' good ofrtune (tat-subhaga) is seen. The word \"pras�d�ya\" may\\nalso be interpreted to mean \"to satisfy the gop�s' passioante desires, Lord\\nK���a enjoyed the r�sa dance pastime\", and the word {.sy 168}pras�d�ya\" may\\nalso be interpreted to mean \"to delight the gop�s, Lord K���a enjoyed the\\nr�sa-dance pastime\". The word \"daur�tmya\" here means \"separated fropm Lord\\nK���a, the gop�s became depressed at heart\". {.sy 168}Daur�tmya\" here does\\nnot mean \"wickedness\" because this passage describes pastimes of\\ntranscendental love.  �r�la �yukadeva Gisv�m�, the king of sages, then gave\\nthis description in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.41):\\n\\n\\ntay� kathitam �kar�ya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"She told them how M�dhava had given Her much respect, but how She\\nthen suffered dishonor because of Her misbehavior. The gopis were extremely\\namazed to hear this.\"***\\n\\n     Actually there was nothing wrong with that gop�s' pride. This is seen\\nin the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.34):\\n\\n\\nreme tay� sv�tma-rata\\n     �tm�r�mo 'py akha��ita�\\nk�min�� dar�ayan dainya�\\n     str���� caiva dur�tmat�m\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a enjoyed with that gop� although He enjoys only within,\\nbeing self-satisfied and complete in Himself. Thus by contrast He showed\\nthe wretchedness of ordinary lusty men and hardhearted women.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"sv�tma-rata�\" means {.sy 168}self-satisfied\",\\n\"�tm�r�ma�\" means \"who enjoys pastimes\", and \"apy akha��ita� reme\" means\\n{.sy 168}attached to that particular gop�, He enjoyed pastimes with Her\".\\nSomeone may ask: \"Why was Lord K���a so attached to that one gop�? Why did\\nHe enjoy pastimes with Her?\" The answer is given here in this verse, �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.30.35. It is also said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.7.10):\\n\\n\\nittham-bh�ta-gu�o hari�\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead possesses transcendental\\nqualities.\"\\n\\n     As the Supreme Personality of Godhead possesses transcendental\\nqualities, so does the gop� (�r� R�dh�) mentioned in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.30.34. Her quality is that She is filled with love for Lord K���a. Here\\nit is specifically said that Her love is pure spiritual love. It is not\\nmaterial lust. Spiritual love is very powerful and glorious. Still, this\\ngop�'s spiritual love may superfically seem like material lust, and in this\\nway it may manifest what seems to be material pride and material passion.\\nThus this gop� reveals a kind of love that seems to be like the\\nwretchedness (dainyam) of lusty women, a wretchedness that is well known in\\nthis material world. Thus this gop�s manifests stubbornness, willfulness,\\nand a personality that tries to dominate Her beloved. With this beloved\\nLord K���a enjoys pastimes (reme). Or, these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam may\\nbe interpreted in a different way. They may be interpreted to mean \"With\\nthese pastimes the Lord shows how wretched and insignificant are\\nmaterialistic men who try to enjoy amorous pastimes with lusty women in the\\nmaterial world. Manifesting the glory of His pastimes, the Lord shows how\\nwretched and devoid of true nectar are the activities (dainyam) of lusty\\nmen (k�minam) and how wretched is the nature (dur�tmat�m) of materialistic\\nwomen (str���m) who do not worship the Supreme Lord.\" The great glory of\\nthe gop�s' pastimes with Lord K���a is also seen in these words of\\nscripture:\\n\\n\\ndar�ayad vidhu-par�jaya� ram�-vaktram ullasati dh�ta-l��chanam\\n\\n\\n     \"As the goddess of fortune enjoys pastimes with the Supreme Lord, the\\nspotless moon of her face, a moon that defeats all other moons, shines with\\nbliss.\"\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.34 discussed in these words was spoken by\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
