      "content": "\\n\\n     Aud�rya is also seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.21):\\n\\n\\napi bata madhu-pury�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O Uddhava! It is indeed regrettable that K���a resides in Mathur�.\\nDoes He remember His father's household affairs and His friends, the\\ncowherd boys? O great soul! Does He ever talk about us, His maidservants?\\nWhen will He lay on our heads His aguru-scented hand? \"***\\n\\n     Dhairya is described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, Anubh�va-\\nprakara�a, Text 23:\\n\\n\\nsthir� cittonnatir y� tu\\n     tad dhairyam iti k�rtyate\\n\\n\\n     \"When the mind is very steady, that state is called dhairya.\"\\n\\n     Dhairya is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.17):\\n\\n\\nm�gayur iva kap�ndram. . .dustyajas tat-kath�rtha�\\n\\n\\n     \"Like a hunter He cruelly shot the king of the monkeys with arrows.\\nBecause He was conquered by a woman, He disfigured another woman who came\\nto Him with lusty desires. And even after consuming the gifts of Bali\\nMaharaja, He bound him up with ropes as if he were a crow. So let us give\\nup all friendship with this dark-compexioned boy, even if we can't give up\\ntalking about Him.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is clear. This verse was spoken by �r� R�dh�\\nHerself.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 324 and 325\\n\\n\\n     K�nti and D�pti are described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i,\\nAnubh�va-prakara�a, Texts 14 and 15:\\n\\n\\n�obhaiva k�ntir �khy�t�\\n     manmath�pyayanojjval�\\n\\n\\n     \"When beauty increases amorous desire, this stage is called k�nti.\"\\n\\n\\nk�ntir eva vayo-bhoga-\\n     de�a-k�la-gu��dibhi�\\nudd�pit�ti-vist�ra�\\n     pr�pt� ced d�ptir ucyate\\n\\n\\n     \"If beauty is greatly increased by youthfulness, amorous enjoyment,\\nplace, time, various qualities, or other things, that condition of\\nincreased beauty is called d�pti.\"\\n\\n     L�l� is described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, Anubh�va-\\nprakara�a, Text 24:\\n\\n\\npriy�nukara�a� l�l�\\n     ramyair ve�a-kriy�dibhi�\\n\\n\\n     \"L�l� is the imitation of the charming apperance, activities, or other\\nthings of the beloved.\"\\n\\n     L�l� is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.1-2):\\n\\n\\nantarhite bhagavati. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"When Lord K���a disappeared so suddenly, the gop�s felt great sorrow\\nat losing sight of Him, like a group of female elephants who have lost\\ntheir mate.\"***\\n\\n\\ngaty�nur�ga-smita. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"As the cowherd women remembered Lord K���a, their hearts were\\noverwhelmed by His movements and loving smiles, His playful glances and\\nenchanting talks, and by the many other pastimes He would enjoy with them.\\nThus absorbed in thoughts of K���a, the Lord of Ram�, the gop�s began\\nacting out His various transcendental pastimes.\"***\\n\\n     The pastimes the gop�s had enjoyed with K���a are seen in these words\\nof �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.46):\\n\\n\\nb�hu-pras�ra. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"K���a threw His arms around the gop�s and embraced them. He aroused\\nCupid in the beautiful young ladies of Vraja by touching their hands, hair,\\nthighs, belts, and breasts, by playfully scratching them with His\\nfingernails, and also by joking with them, glancing at them, and laughing\\nwith them. In this way the Lord enjoyed His pastimes.\"***\\n\\n     As the gop�s imitated Lord K���a's pastimes in their ecstasy of l�l�-\\nanubh�va, they acted as if they were not different from Lord K���a. This is\\nseen in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.3):\\n\\n\\ngati-smita-prek�a�a-bh�sa��di�u. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Because the beloved gop�s were absorbed in thoughts of their beloved\\nK���a, their bodies imitated His way of moving and smiling, His way of\\nbeholding them, His speech and His other distinctive features. Deeply\\nimmersed in thinking of Him and maddened by remembering His pastimes, they\\ndeclared to one another, `I am K��na!' \"***\\n\\n     As they imitated Lord K���a's pastimes, the gop�s' original nature\\nbecame concealed. This is seen in the following words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.30.20):\\n\\n\\nyatanty unnidadhe 'mbaram\\n\\n\\n     \" `Don't be afraid of the wind and rain,' said one gop�. `I will save\\nyou.' And with that she lifted her shawl above her head.\"***\\n\\n     Overcome with ecstatic love, the gop�s passed the time in this way,\\nimitating Lord K���a's pastimes. However, they did not imitate all of Lord\\nK���a's pastimes. They imitated His childhood pastimes and some other\\npastimes, but they did not imitate His pastimes in ���gara-rasa. The gs\\nimitated Lord K���a's pastimes with Putana and other enemies and they also\\nimitated Lord K���a's pastimes with His mother and His other loving\\nassociates. To increase their love for Lord K���a, they passed the time in\\nseparation from Him by artifically acting out His pastimes. They did not\\nbelieve that they were truly Lord K���a. Here some philosophers say: \"When\\nthey were remembering the pastime of killing P�tan�, some gop�s played the\\nrole of P�tan�. Even though it was only play-acting, the other gop�s became\\nfearful for child K���a's welfare. Even in the ordinary affairs of this\\nworld an expert actor playing the role of a ferocious tiger may bring a\\nmoment's fear to his audience. In this way, by feeling fear for Lord\\nK���a's safety in the course of this play-acting, the gop�s felt their love\\nfor Lord K���a increase. Also, the gop� playing the role of P�tan� did not\\nfeel hatred for Lord K���a. In these pastimes of play-acting the gop�s\\naways felt love for Lord K���a. In these pastimes the gop�s also assumed\\nthe role of Mother Ya�od�. An example of this is seen in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.23):\\n\\n\\nbaddh�nyay� sraj� k�cit. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"One gop� tied up her slender companion with a flower garland and\\nsaid, `Now I will bind this boy who has broken the butter pots and stolen\\nthe butter.' The second gop� then covered her face and beautiful eyes,\\npretending to be afraid.\"***\\n\\n     In this way they meditated on Lord K���a's D�modara pastimes, pastimes\\ndescribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (1.8.31):\\n\\n\\nvaktra� nin�ya bhaya-bh�vanay� sthitasya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear K���a, Ya�od� took up a rope to bind You when You committed\\nan offense, and Your perturbed eyes overflooded with tears, which washed\\nthe mascara from Your eyes. And You were afraid, though fear personified is\\nafraid of You. This sight is bewildering to me.\"*\\n\\n     In these words child K���a's fear of Mother Ya�od� is described. The\\ngop�s imitated this pastime and Lord K���a's other childhood pastimes. One\\ngop�, playing the part of Mother Ya�od�, tied up another gop�, who was\\nplaying the part of child K���a. As was said before, in the course of these\\npastimes of play-acting the gop�s always felt love for Lord K���a. In these\\npastimes the gop�s always took dhelter of their natural love for Lord\\nK���a. Even though one gop� played the role of Mother Ya�od� and another\\ngop� played the role of child K���a, their original love for Lord K���a in\\n���g�ra-rasa remained unchanged. �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.3 quoted in this\\nanuccheda was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
