      "sourceStartLine": 20222,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Mo���yita is described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, Anubh�va-\\nprakara�a, Text 42:\\n\\n\\nk�nta-smara�a-v�rt�dau\\n     h�di tad-bh�va-bh�vata�\\npr�ka�yam abhil��asya\\n     mo���yitam ud�ryate\\n\\n\\n     \"When the heroine remembers or hears about her lover, K���a, love for\\nHim is aroused in her heart, and she hankers for His association. This is\\ncalled mo���yita \"\\n\\n     Mo���yita is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.21.12):\\n\\n\\nk���a� nir�k�ya vanitotsava. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"K���a's beauty and character create a festival for all women. Indeed,\\nwhen the demigods' wives flying in airplanes with their husbands catch\\nsight of Him and hear His resonant flute-song, their hearts are shaken by\\nCupid, and they become so bewildered that the flowers fall out of their\\nhair and their belts loosen.\"***\\n\\n     Vik�ta is described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, Anubh�va-\\nprakara�a, Text 53:\\n\\n\\nhr�-m�ner�y�dibhir yatra\\n     nocyate sva-vik�sitam\\nvyajyate ce��ayaiveda�\\n     vik�ta� tad vidur budh��\\n\\n\\n     \"When shyness, anger, and jealousy are not openly expressed in words,\\nbut displayed only by action, that is known as vik�ta by the wise.\"\\n\\n     Vik�ta is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.22.23):\\n\\n\\nparidh�ya sva-v�s��si\\n     pre��ha-sa�gama-sajjit�h\\ng�h�ta-citt� no celus\\n     tasmin lajj�yitek�an�h\\n\\n\\n     \"The gop�s were addicted to associating with their beloved K���a, and\\nthus they became captivated by Him. Thus, even after putting their clothes\\non they did not move. They simply remained where they were, shyly glancing\\nat Him.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is clear. This verse was spoken by �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
