      "sourceStartLine": 20362,
      "content": "\\n\\n     Saml�pa is described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, Anubh�va-\\nprakara�a, Text 77:\\n\\n\\nukti-pratyukti-mad v�kya�\\n     saml�pa iti k�rtyate\\n\\n\\n     \"Conversation is called saml�pa.\"\\n\\n     Saml�pa is seen in the conversation recounted in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.29.18-41.\\n\\n     In this conversation Lord K���a has two purposes: 1. to request the\\nassociation of the contrary gop�s charmed by His flute music and other\\npastimes and qualities, and 2. as a joke, and also to test their love, to\\nreject the company of the gop�s, who had come to Him in the forest to\\nattain His association. Here the words of the gop�s also have two purposes:\\n1. To reject Lord K���a's request that they return home, and 2. with words\\nfilled with yearning to beg His company. Because Lord K���a and the gop�s\\nare equally expert in the art of graceful conversation, the rasa of thwir\\npastimes is nourshied by these talks between them. In the beginning of this\\nconversation (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.18) Lord K���a affirms that He and\\nthe gop�s are equal. Then He tells them in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.\\n\\n\\nrajany e��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"This night is quite frightening, and frightening creatures are\\nlurking about. Return to Vraja, slender-waisted girls. This is not a proper\\nplace for women.\"***\\n\\n     These words, however, have a second, a hidden meaning. By interpreting\\nthe words in a different way this verse comes to mean: \"O girls, somehow\\nyou have met Me here. Please do not now return to Vraja, for this night is\\nquite frightening. Therefore you girls should not leave this place.\\nInstead, you should stay here with Me, your hero.\" By speaking the word\\n{.sy 168}su-madhyam��\" (O slender-waisted girls) Lord K���a hints that He\\nwould become very unhappy if the gop�s left Him and returned to Vraja. Then\\nLord K���a tells them: \"As long as you stay with Me you need not fear your\\nrelatives. He says in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.20):\\n\\n\\nm�tara�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Not finding you at home, your mothers, fathers, sons, brothers and\\nhusbands are certainly searching for you. Don't cause anxiety for your\\nfamily members.\"***\\n\\n     The hidden meaning of this verse is: \"Your mothers and other relatives\\ndid not see you tonight. Therefore they are now searching for you. However,\\nthey will not be able to come here to this place.\" Thn word \"putr��\" (sons)\\nin this verse means either \"the sons of your brothers-in-law\" or \"the sons\\nof your co-wives\". Then Lord K���a tries to enliven the gop�s by showing\\nthem the beauties of His personal forest-garden. He tells them in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.29.21-22):\\n\\n\\nd���a� vanam. . .tad y�ta. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Now you have seen this V�nd�vana forest, full of flowers and\\nresplendent with the light of the full moon. You have seen the beauty of\\nthe trees, with their leaves trembling in the gentle breeze coming from the\\nYamun�. So now go back to the cowherd village. Don't delay. O chaste\\nladies, serve your husbands and give milk to your crying babies and\\ncalves.\"***\\n\\n     The hidden meaning of this verse is: \"The forest is very fearful.\\nTherefore please do not be eager to go to Vraja so soon.\" Or these words\\nmeay also be interpreted to mean: {.sy 168}Please don't leave now.\" They\\nmay also be interpreted to mean: \"Please don't faithfully serve your\\nhusbands.\" Here the word \"sat��\" means \"O best of girls\". {.sy 168}Vats� m�\\nkrandanti\" may mean \"the calvees are not crying\", \"m� p�yayata\" may mean\\n\"don't feed them milk\", \"m� duhyata\" may mean \"don't feed them with cow's\\nmilk\". Here Lord K���a hints: \"If you truly love me, then please don't\\nignore My request.\" Then Lord K���a places this logical argument before the\\ngop�s (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.23):\\n\\n\\natha v�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"On the other hand, perhaps you have come here out of your great love\\nfor Me, which has taken control of your hearts. This is of course quite\\ncommendable on your part, since all living entities possess natural\\naffection for Me.\"***\\n\\n     Here the word \"mayi\" is the same as mama\". In this verse Lord K���a\\nargues: \"If all living entirties naturally love Me, then beautiful girls\\nmay also love Me, thinking of Me as their beloved.\" Here someone may\\nprotest: {.sy 168}But is not wrong for women to renounce the vow of\\nfaithfully serving their husbands?\" To answer this proetst Lord K���a tells\\nthe gop�s (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.24):\\n\\n\\nbhartu� �u�r��a�am. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The highest religious duty for a woman is to sincerely serve her\\nhusband, behave well toward her husband's family and take good care of her\\nchildren.\"***\\n\\n     Here Lord K���a declares: \"The highest religious duty for a woman is\\nto sincerely serve her husband.\" However, Lord K���a implies here: \"But you\\ngirls have never been married to your so-called husbands. Your marriage to\\nthem is only an imagination, a myth. Therefore you do no wrong to abandon\\nthse mythical husbands. You may think these persons aree your husbands.\\nStill, the words of the Sm�ti-��stra affrim that you may indeed abandon the\\nvow of serving such mythical husbands.\" Then Lord K���a tells the gop�s\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.25):\\n\\n\\ndu���lo durbhago v�ddha�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Women who desire a good desfination in the next life should never\\nabandon a husband who has not fallen from his religious standards, even if\\nhe is obnoxious, unfortunate, old, unintelligent, sickly or poor.\"***\\n\\n     Here Lord K���a declares: \"A woman should never abandon a husband is\\nnot fallen.\" Here the hidden meaning of {.sy 168}fallen\" is \"envious\". In\\nthis sense Lord K���a considers the gop�s' mythical husbands to be envious\\nof Him, and therefore fallen. Thinking that the gop�s might consider their\\nhusbands not fallen and therefore stay with them, with these tricky words\\nchanging the original meaning of the Smrti-sastra's words, Lord K���a\\ndenies that the gop�s have any responsibility to serve their so-called\\nhusbands. Then Lord K���a tells the4 gop�s (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.26):\\n\\n\\nasvargyam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"For a woman from a respectable family, petty adulterous affairs are\\nalways condemned. They bar her from heaven, ruin her reputation and bring\\nher difficulty and fear.\"***\\n\\n     The hidden meaning of these words is: \"A woman who stays near (upa)\\nher husband (pati) (aupapatyam) will not enter heaven.\"\\n\\n     Thinking, \"the gop�s may worry that it will be very difficult to\\nattain love for Me\", Lord K���a tells them: {.sy 168}Please do not turn\\naway from Me.\" He tells them (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.27):\\n\\n\\n�rava��t. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Transcendental love for Me arises by the devotional processes of\\nhearing about Me, seeing My Deity form, meditating on Me and faithfully\\nchanting My glories. The same result is not achieved by mere physical\\nproximity. So please go back to your homes.\"***\\n\\n     Here Lord K���a says: \"Transcendental love for Me arises by the\\ndevotional processes that begin with hearing about Me. The same result is\\nnot achieved by mere physical proximity. So please enter this forest grove,\\nwhich is just like your own homes.\" That is the hidden meaning of Lord\\nK���a's request here. The other, original, meaning is well-known in this\\nworld. In this way, in these verses, with many joking words Lord K���a\\nrefutes the idea that the gop�s actions are in any way improper. The\\ncondition the gop�s attained after hearing these words of Lord K���a is\\ndescribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.28-30):\\n\\n\\niti vipriyam �kar�ya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Hearing these unpleasant words spoken by Govinda, the gop�s became\\nmorose. Their great hopes were frustrated and they felt insurmountable\\nanxiety.***\\n     \"Their herads hanging down and their heavy, sorrowful breathing drying\\nup their reddened lips, the gop�s scratched the ground with their toes.\\nTears flowed from their eyes, carrying their kajjala and washing away the\\nvermillion smeared on their breasts. Thus they stood, silently bearing the\\nburden of their unhappiness.***\\n     \"Although K���a was their beloved, and although they had abandoned all\\nother objects of desire for His sake, He had been speaking to hem\\nunfavorably. Nonetheless they remained unflinching in their attachment to\\nHim. Stopping their crying, they wiped their eyes and began to speak, their\\nvoices stammering with agitation.\"***\\n\\n     These verses show that thew gop�s did not understand the second,\\nhidden, meaning of Lord K���a's previous words. Fillied with longing to\\nassociate withe K���a, they considered His words unpleasant. Thus they\\nbecame morose. Hearing Lord K���a's statements that had two meanings, the\\ngop�s became anxious and bowed their faces and performed other like\\nactivities. There is no ras�bh�sa in any of these actions. They scratched\\nthe ground with their toes and they spoek to Lord K���a.\\n     The gop�s said to Lord K���a (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.31):\\n\\n\\nmaivam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The beautiful gop�s said: O all-powerful one, You should not speak in\\nthis cruel way. Do not reject us, who have renounced all material enjoyment\\nto render devotional service to Your lotus feet. Reciprocate with us, O\\nstubborn one, just as the primeval Lord, Sr� N�r�ya�a, reciprocates with\\nHis devotees in their endeavors for liberation.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"m�\" (don't) is used to refute Lord K���a's\\nrequest (that the gop�s return to their homes). Here the gop�s say: \"We\\nhave completely renounced our husbands, children, and all else and become\\nYour devotees. Please reciprocate with us.\" Speaking here the passage\\nbeginning with the word \"p�da-m�lam\" (the soles of Yoru feet), the gop�s\\nchant the glories of Lord K���a. Here the wwords {.sy 168}duravagraha m�\\ntyaj�sm�n\" may also be divided {.sy 168}duravagraham �tyaj�sm�n\". Divided\\nin the second way they mean: \"You have completely rejected us\". Then the\\ngop�s give an example of acting properly by accepting one's own devotees\\nand rejecting others. They say: \"Lord Narayana reciprocates with the sages\\nwho yearn after liberation and who have renounced pious deeds, sense\\npleasures, and all else for His sake. The Lord does not reciprocate with\\nothers.\" Then, quoting from scripture, the gop�s speak the next verse to\\nrefute Lord K���a's request. They say (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.32):\\n\\n\\nyat paty-apatya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Our dear K���a, as an expert in religion You have advised us that the\\nproper religious duty for women is to faithfuIly serve their husbands,\\nchildren and other relatives. We agree that this principle is valid, but\\nactually this service should be rendered to You. After all, O Lord, You are\\nthe dearmost friend of all embodied souls. You are their most intimate\\nrelative and indeed their very Self.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"sva-dharma�\" may be interpreted as \"sv-\\nadharma�\" which means \"great impiety\". If this word is interpreted in that\\nway, then the word \"dharma-vid�\" is employed as a joke, to mock the so-\\ncalled knower of religion described here. If this interpretation is\\naccepted, then these words are only a trick and the words {.sy\\n168}faithfully serve their husbands\" come to mean the opposite of what they\\nseem. Interpreted in this way, these words reject the false religious\\nprinciples described here. Here the word {.sy 168}upade�a-pade\" means\\n\"teacher\" and \"��e tvayy ev�stu\" means \"You are the supremely independent\\nLord. These words thus mean: \"O Lord, you reject these false principles of\\nreligion\". Someone may ask: \"O Lord, what is Your nature?\" Here the gop�s\\nanswer with the word {.sy 168}pre��ha�\" (dearest beloved). The gop�s say:\\n\"You are the friend of all living entities. You are the dearest beloved of\\nall living entities.\" This means: \"O Lord, by associating with You, we will\\nall attain great auspiciousness.\" Then, refuting Lord K���a's statement in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.23, the gop�s say (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.33):\\n\\n\\nkurvanti. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Expert transcendentalists always direct their affection toward You\\nbecause they recognize You as their true Self and eternal beloved. What use\\ndo we have for these husbands, children and relatives of ours, who simply\\ngive us trouble? Therefore, O supreme controller, grant us Your mercy. O\\nlotus-eyed one, please do not cut down our long-cherished hope to have Your\\nassociation.\"***\\n\\n     This verse also has a second, a hidden meaning. The hidden meaning,\\nattained by interpeting the syntatical relationships of the words in the\\nsentence in a different way, is: \"O lotus-eyed Lord, please cut intop\\npieces our desire, the desire that says, `We will become happy by enjoying\\nthe pleasures of our meaterial bodies, material bodies that are alwasy so\\ndear to us, pleasures we enjoy with our husbands and children. Why should\\nwe be attracted to K���a as our beloved? We are not attracted to Him.' \"\\nHere the word \"varade�vara (givcer of boons) is in the vocatuve case. It is\\nused here sarcastically. Here the gop�s say: \"Please give this boon to us.\\nGive us the boon that the indifference we feel in our hearts toward You,\\nthe indifference that says, `Let us not always yearn to attain You, O\\nK���a.', will be cut into pieces.\" Here the word {.sy 168}aravidna-netra\"\\n(O lotus-eyed one) implies: \"It is not right that a person with such\\nbeautiful lotus eyes will be so crooked and deceitful to us\". The word\\n\"sma\" here changes the present tense of the verb into the imperfect tense.\\nThe word \"���m\" is the object of the sentence. In the next verse the gop�s\\ncriticize Lord K���a's description of the way love for Him is attained, a\\ndescription He spoke in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.27. The gop�s say (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.29.34):\\n\\n\\ncittam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Until today our minds were absorbed in household affairs, but You\\neasily stole both our minds and our hands away from our housework. Now our\\nfeet won't move one step from Your lotus feet. How can we go back to Vraja?\\nWhat would we do there?\"***\\n\\n     Here the gop�s say: \"As long as You had not stolen them away, our\\nminds and hearts were very happy. They were happy because they stayed at\\nhome (grhesu). The symptom of their happiness was that our hands were\\nengaged in household duties.\" Thinking of K���a's addressing them as \"su-\\nmadhyam��\" (O slender-waisted girls) in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.28.19, the\\ngop�s say in this verse: \"Now our feet won't move one step from Your lotus\\nfeet. How can we go back to Vraja?\" The meaning here is: \"We will not go\\nback to Vraja. We will stay here.\" Then the gop�s say: \"What would we do\\nthere?\" In reply to Lord K���as words \"pratiy�ta g�h�n\" (Please go back to\\nyour homes) in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.27, the gop�s\\nspeka the next verse (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.35):\\n\\n\\nsi�ca. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear K���a, please pour the nectar of Your lips upon the fire within\\nour hearts--a fire You ignited with Your smiling glances and the sweet song\\nof Your flute. If You do not, we will consign our bodies to the fire of\\nseparation from You, O friend, and thus like yog�s attain to the abode of\\nYour lotus feet by meditation.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the gop�s say: \"beloved, please pour the nectar of Your\\nlips upon the fire within our hearts--a fire You ignited with Your smiling\\nglances and the sweet song of Your flute.\" Then the gop�s jokingly say: \"If\\nYou will not give us that nectar, then we will only stand here and lick our\\nown lips with desire to taste that nectar.\" Here the gop�s hint that it is\\nLord K���a's duty to protect them. That the Lord protects His devotees is\\nseen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (11.4.10):\\n\\n\\ndhatte pada� tvam avit� yadi vighna-m�rdhni\\n\\n\\n     \"O Lord, because You are the direct protector of Your devotee, he is\\nable to step over the head of whatever obstacle the demigods place before\\nhim.\"***\\n\\n     Then the gop�s say (in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.35): {.sy 168}If You do\\nnot give us the nectar of Your lips, we will consign our bodies to the fire\\nof separation from You.\" The gop�s address K���a as \"sakhe\" (O friend), in\\nthe vocative case, because in the past they had exchanged playful and\\naffectionate glances with Him. Here Lord K���a may ask: {.sy 168}When We\\nwere children we played and touched. Why have you gop�s now become like\\nstrangers?\" To answer this question the gop�s speak the next verse (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.29.36):\\n\\n\\nyarhi. . .\\n\\n\\n      \"O lotus-eyed one, the goddess of fortune considers it a festive\\noccasion whenever she touches the soles of Your lotus feet. You are very\\ndear to the residents of the forest, and therefore we will also touch those\\nlotus feet. From that time on we will be unable even to stand in the\\npresence of any other man, for we will have been fully satisfied by You.\"*\\n\\n     This verse may also be interpreted to mean: \"O lotus-eyed one, the\\nbirds, beasts, and other beings in the forest all dearly love You as You\\nenjoy Your childhood pastimes. Beasutiful girls think the soles of Your\\nfeet are a great festival of happiness. We yearn to attain tyhe soles of\\nYour feet. Still, somehow we remain unable to touch them. When we played as\\nchildren we enjoyed Your company. But now we must stand before our elders\\nand suepriors. No longer can we easily associate with You.\" In this verse\\nthe word \"bata\" is used in the sense of doubt or worry. These words mean:\\n{.sy 168}We should escape from the circle of our elders and superiors.\"\\nRemembering Lord K���a statement (in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.23) \"pr�yante\\nmayi jantava�\" (all living entities possess natural affection for Me), a\\nstamenet that hints, \"beautiful girls naturally love Me as their beloved\",\\nthe gop�s cite Goddess Lak�m� as an example in the next verse (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.29.37):\\n\\n\\n�r��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Goddess Lak�m�, whose glance is sought after by the demigods with\\ngreat endeavor, has achieved the unique position of always remaining on the\\nchest of her Lord, Naraya�a. Still, she desires the dust of His lotus feet,\\neven though she has to share that dust with Tulas�-dev� and indeed with the\\nLord's many other servants. Similarly, we have approached the dust of Your\\nlotus feet for shelter.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the gop�s say: \"After attaining a place at Lord Vi��u's\\nchest, Goddess Lak�m� still yearns to attain to dust of Your feet in\\nGokula's ofrest, dust Tulas�-dev� also yearns to attain. From the moment of\\nYour birth there, Nanda's land of Vraja has been a place of pastimes for\\nGoddess Lak�m�. To attain the dust of your feet Goddess Tulas� always\\nresides in V�nd�vana forest.\" Someone may ask: \"Why do Lak�m� and Tulas�\\nyearn to attain the dust of Lord K���a's feet?\" The answer is given here in\\nthe words \"bh�tya-ju��am\" (served by His servants). It is natural for a\\nmaidservant to place her master's feet on her head. Someone may ask: {.sy\\n168}What are the glories of Goddess Lak�m�?\" The answer is given here in\\nthe words \"yasy�� sva-v�k�a�a ut�nya-sura-pray�sa�\", which mean: \"the\\ngloruy of Goddess Lak�m� is such that the other divine beings who are her\\nassociates yearn to attain her glance.\" Their voices choked with emotion,\\nthe gop�s then say the words \"vaya� ca\", which menas \"we are like them\".\\nTherse words mean: \"As Lak�m� and Tulas� are enchanted by You, we are also\\nenchanted by You. That is why we have now approached the dust of Your feet.\\nIt is not otherwise.\" Then the gop�s speak the next verse (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.29.38):\\n\\n\\ntan na�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Therefore, O vanquisher of all distress, please show us mercy. To\\napproach Your lotus feet we abandoned our families and homes, and we have\\nno desire other than to serve You. Our hearts are burning with intense\\ndesires generated by Your beautiful smiling glances. O jewel among men,\\nplease make us Your maidservants.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"vrjinardana\" mreans {.sy 168}O Lord who\\nremoves all distress\", and \"nah prasida\" means \"please be merciful to us\\nand remove the obstacles that keep us from seeing You\". Here Lord K���a may\\nask: {.sy 168}Did you gop�s really come here only to touch My feet?\" To\\nthis question the gop�s reply with the words: \"We left our homes not only\\nto touch Your feet, but because we yearn to worship You. We did not come\\nhere to see V�nd�vana forest in the moonlight.\" Then the gop�s also say:\\n\"Our hearts are burning with intense desires generated by Your beautiful\\nsmiling glances. Please make us Your maidservants.\" Because the idea of a\\ngift here is not very explicit, the word \"�tman�m\" is in the genitive\\ncase, not the dative case. From this is may be understood that this gift of\\ndevotional service is not always given, even in Gokula. The word \"puru�a-\\nbh��a�a\" (O ornament among men\", which is in the vocative case, has a\\ndouble meaning. It may also mean: \"O Lord who have become the ornament of\\nYour boyfriends in Gokula\". This interpretation implies: \"Therefore You are\\nnever the ornament of Gokula's beautiful girls.\" Their hearts burning, the\\ngop�s think all these thoughts. In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.21 Lord K���a\\nignored the udd�panas that attracted the gs to Him. Then, in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.29.27, Lord K���a revealed the great power of His own\\nattractive handsomeness. Thinking of these statements of Lord K���a, the\\ngop�s spoke the next verse (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.39):\\n\\n\\nv�k�ya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Seeing Your face encircled by curling locks of hair, Your cheeks\\nbeautified by earrings, Your lips full of nectar, and Your smiling glance,\\nand also seeing Your two imposing arms, which take away our fear, and Your\\nchest, which is the only source of pleasure for the goddess of fortune, we\\nmust become Your maidservants.\"***\\n\\n     The word \"ca\" (and) spoken near the end of this verse by voices choked\\nwith emotion, refers to the words that precede it. Here the gop�s say:\\n\"Seeing You, we must become Your maidservants. It is not otherwise.\" Here\\nLord K���a may ask: \"If You are so dedicated to Me, why do you not stay\\nhere every night?\" Thinking Lord K���a may ask this question, the gop�s\\nspeak the next verse (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.40):\\n\\n\\nk� stry a�ga te. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear K���a, what woman in all the three worlds wouldn't deviate from\\nreligious behavior when bewildered by the sweet, drawn-out melody of Your\\nflute? Your beauty makes all three worlds auspicious. Indeed, even the\\ncows, birds, trees and deer manifest the ecstatic symptom of bodily hair\\nstanding on end when they see Your beautiful form.\"***\\n\\n     This verse means: \"O beloved (a�ga) who sweetly plays on the flute\\n(kala-pad�yata-ve�u-g�ta) and who is enchanting (sammohita) with the arrows\\nshot by K�madeva, what woman in all the three worlds wouldn't deviate from\\nreligious behavior for Your sake? For Your sake we throw far away all vows\\nof pious morality.\" If someone were to try to find fault in the qualities\\nof the Lord described in the two vocative words (kala-pad�yata-ve�u-g�ta\\nand sammohita) mentioued above, the gop�s reply by speaking the word\\n\"trailokya-saubhagam\" (who makes all three worlds auspicious). The reason\\nfor the gop�s deviating from religious behavior is given here in the words\\n\"�rya-carit�n nir�k�ya r�pa� k� na calet\" (upon seeing Your handsome form,\\nwhat woman would not deviate from religious behavior?). The gop�s then say:\\n\"Therefore (yat) even the cows, birds, trees and deer manifest the ecstatic\\nsymptom of bodily hair standing on end when they see Your beautiful form.\"\\nBecause they may thus easily deviate from the principles of religious\\nbehavior, beautiful girls and handsome are forbidden to associate in this\\nway in this world. Thinking of the words Lord K���a spoke in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.29.19, and thinking that in the context of those words Lord\\nK���a might say: {.sy 168}I am a powerful hero. Stay with Me I I will\\nprotect You from all dangers\", the gop�s offer a prayer in the next verse\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.41):\\n\\n\\nvyakta� bhav�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Clearly You have taken birth in this world to relieve the fear and\\ndistress of the people of Vraja, just as the Supreme Personality of\\nGodhead, the primeval Lord, protects the domain of the demigods. Therefore,\\nO friend of the distressed, kindly place Your lotus hand on Your\\nmaidservants' heads and burning breasts.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"arta-bandho\" means {.sy 168}O You who have\\ntaken birth in this world to protect the people of Vraja from the fearful\\ncondition of falling away from the principles of religion\". Here the words\\n{.sy 168}ki�kar���� tapta-stane�u kara-pa�kaja� ca �ira�su no nidhehi\" have\\na hidden possible second interpretation, which gives this meaning of the\\nwords: \"Please doi not place Your lotus hand on Your maidservants' heads\\nand breasts, breasts burning with desire from the sight of You\". These\\nwords thus imply: \"Evenb in Your thoughts do not do such a thing to saintly\\nchaste girls like ourselves.\" This hidden meaning is hinted in this verse.\\nQuoting the dharma-��stras, Lord K���a had previously proved that ther\\ngop�s should always faithfully serve their husbands and relatives. Quoting\\nthe j��na-��stras, the gop�s now brilliantly refute K���a's arguments. The\\ngop�s here prove that K���a is the Supreme Personality of Godhead\\n(param�tm�), and therefore He is the meaning and the goal of all the\\nscriptures' teachings. In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.32 the gop�s hint: \"O\\nK���a, You are the meaning and goal of all the scriptures' teachings.\\nDevotional service to You is the final goal taught in the scriptures.\" Here\\nLord K���a may protest: {.sy 168}How is it possible that I am the goal of\\nall the scriptures?\" To this the gop�s reply (in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.29.32) by speaking the word \"�tm�\", which means: \"You are the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead.\" The gop�s hint here: {.sy 168}You are the goal of\\nall the scriptures, the scriptures that affirm (B�had-�ra�yaka Upani�ad\\n4.4.22):\\n\\n\\ntam eta� ved�nuvacanena br�hma�� vividi�anti\\n\\n\\n     \"By studying the Vedas the br�hma�as understand the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead.\"\\n\\n     Then K���a may again protest: \"How is it possible that I am the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead?\" To this protest the gop�s reply with the\\nwords \"tanu-bh�t�� pre��ha�\", which mean \"for the living entities You are\\nthe object of love without limit\". Here the gop�s also say: \"bandhur\\nbhav�n\", which means \"You bring limitless auspiciousness to the living\\nentities\". In these two ways Lord K���a possesses the symptoms of being the\\nSupreme Personality of Godhead, symptoms described in these words of the\\nj��na-��stras (B�had-�ra�yaka Upani�ad 2.4.5):\\n\\n\\n�tmanas tu k�m�ya sarva� priya� bhavati\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead is loved by everyone. He fulfills\\neveryone's desire.\"\\n\\n     Here the gop�s may say: \"In this way we have proved that You are\\nindeed the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Because You are the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead we are very eager to worship and serve You.\" It is\\nsaid in Mu��aka Upani�ad (1.2.12):\\n\\n\\nbr�hma�o nirvedam �y�t, n�sty ak�ta� k�tena\\n\\n\\n     \"N