      "sourceStartLine": 22086,
      "content": "\\n\\n     An example of the devotees who directly enjoy amorous pastimes with\\nLord K���a is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.11-12):\\n\\n\\napy e�a-patny upagata� priyayeha g�trais\\n     tanvan d���� sakhi su-nirv�tim acyuto va�\\nk�nt��ga-sa�ga-kuca-ku�kuma-ra�jit�y��\\n     kunda-sraja� kula-pater iha v�ti gandha�\\n\\n\\n     \"O friend, wife of the deer, has Lord Acyuta been here with His\\nbeloved, bringing great joy to your eyes? Indeed, blowing this way is the\\nfragrance of His garland of kun�a flowers, which was smeared with the\\nku�kuma from the breasts of His girlfriend when He embraced Her.***\\n\\n\\nb�hu� priy��sa upadh�ya g�h�ta-padmo\\n     r�m�nujas tulasikali-kulair madandhai�\\nanv�yam�na iha vas tarava� pra��ma�\\n     ki� v�bhinandati caran pra�ay�valokai�\\n\\n\\n       \"O trees, we see that you are bowing down. When the younger brother\\nof R�ma walked by here, followed by intoxicated bees swarming around the\\ntulas� ma�jar�s decorating His garland, did He acknowledge your obeisances\\nwith His affectionate glances? He must have been resting His arm on the\\nshoulder of His beloved and carrying a lotus flower in His free hand.\"***\\n\\n     Here the word \"ena-patni\" means \"O beautiful-eyed wife of the deer\".\\nHere the gop�s imagine that the doe is like a human being, like the gop�s\\nthemselves, and thus is the wife of a deer. The word \"sakhi\" means {.sy\\n168}you have attained my friendship because of your good fortune, which I\\nwill soon describe\". \"Priyay� acyuta�\" means {.sy 168}�r� K���a accompanied\\nby His beloved\". \"G�trai�\" means \"Their limbs entwined in an embrace\". {.sy\\n168}Va�\" means \"ofe persons like tyourselves\", {.sy 168}d���m\" means \"of\\nthe eyes\", \"sunirv�tim\" means \"the spiritual bliss born from seeing Lord\\nK���a\", \"tanvan\" means \"granting the best of all\", and \"upagata�\" means\\n\"did He come near you?\" Here the wives of the deer may ask: \"How can you\\nknow if Lord K���a was here?\" Fearing the does might speak those words, the\\ngop�s speak the second half (beginning with the word k�nta) of this verse,\\nwhere, speaking words that indirectly praise the divine couple's amorous\\npastimes, they describe the symptoms of Lord K���a's presence. Here the\\nword \"kula-pate�\" means \"the tilaka mark decorating the family of Vraja's\\nking\", \"kunda-sraja� gandha�\" means \"the fragrance of the jasmine garland\",\\nand \"iha v�ti\" means \"is blown here by the breeze\". What is the garland\\nlike? It is described here in the words \"k�nt��ga-sa�ga-kuca-ku�kuma-\\nra�jit�y��\", which mean \"reddened with ku�kuma from the limbs of His\\npassionate and all-virtuous beloved\". The gop�s say: \"We think that you,\\nwho perceive everything here, might have smelled this fragrance as you\\nwalked near Them.\"\\n     Fearing this doe is concealing the joy she feels on seeing the divine\\ncouple and the grief she feels by separation from Them, and thinking the\\ndoe must certainly have seen the divine couple, the gop�s joyfully describe\\na specific pastime the divine couple might have enjoyed at that time. Then,\\nseeing the bent-down trees, and thinking them servants now bowing down\\nbefore them, the gop�s ask the trees if they had the good fortune of seeing\\nthe divine couple. In this way the gop�s spoke �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.12.\\nIn that verse the word \"anviyamanah\" means \"being followed\", \"caran\\npra�ay�valokai�\" means {.sy 168}enjoying pastimes of mutual loving\\nglances\", \"va� pra��ma� ki� v�bhinandati\" means \"did He respectfully\\nackowledge your obeisances?\" Here the gop�s mean to say: {.sy 168}Rapt in\\nenjoying pastim,es with His beloved, did Lord K���a greet you? We do not\\nthink He did.\" These verses were spoken by �r� R�dh�'s girlfriends.\\n\\n"
