     n�pam �ruhya satvara�\\nhasadbhi� prahasan b�lai�\\n     parih�sam uv�ca ha\\n\\n\\n     \"Taking the girls' garments, He (K���a) quickly climbed to the top of\\nthe kadamba tree. Then, as He laughed loudly and His companions also\\nlaughed, He addressed the girts jokingly.\"***\\n\\n     Here it may be said that although the gop�s already had very exalted\\nlove for Lord K���a, Lord K���a still manifested this joking pastime to\\nopenly display that love and to enjoy a certain kind of loving pastime with\\nthem. Scholars learned in rasa-��stra yearn to relish this display of the\\ngop�s' love. In this pastime Lord K���a does not touch the gop�s or enjoy\\nother kinds of sambhoga pastimes with them. This kind of p�rva-rasa is\\ncalled \"lajj�-ccheda\" (breaking the shyness). It is one of the different\\nkinds of sambhoga. These different kinds of sambhoga are described in these\\nwords of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, ���g�ra-bheda-prakara�a, Text 66:\\n\\n\\nnayana-pr�ti� prathama�\\n     cint�-sa�gas tato 'tha sa�kalpa�\\nnidr�-cchedas tanut�\\n     vi�aya-niv�ttis trap�-n��a�\\nunm�do m�rch� m�tir\\n     ity et�� smara-da�� da�aiva syu�\\n\\n\\n     \"Some other authorities list the stage of sambhoga in this way: 1.\\nnayana-pr�ti (falling in love by seeing the beloved), 2. cint� (thinking of\\nthe beloved), 3. sa�kalpa (deciding to pursue the beloved), 4. nidr�-ccheda\\n(loss of sleep), 5. tanut� (becoming emaciated), vi�aya-niv�tti (becoming\\noblivious to everything except the beloved), 7. trap�-n��a (destroying\\nshyness), 8. unm�da (becoming wild), 9. m�rch� (fainting), and 10. m�ti\\n(death).\"\\n\\n     This condition of breaking shyness is the most exalted state in\\nanur�ga. Even when they accept death, the last of these ten states, the\\ngop�s do not abandon their shyness. To relish this kind of anur�ga\\n(breaking the gop�s' shyness), Lord K���a arranged these joking pastimes.\\nIn these pastimes Lord K���a said to the gop�s (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.22.11):\\n\\n\\nna mayodita-p�rva� v�\\n     anrtam tad ime vidu�\\n\\n\\n     \"I have never before spoken a lie, and these boys know it. Therefore,\\nO slender-waisted girls, please come forward, either one by one or all\\ntogether, and pick out your clothes.\"***\\n\\n     That the gop�s' love for Lord K���a is never broken and remained\\nunchanged in every situation (even when Lord K���a laughed at them) is seen\\nin these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (01.229):\\n\\n\\nhasadbhi�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a laughed loudly and His companions also laughed,\"\\n\\n     Here the word \"b�la\" indicated Lord K���a's friends. Only persons who\\nare Lord K���a's friends may touch His body. Other's cannot. Lord K���a\\nfriends are described in these words of Gautam�ya Tantra, Pratham�vara�a-\\np�j�:\\n\\n\\nd�ma-sud�ma-vasud�ma-\\n     ki�ki��r gandha-pu�pakai�\\nanta�kara�a-r�p�s te\\n     k���asya parik�rtit��\\n�tm�bhedena te p�jy�\\n     yath� k���as tathaiva te\\n\\n\\n     \"With fragrant flowers D�m�, Sud�m�, Vasud�m�, and Ki�ki�� worshiped\\nLord K���a. These boys were all praised by Lord K���a Himself. These boys\\nall know Lord K���a's heart. Their hearts are not different from Lord\\nK���a's heart. As Lord K���a is worthy of worship, so are they.\"\\n\\n     Because these pastimes of Lord K���a's relishing the gop�s' anur�ga\\nlove for Him are confidential, Lord K���a pretended these activities were\\nall a joke. This joking pretense is not incompatible with these pastimes.\\nOn the contrary, Lord K���a joking enhances the bliss of the pastimes. The\\ngreat sage Sukadeva Gosvami describes Lord K���a's purpose in performing\\nthese activities. The sage explains (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.22.18):\\n\\n\\nbhagav�n �hat� v�k�ya\\n     �uddha-bh�va-pras�dita�\\nskandhe nidh�ya v�s��si\\n     pr�ta� prov�ca sa-smitam\\n\\n\\n     \"When the Supreme Lord saw how the gop�s were struck with\\nembarrassment, He was satisfied by their pure loving affection. Putting\\ntheir clothes on His shoulder, the Lord smiled and spoke to them with\\naffection.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"�hat��\" means {.sy 168}approached\". Or,\\n\"�ha�a�\" may also mean {.sy 168}Although they had renounced almost all\\nshyness, because of their feminine nature a small fragment of shyness\\nremained. Because of this remaining shyness they gently bent their bodies.\"\\nThe word \"�uddha-bh�va-pras�dita�\" means \"pleased at heart by relishing the\\ngop�s' splendid love, love that showed their longing to be with Him and\\ntheir being enchanted by Him\".\\n     The next verse explains (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.22.19):\\n\\n\\ny�ya� vivastr� yad apo dh�ta-vrat��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"(Lord K���a said:) You girls bathed naked while executing your vow,\\nand that is certainly an offense against the demigods. To counteract Your\\nsin you should offer obeisances while placing your joined palms above your\\nheads. Then you should take back your lower garments.\"***\\n\\n     In order to see the last remnants of the gop�s' shyness come to their\\nfinal end Lord K���a spoke these joking words. The next verse explains\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.22.20):\\n\\n\\nity acyutena. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Thus the young girls of V�nd�vana, considering what Lord Acyuta had\\ntold them, accepted that they had suffered a falldown from their vow by\\nbathing naked in the river. But they still desired to successfully complete\\ntheir vow, and since Lord K���a is Himself the ultimate result of all pious\\nactivities, they offered their obeisances to Him to cleanse away all their\\nsins.\"***\\n\\n     In this way �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�, the king of sages, reveals first\\nthe gop�s'being enchanted by Lord K���a and their longing to associatte\\nwith Him, and then their obedience to His command. In the next verse �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m� explains (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.22.22):\\n\\n\\nd��ha� pralabdh�s trapay� ca h�pit��\\n     prastobhit�� kr��ana-vac ca k�ritah\\nvastr��i caiv�pah�t�ny ath�py amum-\\n     t� n�bhyas�yan priya-sa�ga-nirvrt�h\\n\\n\\n     \"Although the gop�s had been thoroughly cheated, deprived of their\\nmodesty, ridiculed and made to act just like toy dolls, ana although their\\nclothing had been stolen, they did not feel at all inimical toward Sr�\\nK���a. Hather, they were simply joyful to have this opportunity to\\nassociate with their beloved.\"***\\n\\n     In the verse the word \"d��ham\" means {.sy 168}greatly\", and\\n\"pralabdh��\" means \"cheated\". The cheating of the gop�s is described here\\nin �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.22.19. \"Trapay� h�pit��\" means \"deprived of their\\nmodesty\". This is descibed in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.22.10. \"Prastobhit��'\\nhere means \"mocked\". Lord K���a mocked the gop�s in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.22.10. Lord K���a's making the gop�s act like toy dolls is desscribed in\\nHis making them stand with folded palms on the pretext of performing\\natonement. Even though Lord K���a cheated them and teased them in many\\nways, the gop�s did not find fault with Lord K���a. On the contrary. It is\\nsaid here that even though He had stolen their garments, the gop�s did not\\nfeel eneimical toward Lord K���a. On the contrary. They were plunged into\\nbliss because they had an opportunity to associate with their beloved.\\nThese verses were spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 379-382\\n\\n\\n     Because they were br�hma��s, the yaj�a-patn�s were not qualified to\\nassociate with with Lord K���a as the gop�s did. Still, the yaj�a-patn�s\\ndid have an ecstatic sentiment that was something like p�rva-r�ga. Gazing\\nat Lord K���a and conversing with Him, they experienced something in some\\nways like sambhoga. They experienced sambhog�bh�sa (the reflection of\\nsambhoga). After the winter season had ended and summer had come, Lord\\nK���a's pastimes with the yajna-patn�s became visible. The beginning of\\nthese pastimes is desacribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.22.29):\\n\\natha gopai� pariv�to\\n     bhagav�n devak�-suta�\\nvrnd�van�d gato d�ra�\\n     c�rayan g�� sah�grajah\\n\\n\\n     \"Some time later Lord K���a, the son of Devak�, surrounded by His\\ncowherd friends and accompanied by His elder brother, Balar�ma, went a good\\ndistance away from V�nd�vana, herding the cows.\"***\\n\\n     This verse means: \"After Lord K���a had given His mercy to the\\nunmarried girls of Vraja, a summer day came\". That the day was a summer day\\ndistinguishes this pastime from the previous pastime (which occurred in the\\nwinter). On this day Lord K���a was accompanied by Lord Balar�ma. That is\\nindicated here by the word \"s�graja�\" (with His elder brother). Here the\\nphrase \"a good distance away from V�nd�vana\" indicates that K���a and the\\nboys were in the hilly part of K�myavana. That they were in the hills is\\nseen by the fact that they decorated their bodies with mineral pigments\\nfrom the hills. That place is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.22.36):\\n\\n\\ntar���� namra-��kh�n��\\n     madhyato yamun�� gatah\\n\\n\\n     \"Thus moving among the trees, whose branches were bent low by their\\nabundance of twigs, fruits, flowers and leaves, Lord K���a came to the\\nYamun� River.\"***\\n\\n     With Vraja to their south and the Yamun� to the west, they entered a\\nclearing to enjoy pastimes. To the south was Mathur� City, and to the north\\nwas the place where some y�j�ika-br�hma�as lived. The home of these\\nbrahmana's was near King Ka�sa's palace. That is seen in these words\\ndescribing the thoughts of these br�hma�as (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.23.52):\\n\\n\\nka�s�d bh�t� na c�calan\\n\\n\\n     \"Thus reflecting on the sin they had committed by neglecting Lord\\nK���a, they (the y�j�ika-br�hma�as) became very eager to see Him. But being\\nafraid of King Ka�sa, they did not dare go to Vraja.\"***\\n\\n     In this way it is seen that those br�hma�as did not go to meet Lord\\nK���a. The summer qualities of that day are described in t