      "content": "\\n\\n     In the autumn after (Lord �r� K���a's pastime of stealing the gop�s'\\ngarments) all the vraja-gop�s enjoyed sambhoga pastime with Lord K���a,\\npastimes born of the gop�s' purva-raga love for Him, pastimes that begin\\nwith gazing at Lord K���a. These sambhoga pastimes are described (in the\\nscriptures). Although the unmarried girls of Vraja had already attained\\nLord K���a's association, theydid not feel fulfilled. Their purva-raga love\\nfor Him had still not attained its full nature. Still, at least one gop�s\\nhad enjoyed pastimes with Lord K���a, as is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.21.17):\\n\\n\\np�r��� pulindya�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The aborigine women of the V�nd�vana area become disturbed by lust\\nwhen they see the grass marked with reddish ku�kuma\\npowder. Endowed with the color of K���a's lotus feet, this powder\\noriginally decorated the breasts of His beloveds, and when the aborigine\\nwomen smear it on their faces and breasts, they give up all their\\nanxiety.\"***\\n\\n     Still, many other gop�s had not yet even touched Lord K���a. This is\\nseen in these words spoken by the gop�s in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.36):\\n\\n\\nyarhy ambuj�k�a. . .\\n\\n\\n      \"O lotus-eyed one, the goddess of fortune considers it a festive\\noccasion whenever she touches the soles of Your lotus feet. You are very\\ndear to the residents of the forest, and therefore we will also touch those\\nlotus feet. From that time on we will be unable even to stand in the\\npresence of any other man, for we will have been fully satisfied by\\nYou.\"***\\n\\n     When the gop�s first heard Lord K���a's flute-music they at first\\ntried to supress the ecstatic emotions that came to overwhelm them. From\\nthis it may be seen that they had not yet directly enjoyed sambhoga\\npastimes with Him. Also, because they had not yet enjoyed sambhoga pastimes\\nwith Him, Lord K���a at first rejected them, and they then appealed to Him.\\nThese pastimes are seen in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam, Canto 10, Chapter 29, in the\\npassage that begins with these words (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.29.4):\\n\\n\\nni�amya g�ta� tad ana�ga-vardhana�\\n     vraja-striya� k���a-g�h�ta-m�nas��\\n�jagmur anyonyam alak�itodyam��\\n     sa yatra k�nto java-lola-kundal��\\n\\n\\n     \"When the young women of V�nd�vana heard K���a's flute song, which\\narouses romantic feelings, their minds were captivated by the Lord. They\\nwent to where their lover waited, each unknown to the others, moving so\\nquickly that their earrings swung back and forth.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is clear. This verse was spoken by �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
