      "content": "\\n\\n     Jealous anger (m�na) may bring separation (vipralambha) of Lord K���a\\nand His beloved. This is seen in the following words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i,\\n���g�ra-bheda-prakara�a (Text 93):\\n\\n\\naher iva gati� prem��\\n     sva-bh�va-ku�il� bhavet\\nato hetor aheto� ca\\n     y�nor m�na uda�cati\\n\\n\\n     \"The progress of loving affairs between young couples is by nature\\ncrooked, like the movement of a snake. Becuase of this, two types of nager\\narise between young couples-anger with cause and anger without cause.\"*\\n\\n     Jealous anger without a cause is described in these words of Ujjvala-\\nn�lama�i, ���g�ra-bheda-prakara�a (Text 70):\\n\\n\\nahetur neti nety ukter\\n     hetor yan m�na ucyate\\nasya pra�aya eva sy�n\\n     m�nasya padam uttamam\\n\\n\\n     \"When without any cause the beloved says `No. No.' and rejects the\\nlover, this is called jealous anger (m�na). This is the highest state of\\nloving jealous anger.\"\\n\\n     Jealous anger is thus with a cause and without a ccause. Jealous anger\\nwith a cause is described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, ���g�ra-bheda-\\nprakara�a (Text 71):\\n\\n\\nhetur �r�y�-vipak��der\\n     vi�i��ye preyas� k�te\\nbh�va� pra�aya-mukhyo 'yam\\n     �r�y�-m�natvam �cchati\\n\\n\\n     \"When the lover gives special attention to a certain beloved, then all\\nthe rivals of the beloved become jealous. In this way what was once love\\nbecomes jealous anger. This is anger with cause.\"\\n\\n     Jealous anger is also described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i,\\n���g�ra-bheda-prakara�a (Text 72):\\n\\n\\nsneha� vin� bhaya� na sy�n\\n     ner�y� ca pra�aya� vin�\\ntasm�n m�na-prak�ro 'ya�\\n     dvayo� prema-prak��aka�\\n\\n\\n     \"Without love there cannot be fear (for the loss of the beloved), and\\nwithout love there cannot be jealousy. For this reason fear and jealousy\\nmay be manifestations of love.\"\\n\\n     Jealous anger is seen in the following words of Hari-va��a, Vi��u-\\nparva (66.4 and 65.50):\\n\\n\\nru�it�m iva t�� dev��\\n     sneh�t sa�kalpayann iva\\nbh�ta-bh�to 'ti-�anakair\\n     vive�a yadu-nandana�\\n\\nr�pa-yauvana-sampann�\\n     sva-saubh�gyena garvit�\\nabhim�navat� dev�\\n     �rutvaiver�y�-va�a� gat�\\n\\n\\n     \"As He slowly entered the palace, Lord K���a, who is feared by fear\\nitself, and who is the descendent of Maharaja Yadu, lovingly meditated on\\nHis angry queen Satyabh�m�.\"\\n     \"Meanwhile, hearing (how her rival Rukmi�� had attained a p�rij�ta\\nflower from Indraloka), Queen Satyabh�m�, who was very proud of her opulent\\nbeauty and youthfulness, bexcame overwhelmed with jealous anger.\"\\n\\n     When the beloved suspects the lover no longer loves her, then jealous\\nanger with cause is manifest. Lord K���a enjoys teasing His beloved in this\\nway (to make her angry because she suspects He no longer loves her). An\\nexample of such teasing is seen in these words Lord K���a spoke to Queen\\nRukmi�� (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.60.29):\\n\\n\\ntvad-vaca� �rotu-k�mena\\n     k�vely�caritam a�gane\\n\\n\\n     \"I only spoke in jest, dear lady, because I wanted to hear what you\\nwould say.\"***\\n\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.30):\\n\\n\\nmukha� ca prema-samrambha-\\n     sphurit�dharam �k�itum. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"I wanted to see your face with lips trembling in loving anger, the\\nreddish corners of your eyes throwing sidelong glances and the line of your\\nbeautiful eyebrows knit in a frown.\"***\\n\\n     Queen Rukmi�� refuted Lord K���a's teasing arguments by speaking these\\nwords (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.60.40):\\n\\n\\nj��ya� vacas tava gad�graja. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My Lord, as a lion drives away lesser animals to claim his proper\\ntribute, You drove off the assembled kings with the resounding twang of\\nYour ��r�ga bow and then claimed me, Your fair share. Thus it is sheer\\nfoolishness, my dear Gad�graja, for You to say You took shelter in the\\nocean out of fear of those kings.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a's teasing words in the pastime were proper, for they were\\nmeant only to increase His beloved's love for Him. Such words are approved\\nby ancient and modern poets and philosophers. Now jealous anger in the mood\\nof worship, which is called {.sy 168}�dara��ya-m�na\", will be considered.\\nWhen, during the r�sa dance, at the time of their first intimate meeting,\\nLord K���a abandoned all the gop�s and remained hidden from their sight,\\nthe gop�s became slightly angry with Him. This is seen in the passage\\nbeginning with these words (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.32.15):\\n\\n\\nsabh�jayitv� tam ana�ga-d�pana�\\n     sah�sa-l�lek�a�a-vibhrama-bhruv�\\nsa�spar�anen��ka-k�t��ghri-hastayo�\\n     sa�stutya i�at kupita babhasire\\n\\n\\n     \"Sr� K���a had awakened romantic desires within the gop�s, and they\\nhonored Him by glancing at Him with playful smiles, gesturing amorously\\nwith their eyebrows, and massaging His hands and feet as they held them in\\ntheir laps. Even while worshiping Him, however, they felt somewhat angry,\\nand thus they addressed Him as follows.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is clear. This verse was spoken by �r�la\\n�ukadwva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
