      "content": "\\n\\n     Jealous anger or pride without a cause is called {.sy 168}pra�aya-\\nm�na\" (anger or pride caused by love). Such anger or pride has no external\\ncause. Its only cause is the pastimes of love. The hero (Lord K���a) also\\nmanifests this kind of pride or anger. In the rasa of love for the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead this kind of anger or pride is one of the udd�panas.\\nAn example of causeless anger or pride, pride born of love, is seen in the\\npassage that begins with these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.29.48):\\n\\n\\nt�s�� tat-saubhaga-mada�\\n     v�k�ya m�na� ca ke�ava�\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord Ke�ava, seeing the gop�s too proud of their good fortune, wanted\\nto relieve them of this pride and show them further mercy. Thus He\\nimmediately disappeared.\"***\\n\\n     Here the gop�s had pride born from love (pranaya-mana). There was a\\ncause of their pride. They were proud of their own good fortune. Therefore,\\nto curb this pride and give them His mercy, Lord K���a suddenly disappeared\\nfrom the gop�s' sight. Then it is said in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.36):\\n\\n\\ny�� gop�m anayat k���o\\n     vih�y�ny�� striyo vane. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The particular gop� whom K���a had led to a secluded forest when He\\nhad abandoned all the other girls began to think Herself the best of women.\\n`My beloved has rejected all the other gop�s', She thought, `even though\\nthey are driven by Cupid himself. He has chosen to reciprocate with Me\\nalone.' \"***\\n\\n     Tbis gop� also became proud with love (pra�aya-m�na). Filled with\\npride, She spoke these words to Lord K���a (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.37):\\n\\n\\nna p�raye 'ham calitu�\\n     naya m�� yatra te mana�\\n\\n\\n     \"I cannot walk any further. Please carry Me wherever You want to\\ngo.\"***\\n\\n     As the other gop�s were previously, this gop� also became proud with\\nlove. Then, with anger also born of love, Lord K���a spoke to Her the\\nfollowing words (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.30.38):\\n\\n\\nskandha �ruhyat�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a replied: `Just climb on My shoulder.' \"***\\n\\n     After speaking these words, Lord K���a suddenly disappeared. The gop�s\\nhave pride without cause (ahetu) and Lord K���a has pride with a faint\\nreflection of a cause (hetv-�bh�sa). Flowing through the great whirlpool of\\ndeceitful crookedness, the gop�s' loving pride and anger become very\\nintense.When they are separated from Lord K���a, their anger becomes\\nsublimely pure. Lord K���a's other beloveds (other than the gop�s) manifest\\ngrief, fear, and worry when they become angry with a cause. As example of\\nthis is seen in the �r�mad-Bh�gavatam chapter when Lord K���a teased Queen\\nRukmi�� with loving joking words. There Lord K���a's intention was to make\\na playful joke. Queen Rukmi�� was very deep and her love very sincere.\\nThere Lord K���a thought: \"My wish is to enjoy pastimes of making My\\nbeloved angry. Her throat will be filled with emotional words that reveal\\nher great love for Me. However, she may not clearly understand My\\nintention. She will either become playfully angry, or she will say she does\\nnot understand My words. In this way I will try to joke with her. Even when\\nI made her brother very ugly-looking she still did not become angry. It is\\nnot appropriate to continue in that direction. However, the happiness she\\nfeels by associating with Me is the be-all-and-end-all of her life. It is\\neverything for her.  Perhaps by mocking the pride she feels by being able\\nto associate with Me I will make her angry. If she does not become angry in\\nthat way, then perhaps by making her afraid that she will be separated from\\nMe she will speak many words revealing her love for Me.\" Thinking in this\\nway, Lord K���a spoke the loving joking words that begin in this verse of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.10):\\n\\n\\nr�ja-putr�psit� bh�pai�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My dwear princess, you were sought after by many kings as powerful as\\nthe rulers of planets. They were all abundantly endowed with polituical\\ninfluence, wealth, beauty, generosity, and physical strength.\"***\\n\\n     That Lord K���a was joking is also seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.60.9):\\n\\n\\nt�� r�pi��m. . .pr�ta� smayan\\n\\n\\n     \"As He contemplated her, the goddess of fortune herself, who desires\\nonly Him, Lord K���a smiled.\"***\\n\\n     In this way it is seen that Lord K���a was only joking. The sincerity\\nof Queen Rukmi��'s love is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.60.25):\\n\\n\\ntad d���v� bhagav�n k���a�\\n     priy�y�� prema-bandhanam\\nh�sya-prau�him aj�nanty��\\n     karu�a� so 'nvakampata. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Seeing that His beloved was so bound to Him in love that she could\\nnot understand the full meaning of His teasing, merciful Lord K���a felt\\ncompassion for her.***\\n     \"The Lord quickly got down from the bed. Manifesting four arms, He\\npicked her up, gathered her hair, and caressed her face with His lotus\\nhand.\"***\\n\\n     In these verses the word \"h�sya-prau�him\" means \"the meaning of His\\njoking words\". Here Lord K���a proudly thought: \"Even though she is very\\nwise and profound, and even though her love is very sincere, I will agitate\\nher.\" The word \"aj�nanty��\" means \"because there was no crookedness or\\ndeceit in her sweet and sincere love, she did not understand the meaning of\\nLord K���a's words\". This is described in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.60.28):\\n\\n\\nh�sya-prau�hi-bhramac-citt�m\\n\\n\\n     \"Rukmi��'s mind was bewildered by Lord K���a's clever joking.\"\\n\\n     As a joke Lord K���a had wished to see her playful anger. Thus He said\\nto her in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.29-31):\\n\\n\\nm� m� vaidarbhy as�yetha\\n     j�ne tv�� mat-par�ya��m\\ntvad-vaca� �rotu-k�mena\\n     k�vely�caritam a�gane\\n\\n\\n     \"O Vaidarbh�, do not be displeased with Me. I know that you are fully\\ndevoted to Me. I only spoke in jest, dear lady, because I wanted to hear\\nwhat you would say.***\\n\\n\\nmukha� ca prema-samrambha-\\n     sphurit�dharam �k�itum\\nka��-k�ep�ru��p��ga�\\n     sundara-bhr�-ku��-ta�am\\n\\n\\n     \"I also wanted to see your face with lips trembling in loving anger,\\nthe reddish corners of your eyes throwing sidelong glances, and the line of\\nyour beautiful eyebrows knit in a frown.***\\n\\n\\naya� hi paramo l�bho\\n     g�he�u g�ha-medhin�m\\nyan narmair iyate y�ma�\\n     priyay� bh�ru bh�mini\\n\\n\\n     \"The greatest pleasure worldly householders can enjoy at home is to\\nspend time joking with their beloved wives, My dear timid and tempermental\\none.\"***\\n\\n     Although at first she was frightened, now Rukmi�� may be displeased.\\nThat is why Lord K���a now throws away the idea that Rukmi�� is meekly\\nsubmissive to Him. Here the woprd {.sy 168}bhamini\" is in the vocative\\ncase.\\n\\n     Lord K���a, who had yearned to see Rukmi�� reveal Her love for Him,\\nsays in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.49):\\n\\n\\ns�dhvy etac-chrotu-k�mais tva�\\n     r�ja-putri pralambhit�\\n\\n\\n     \"O saintly lady, O princess, We deceived you only because We wanted to\\nhear you speak like this. Indeed, everything you said in reply to My words\\nis most certainly true.\"***\\n\\n     Wishing to refute Lord K���a's assertyion of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.60.49 and also wishing to reveal the truth about Lord K���a, Queen\\nRukmi�� spoke these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.38):\\n\\n\\ntva� vai samasta-puru��rtha-maya� phal�tm�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"You are the embodiment of all human goals and You are Yourself the\\nfinal aim of life. Desiring to attain You, O all-powerful Lord, intelligent\\npersons abandon everything else. It is they who are worthy of Your\\nassociation, not men and women absorbed in the pleasure and grief resulting\\nfrom their mutual lust.\"***\\n\\n     After understanding that Lord K���a was only joking, Queen Rukmi��\\nmanifested a little of the anger that He had yearned to see. She said in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.40):\\n\\n\\nj��ya� vacas tava gad�graja. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My Lord, as a lion drives away lesser animals to claim his proper\\ntribute, You drove off the assembled kings with the resounding twang of\\nYour ��r�ga bow and then claimed me, Your fair share. Thus it is sheer\\nfoolishness, my dear Gad�graja, for You to say You took shelter in the\\nocean out of fear of those kings.\"***\\n\\n     Here the word \"j��yam\" means \"great foolishness\" just as \"m�dhuryam\"\\nmeans \"great sweetness\" in these words of �r�la Bilvama�gala �h�kura's �r�\\nK���a-kar��m�ta (Text 68):\\n\\n\\nm�dhuryam eva nu mano-nayan�m�ta� nu\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a is the great nectar sweetness that fills the eyes and the\\nheart.\"\\n\\n     In speaking these joking words, Lord K���a's purpose was to agitate\\nQueen Rukmi�� and destroy the pride she felt in always having His\\nassociation. This is seen in these words spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�\\nin �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.21):\\n\\n\\net�vad uktv� bhagav�n\\n     �tm�na� vallabh�m iva\\nmanyam�n�m avi�le��t\\n     tad-darpa-ghna up�ramat\\n\\n\\n     \"Rukmi�� had thought herself especially beloved by the Lord because He\\nnever left her company. By saying these things to her He vanquished her\\npride, and then He stopped speaking.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a had another purpose also, a purpose seen in these words He\\npersonally spoke in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.60.56):\\n\\n\\nbhr�tur vir�pa-kara�a� yudhi nirjitasya\\n     prodv�ha-parva�i ca tad-vadham ak�a-go��hy�m\\ndu�kham samuttham asaho 'smad-ayoga-bh�ty�\\n     naiv�brav�� kim api tena vaya� jit�s te\\n\\n\\n     \"When your brother, who had been defeated in battle and then\\ndisfigured, was later killed during a gambling match on Aniruddha's wedding\\nday, you felt unbearable grief, yet out of fear of losing Me you spoke not\\na word. By this silence you have conquered Me.\"***\\n\\n     In this chapter of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam are seen Queen Rukmi��'s great\\nlove for Lord K���a and her natural absence of anger. From this it is seen\\nthat it certainly truthfully said that Lord K���a's beloveds love Him very\\ndearly. When the manifestation of pride and anger is concluded, then\\nsambhoga is manifest. This is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.33.1):\\n\\n\\nittha� bhagavato gopya�\\n     �rutv� v�ca� su-pe�al��\\njahur viraha ja� t�pam-\\n     tad-a�gopacit��i�a�\\n\\n\\n     \"When the cowherd girls heard the Supreme Personality of Godhead speak\\nthese most charming words, they forgot their distress caused by separation\\nfrom Him. Touching His transcendental limbs, they felt all their desires\\nfulfilled.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is clear. This verse was spoken by �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 387 and 388\\n\\n\\n     Now prema-vaicittyam will be considered. Its qualities are described\\nin these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, ���g�ra-bheda-prakara�a (Text 134):\\n\\n\\npriyasya sannikar�e 'pi\\n     premotkar�a-svabh�vata�\\ny� vi�le�a-dhiy�rtis tat\\n     prema-vaicittyam ucyate\\n\\n\\n     \"When, even in the beloved's presence, the lover, out of intense love,\\nis aggrieved with fears of separation in the future, such a state is called\\nprema-vaicittyam.\"\\n\\n     Prema-vaicittyam is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.90.13-\\n24):\\n\\n\\nk���asyaiva� viharato\\n     gaty-�l�pek�ita-smitai�\\nnarma-k�veli-pari�vangai�\\n     str���� kila h�t� dhiya�\\n\\n\\n     \"In this way Lord K���a would sport with His queens, totally\\ncaptivating their hearts with His gestures, talks, glances, and smiles, and\\nalso with His jokes, playful exchanges, and embraces.***\\n\\n\\n�cur mukundaika-dhiyo\\n     gir� unmatta-vaj ja�am\\ncintayantyo 'ravind�k�am\\n     t�ni me gadata� ���u\\n\\n\\n     \"The queens would become stunned in ecstatic trance, their minds\\nabsorbed in K���a alone. Then, thinking of their lotus-eyed Lord, they\\nwould speak as if insane. Please hear these words from me as I relate\\nthem.***\\n\\n\\n�r�-mahi�ya �cu�\\n\\nkurari vilapasi tva� v�ta-nidr� na �e�e\\n     svapiti jagati r�try�m ��varo gupta-bodha�\\nvayam iva sakhi kaccid g��ha-nirviddha-cet�\\n     nalina-nayana-h�sod�ra-l�lek�itena\\n\\n\\n     \"The queens said: O kurari bird, you are lamenting. Now it is night,\\nand somewhere in this world the Supreme Lord is asleep in a hidden place.\\nBut you are wide awake, O friend, unable to fall asleep. Is it that, like\\nus, you have had your heart pierced to the core by the lotus-eyed Lord's\\nmunificent, playful smiling glances?\"***\\n\\n\\nnetre nim�layasi. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Poor cakrav�k�, even after closing your eyes, you continue to cry\\npitifully through the night for your unseen mate. Or is it that, like us,\\nyou have become the servant of Acyuta and hanker to wear in your braided\\nhair the garland He has blessed with the touch of His feet?***\\n\\n\\nbho bho� sad� ni���nase udanvann. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear ocean, you are always roaring, not sleeping at night. Are you\\nsuffering insomnia? Or is it that, as with us, Mukunda has taken your\\ninsignias and you are hopeless of retrieving them?***\\n\\n\\ntva� yak�m�n�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear moon, having contracted a severe case of tuberculosis, you\\nhave become so emaciated that you fail to dispel the darkness with your\\nrays. Or is it that you appear dumbstruck because, like us, you cannot\\nremember the encouraging promises Mukunda once made to you?***\\n\\n\\nki� nv �c�ritam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O Malayan breeze, what have we done to displease you, so that you\\nstir up lust in our hearts, which have already been shattered by Govinda's\\nsidelong glances?***\\n\\n\\nmegha �r�m�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O revered cloud, you are indeed dear ot the chief of the Yadus, who\\nbears the mark of �r�vatsa. Like us, you are bound to Him by love and are\\nmeditating upon Him. Your heart is distraught with great eagerness, as our\\nhearts are, and as you remember Him again and again you shed a torrent of\\ntears. Association with K���a brings such misery!***\\n\\n\\npriya-r�va. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O sweet-throated cuckoo, in a voice that could revive the dead you\\nare vibrating the same sounds we once heard from our beloved, the most\\npleasing of speakers. Please tell me what I can do today to please you.***\\n\\n\\nna calasi. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O magnanimous mountain, you neither move nor speak. You must be\\npondering some matter of great importance. Or do you, like us, desire to\\nhold on your breasts the feet of Vasudeva's darling son?***\\n\\n\\n�u�yad-dhrada�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O rivers, wives of the ocean, your pools have now dried up. Alas, you\\nhave shriveled to nothing, and your wealth of lotuses has vanished. Are\\nyou, then, like us, who are withering away because of not receiving the\\naffectionate glance of our dear husband, the Lord of Madhu, who has cheated\\nour hearts?***\\n\\n\\nha�sa sv�gatam �syat�� piba payo br�hy a�ga �aure� kath��\\n     d�ta� tva� nu vid�ma kaccid ajita� svasty asta ukta� pur�\\nki� na va� cala-sauh�da� smarati ta� kasm�d bhaj�mo vaya�\\n     k�audr�l�paya k�ma-da� �r�yam �te saivaika-ni��ha striyam\\n\\n\\n     \"Welcome, swan. Please sit here and drink some milk. Give us some news\\nof the descendent of ��ra, dear one. We know that you are His messenger. Is\\nthat invincible Lord doing well, and does that unreliable friend of ours\\nstill remember the words He spoke to us long ago? Why should we go and\\nworship Him? O servant of a petty master, go tell Him who fulfills our\\ndesires to come here without the goddess of fortune. Is she the only woman\\nexclusively devoted to Him?\"***\\n\\n     �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.13 means: \"With His gestures and other\\nactivities Lord captivated the hearts of His queens as He enjoyed pastimes\\nwith them.\" �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.14 means: \"Rapt in meditation on Lord\\nK���a and thus momentarily speechless (agira�), and again and again\\nmaddened by their anur�ga love, and meditating on playful lotus-eyed K���a\\nas if He were not present before them, the gop�s spoke words devoid of\\ncommon sense. Please hear these words as I relate them.\"\\n     Maddened by the touchof separation from Lord K���a, the queens speak\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.15-24. �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.15 means: \"O Kurari\\nbird, all alone, you lament at night somewhere in this world. Therefore you\\ndo not sleep. In some hidden place our master sleeps. Thinking, \"Because of\\nthe similar sweetness of your lament and our lament, we think. . . \", the\\nqueens spoke the second half of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.15. In the\\nfollowing verses the queens think similar ideas in otber ways. Then,\\nthinking that, sent by the Lord, a swan has come as a messenger before\\nthem, the queens speak �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.24. In that verse they say:\\n{.sy 168}Does K���a remember what He spoke to us in private? or does He not\\nremember it?\" In that verse the word \"ksaudra\" means \"O messenger of our\\nfickle friend\", {.sy 168}k�madam\" means \"who agitates young girls\", and\\n{.sy 168}�l�paya\" means \"please call\". Then the queens say: \"We leave\\neverything for Him. He should comw without the goddess of fortune (sriyam\\nrte). Here the queens sarcastically pretend to glorify the goddess of\\nfortune. They say: \"Of all women the goddess of fortune alone has true\\ndevotion to Lord K���a. Therefore how is it possible that Lord K���a will\\nnto be devoted to her.\" Speaking these words with voices choked with\\nemotion, the queens sarcastically speak of the goddess of fortune and also\\nreveal their own love and devotion for Lord K���a. That is seen in this\\nverse.\\n     After this manifestation of love-in-separation, the queens enjoy\\neternal sambhoga pastimes with Lord K���a. It is said of these queens\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.90.25):\\n\\n\\nit�d��ena bh�vena\\n     k���e yoge�vare�vare\\nkriyam�nena m�dhavyo\\n     lebhire param�� gatim\\n\\n\\n     \"By thus speaking and acting with such ecstatic love for Lord K���a,\\nthe master of all masters of mystic yoga, His loving wives attained the\\nultimate goal of life.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the words \"gati� lebhire\" mean {.sy 168}they attained an\\neternal relationship with Lord Vi��u-K���a.\" The reason they attained this\\nrelationship is given here in the word \"m�dhavya�\", which means \"the\\neternal beloveds of Lord K���a, who is called M�dhava because He was born\\nin the Madhu dynasty\". This verse was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 389 and 390\\n\\n\\n     Now prav�sa (separation when the belovedhas gone to a faraway place)\\nwill be considered. Prav�sa is of many kinds. In the pravasa separation\\nfelt by the vraja-gop�s, the gop�s did meet Lord K���a again. The qualities\\nof prav�sa are described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, ���g�ra-bheda-\\nprakara�a (Text 139-140):\\n\\n\\np�rva-sa�gatayor y�nor\\n     bhaved de��ntar�dibhi�\\nvyavadh�na� tu yat pr�j�ai�\\n     sa prav�sa it�ryate\\n\\ntaj-janya-vipralambho 'ya�\\n     prav�satvena kathyate\\n\\n\\n     \"When ther young couple, after meeting and establishing Their\\nrelationship become again separated by one of them moving to a foreign\\ncountry or a distant place, such separation is called prav�sa by the wise.\"\\n\\n     Prav�sa is also described in these words of Ujjvala-n�lama�i, ���g�ra-\\nbheda-prakara�a (Text 153):\\n\\n\\ncint�tra j�garodvegau\\n     t�nava� malin��gat�\\npral�po vy�dhir unm�do\\n     moha-m�tyur da�� da�a\\n\\n\\n     \"In Prav�sa ten conditions are manifest: 1. cint� (meditation), 2.\\nj�gara (insomnia), 3. udvega (agitation), 4. t�navam (becoming thin and\\nemaciated), 5. malin��gat� (withering of the body), 5. pral�pa (abundant\\ntalking), 7. vy�dhi (disease), 8. unm�da (madness), 9. moha (bwilderment),\\nand 10. m�tyu (death.\"\\n\\n     Prav�sa is of two kinds: 1. ki�cid-d�ra-gamana-maya (where the beloved\\nis separated by a short distance) and 2. su-d�ra-gamana-maya (where the\\nbeloved isseparated by a great distance). The first of these is also of two\\nkinds: 1. eka-l�l�-gata (in one pastime), and 2. parampar�ntar�la-gata\\n(within a series of pastimes). The first of these (ki�cid-d�ra-gamana-maya\\nprav�sa) is seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.30.1):\\n\\n\\nantarhite bhagavati\\n     sahasaiva vraj��gan��\\natapya�s tam acaks�n��\\n     kari�ya iva y�thapam\\n\\n\\n     \"When Lord K���a disappeared so suddenly, the gop�s felt great sorrow\\nat losing sight of Him, like a group of female elephants who have lost\\ntheir mate.\"***\\n\\n     This kind of prav�sa is also seen in these owrds of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.30.38):\\n\\n\\ntata� c�ntardadhe k���a�\\n     s� vadh�r anvatapyata\\n\\n\\n     \"But as soon as He said this, Lord K���a disappeared. His beloved\\nconsort then immediately felt great remorse.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is cleear. This verse was spoken by �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
