      "content": "\\n\\n     Another example of the gop�s greeting a messenger from Lord K���a is\\nseen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.65.9):\\n\\n\\ngopyo hasantya� papracch�\\n     r�ma-sandar�an�d�t��\\nkaccid �ste sukha� k���a�\\n     pura-str�-jana-vallabha�\\n\\n\\n     \"Honored to have the personal audience of Lord Balar�ma, the young\\ngop�s smiled and asked Him: `Is K���a, the darling of city women, living\\nhappily.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"hasantya�\" means {.sy 168}with smiles mixed\\nwith love and anger, smiles that mock Lord K���a\". This verse was spoken by\\n�r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 403-413\\n\\n\\n     In Uddhava's presence the gop�s spoke wildly. This is seen in these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.11):\\n\\n\\nk�cin madhukara� d���v�\\n     dhy�yant� k���a-sa�gamam\\npriya-prasth�pita� d�ta�\\n     kalpayitvedam abrav�t\\n\\n\\n     \"One of the gop�s, while meditating on Her previous association with\\nK���a, saw a honeybee before Her and imagined it to be a messenger sent by\\nHer beloved. Thus She spoke as follows.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"k�cit\" (a certain gop�) refers to �r� R�dh�\".\\nThis is explained in the V�san�-bh��ya commentary. This truth is also seen\\nin the Da�ama-�ippan� commentary of �r�la San�tana Gosv�m�. In the\\nfollowing eigth verses (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.12-19), angry �r� R�dh�\\nspeaks wildly. She says (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.12):\\n\\n\\nmadhupa kitava-bandho. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O honeybee, O friend of a cheater, don't touch My feet with your\\nwhiskers, which are smeared with the ku�kuma that rubbed onto K���a's\\ngarland when it was crushed hy the breasts of a rival lover! Let K���a\\nsatisfy the women of Mathur�. One who sends a messenger like you will\\ncertainly be ridiculed in the Yadus' assembly.\"***\\n\\n     Then She explains the reason for Her anger in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.47.13):\\n\\n\\nsak�d adhara-sudh�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"After making us drink the enchanting nectar of His lips only once,\\nK���a suddenly abandoned us, just as you might quickly abandon some\\nflowers. How is, then, that Goddess Padm� willingly serves His lotus feet?\\nAlas! The answer must certainly be that her mind has been stolen away by\\nHis deceitful words.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse �r� R�dh� criticizes Her rival, Goddess Padm�. In the\\nnext verse She rejects the words of praise brought by the messenger. She\\nsays (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.14):\\n\\n\\nkim iha. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O bee, why do you sing here so much about the Lord of the Yadus, in\\nfront of us homeless people? These topics are old news to us. Better you\\nsing about that friend of Arjuna in front of His new girlfriends, the\\nburning desire in whose breasts He has now relieved. Those ladies will\\nsurely give you the charity you are begging.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"vijaya\" means \"Lord K���a, who brings all\\nothers under His control\", \"sakh�\" means \"your friend\", \"sakh�n�m\" means\\n{.sy 168}of the women of Mathur�\", and \"prasa�ga�\" means \"songs that bring\\none under their control\". The gop�s here say: \"These songs are faulty\\nbecause they make us attached to K���a. Even if you take that fault away,\\nthese topics still make us wretched and miserable. This proves that K���a\\nis completely merciless.\" The gop�s say in the next verse (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.47.15):\\n\\n\\ndivi bhuvi. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"In heaven, on earth, or in the subterranean sphere, what woman is\\nunavailable to Him? He simply arches His eyebrows and smiles with deceptive\\ncharm, and they all become His. The supreme goddess herself worships the\\ndust of His feet, so what is our position in comparison? But at least those\\nwho are wretched can chant His name, Uttama�sloka.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"api ca\" means {.sy 168}nevertheless\", \"k�pa�a-\\npak�e\" means \"wretched persons like us\", \"and \"uttama-�loka-�abda�\" means\\nthe sound of Lord K���a's name may be present\". Here the gop�s hint:\\n\"Because He will not allow us to see Him, He is not merciful, and because\\nHe is not merciful, He should not be called by the name Uttama�loka\".\\n     Thinking the bee's gentle humming to be a host of flattering words\\nfrom Lord K���a, �r� R�dh� says in the next verse (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.47.16):\\n\\n\\nvis�ja �irasi. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Keep your head off My feet! I know what you're doing. You expertly\\nlearned diplomacy from Mukunda, and now you come as His messenger with\\nflattering words. But he abandoned those who fro His sake alone gave up\\ntheir children, husbands, and all other relations. He's simply ungrateful.\\nWhy should I make up with Him now?\"***\\n\\n     With love and anger criticizing Lord K���a, �r� R�dh� admitShe cannot\\nstop talking of Him. Then She finds fault with Him. She says (�r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.47.17):\\n\\n\\nm�gayur iva. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Like a hunter He cruelly shot the king of the monkeys with arrows.\\nBecause He was conquered by a woman, He disfigured another woman who came\\nto Him with lusty desires. And even after consuming the gifts of Bali\\nMaharaja, He bound him up with ropes as if he were a crow. So let us give\\nup all frindship with this dark-compexioned boy, even if we can't give up\\ntalking about Him.\"***\\n\\n     Here the word \"asita\" means \"K���a, who is black because of all these\\nmisdeeds\" and \"sakhyai�\" means with loving friendship\". Again and again She\\naffirms that K���a should be rejected. She again finds fault with K���a\\nwhen She speaks the next verse of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.18):\\n\\n\\nyad-anucarita. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"To hear about the pastimes that K���a regularly performs is nectar\\nfro the ears. For those who relish just a single drop of that nectar, even\\nonce, their dedication to material duality is ruined. Many such persons\\nhave suddenly given up their wretched homes and families and, themselves\\nbecoming wretched, traveled here to V�nd�vana to wander about like birds,\\nbegging for their living.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"karna-piyusa\" means {.sy 168}these topics may\\nbe nectar for the ears, but they are not nectar for the heart\". They are\\nsweet only in the beginning. The words \"vidh�ta-dvandva-dharm� vina����\"\\nmean {.sy 168}at the end these topics make one almost unconscious\". {.sy\\n168}Iha\" means \"in V�nd�vana\", \"viha�g��\" means \"parrots and other birds\",\\n\"bhik�u-cary�� caranti\" means \"they follow the activities of the sannyasis,\\nwhich means they ignore their material bodies and other material things. It\\nis seen that they act in this way\". That is the meaning here.\\n\\n     Then �r� R�dh� laments in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.19):\\n\\n\\n\\nvayam �tam iva. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Faithfully taking His deceitful words as true, we became just like\\nthe black deer's foolish wives, who trust the cruel hunter's song. Thus we\\nrepeatedly felt the sharp pain of lust cause by the touch of His nails. O\\nmessenger, please talk about something besides K���a.\"***\\n\\n     Then, fearing that Her harsh and angry words spoken in these eight\\nverses will chase the messenger away, �r� R�dh� tries to end Her quarrel\\nwith Lord K���a. She says in the next verse of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.47.20):\\n\\n\\npriya-sakha. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O friend of My dear one, has My beloved sent you here again? I should\\nhonor you, friend, so please choose whatever boon you wish. But why have\\nyou came back to take us to Him, whose conjugal love is so difficult to\\ngive up? After all, gentle bee, His consort is the goddess �r�, as she is\\nalways with Him, staying upon His chest.\"***\\n\\n     With words of crooked irony �r� R�dh� speaks the second half of this\\nverse. Here the word \"dvandva\" means {.sy 168}amorous pastimes\"\". The\\nreason amorous pastimes with K���a are difficult to give up is given in the\\nword \"satatam\" (Goddess Lak�m� always on Lord K���a's chest). Here,\\nspeaking words of love and anger, �r� R�dh� imagines that the �r�vatsa line\\non Lord K���a's chest is actually Goddess Lak�m�. At the end of this\\npassage �r� R�dh� humbly speaks these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.47.21):\\n\\n\\napi bata. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O Uddhava! It is indeed regrettable that K���a resides in Mathur�.\\nDoes He remember His father's household affairs and His friends, the\\ncowherd boys? O great soul! Does He ever talk about us, His maidservants?\\nWhen will He lay on our heads His aguru-scented hand? \"***\\n\\n     The messenger (Uddhava) sent by Lord K���a consoled the gop�s in two\\nways: 1. by praising them, and 2. by speaking Lord K���a's message to them.\\nHe praised them in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.23):\\n\\n\\naho y�ya� sma p�r��rth��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Certainly you gop�s are all-successful and are universally worshiped\\nbecause you have dedicated your minds in this way to the Supreme\\nPersonality of Godhead, V�sudeva.\"***\\n\\n     Uddhava's repeating Lord K���a's message is discussed in K���a-\\nsandarbha (Anuccheda 155)> That message is given in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.47.29):\\n\\n\\nbhavat�n�� viyogo me. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Lord said: You are never actually separated from Me, for\\nI am the soul of all creation. Just as the elements of nature-ether, air,\\nfire, water, and earth-are present in every created thing, so I am present\\nwithin everyuone's mind, life-air, and senses, and also within the physical\\nelements and the modes of material nature.\"***\\n\\n     These words describe Lord K���a's eternal pastimes with the people of\\nVraja in the eternal V�nd�vana in another manifestation (in the spiritual\\nsky). These words also describe the meditational process of j��na-yoga, by\\nwhich the people of this world may obtain relief from material sufferings.\\nHowever, the teaching of j��na-yoga do not bring peace to the gop�s. Lord\\nK���a then explains in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.34):\\n\\n\\nyat tv aha� bhavat�n�� vai. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"But the actual reason why I, the beloved object of your sight, have\\nstayed far away from you is that I wanted to intensify your meditation upon\\nMe and thus draw your minds closer to Me.\"***\\n\\n     Lord K���a also explains in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.37):\\n\\n\\ny� may� kr��at� r�try�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Although some gop�s had to remain in the cowherd village and so could\\nnot join the r�sa dance to sport with Me at night in the forest, they were\\nnonetheless fortunate. Indeed, they attained Me by thinking of My glorious\\npastimes.\"***\\n\\n     Further explaining the meaning of the words He spoke in �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam 10.47.34, Lord K���a says in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.41):\\n\\n\\napi smaratha na� sakhya�\\n     sv�n�m artha-cik�r�ay�\\ngat�n. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear girlfriends, do you still remember Me? It was for My\\nrelatives' sake that I stayed away so long, intent on destroying my\\nenemies.\"***\\n\\n     By describing his purpose (in staying way) Lord K���a's intention to\\nincrease the gop�s' feelings of happiness and love for Him. This is seen in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.47.53):\\n\\n\\ntatas t�� k���a-sande�air\\n     vyapeta-viraha-jvar��\\nuddhava� p�jay�� cakrur\\n     j��tv�tm�nam adhok�ajam\\n\\n\\n     \"Lord K���a's messages having relieved their fever of separation, the\\ngop�s then worshipped Uddhava, recognizing him as nondifferent from their\\nLord, K���a.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the word \"vyapeta-viraha-jvar��\" means \"by hearing of\\nLord K���a's return and by hearing of other topics also, the gop�s became\\npacified\". In this context the gop�s asked (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.40):\\n\\n\\nkaccid gad�graja� saumya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Gentle Uddhava, is the elder brother of Gada now bestowing on the\\ncity women the pleasure that actually belongs to us? We suppose those\\nladies worship Him with generous glances full of affectionate, shy\\nsmiles.\"***\\n\\n     In �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.47.53 the word \"�tm�nam\" means \"because he was\\na messenger sent by K���a, Uddhava was able to know what was in Lord\\nK���a's heart\", and {.sy 168}adhok�ajam\" means \"the gop�s thought Uddhava\\nwas Lord K���a Himself\". Because they thought he was Lord K���a, the gop�s\\nworshiped Uddhava (uddhava� p�jay�� cakru�). The idea that Uddhava was\\nLorrd K���a Himself is also seen in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n(10.46.14):\\n\\n\\ntam �gatam sam�gamya\\n     k���asy�nucara� priyam\\nnanda� pr�ta� pari�vajya\\n     v�sudeva-dhiy�rcayat\\n\\n\\n     \"As sson as Uddhava arrived in Nanda Mah�r�ja's home, Nanda came\\nforward to meet him. The cowherd king embraced him in great happiness and\\nworshipped him as nondifferent from Lorrd V�sudeva.\"***\\n\\n     This verse was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosvnamni.\\n\\n\\nAnucchedas 414-421\\n\\n\\n     Lord Balar�ma's carrying a message from Lord K���a in Dv�rak� is seen\\nin these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.65.16):\\n\\n\\nsa�kar�a�as t�� k���asya\\n     sande�air h�daya�gamai�\\ns�ntvay�m �sa bhagav�n\\n     n�n�nunaya-kovida�\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Loord Balar�ma, the attractoir of all, being expert at\\nvarious kinds of conciliation, consoled the gop�s by relaying to them the\\nconfidential messages Lord K���a had sent with Him. These messages deeply\\ntouched the gop�s' hearts.\"***\\n\\n     After this message the gop�s enjoyed sambhoga pastimes with Lord\\nK���a, directlyseeing Him at Kuruk�etra. Those pastimes are described in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.39):\\n\\n\\ngopya� ca k���am upalabhya cir�d abhi��a�\\n     yat-prek�a�e d��i�u pak�ma-k�ta� �apanti\\nd�gbhir h�d�-k�tam ala� parirabhya sarv�s\\n     tad-bh�vam �pur api nitya-yuja� dur�pam\\n\\n\\n     \"While gazing at their beloved K���a, the young gop�s used to condemn\\nthe creator of their eyelids (which would momentarily block their vison of\\nHim). Now, seeing K���a again after such a long separation, with their eyes\\nthey took Him into their hearts, and there they embraced Him to their full\\nsatisfaction. In this way they became totally absorbed in ecstatic\\nmeditation on Him, although those who constantly practice mystic yoga find\\nsuch absorption difficult to achieve.\"***\\n\\n     After in this way describing the condition of the gop�s' hearts,\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam explains the gop�s' ecstatic love for Lord K���a in these\\nwords (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 10.82.40):\\n\\n\\nbhagav��s t�s tath�-bh�t�\\n     vivikt� upasa�gata�\\n��li�yanamaya� pr��v�\\n     prahasann idam abrav�t\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Lord approached the gop�s in a secluded place as they\\nstood in their ecstatic trance. After embracing each of them and inquiring\\nabout their well-being, He laughed and spoke as follows.\"***\\n\\n     Agitating the gop�s' hearts, Lord K���a made the gop�s forgive his\\noffenses. His smile was glorious. Then He consoled the gop� with these\\nwords of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.41):\\n\\n\\napi smaratha na� sakhya�\\n     sv�n�m artha-cik�r�ay�\\ngat��� c�rayatan chatru-\\n     pak�a-k��pa�a-cetasa�\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear girlfriends, do you still remember Me? It was for My\\nrelatives' sake that I stayed away so long, intent on destroying my\\nenemies.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse Lord K���a says: \"Please do not remember Me with anger.\\nThen, to show that He is indeed faultless, Lord K���a says: \"It was for My\\nrelatives' sake that I stayed away so long, intent on destroying my\\nenemies.\" Here the word \"svanam\" means \"of My father, the king of Vraja,\\nand also of My kinsmen, the Y�davas\". Thess words may also hint: \"These two\\nsides of the family (K���a's kinsmen in Vraja and Dv�rak�) were both part\\nof the Y�dava dynasty\". Then Lord K���a explains why He was so long in\\nreturning to Vraja. He says: \"I was intent on destroying my enemies\". These\\nwords hint: \"From now only you will enjoyMy company (sambhoga) without\\ninterruption\".\\n\\n     Fearing the gop�s may remain dissatisfied at heart, Lord K���a\\ncomforted them by explaining that the Supreme Personality of Godhead is the\\nultimate independent controller of everyone. He said (�r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.82.42):\\n\\n\\napy avadhyayath�sman svid\\n     ak�ta-j��vi�a�kay�\\nn�na� bh�t�ni bhagav�n\\n     yunakti vinuyakti ca\\n\\n\\n     \"Do you perhaps think I'm ungrateful and thus hold Me in contempt?\\nAfter all, it is the Supreme Lord who brings living beings together and\\nthen separates them.\"***\\n\\n     Fearing that the gop�s might reply: \"You are the Supreme Personality\\nof Godhead\", Lord K���a explains: {.sy 168}Your separation from Me gave\\nbirth to great love for Me in your hearts. This is not against your actual\\ndesire.\" Lord K���a told them in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.44):\\n\\n\\nmayi bhaktir hi bh�t�n�m\\n     am�tatv�ya kalpate\\ndi��y� yad �s�n mat-sneho\\n     bhavat�n�� mad-�pana�\\n\\n\\n     \"Rendering devotional service to Me qualifies any living being for\\neternal life. But by your good fortune you have developed a special loving\\nattitude toward Me, by which you have attained Me.\"***\\n\\n     �r�dhara Sv�m� comments on this verse:\\n\\n     \"This verse means: `Rendering devotional service to Me qualifies any\\nliving being for eternal life. But by your good fortune you have developed\\na special loving attitude toward Me.\" The question may be asked: `Why is\\nthis so?' The answer is given here in the words: `You have attained Me'.\"\\n\\n     Then, in order to console the gop�s and reassure them they they indeed\\nhave attained His association, Lord K���a explains to them that because He\\nis the Supreme Personality of Godhead manifest in a humanlike form. He\\nexplains to the gop�s that He is present everywhere and He is the shelter\\nin which everything rests. He tells them in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.45-\\n46):\\n\\n\\naha� hi sarva-bh�t�n�m. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear ladies, I am the beginning and end of all created beings and\\nexist both within and without them, just as the elements ether, water,\\nearth, air, and fire are the beginning and end of all material objects and\\nexist both within and without them.\"*\\n     \"In this way all created beings reside within the basic elements of\\ncreation, while the spirit souls pervade the creation, remaining in their\\nown true identity. You should see both of these-the material creation and\\nthe self-as manifest within Me, the imperishable Supreme Truth.\"***\\n\\n     In the description of the Lord's D�modara pastimes it is also said in\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.9.13-14):\\n\\n\\nna c�ntar na bahir yasya. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The Supreme Personality of Godhead has no beginning and no end, no\\nexterior and no interior, no front and no rear. In other words, He is all-\\npervading. Because He is not under the influence of the element of time,\\nfor Him there is no difference between past, present and future. He exists\\nin His own transcendental form at all times. Being absolute, beyond\\nrelativity, He is free from distinctions between cause and effect, although\\nHe is the cause and effect of everything. That unmanifested person, who is\\nbeyond the perception of the senses, had now appeared as a human child, and\\nmother Ya�od�, considering Him her own ordinary child, bound Him to the\\nwooden mortar with a rope.\"*\\n\\n     In these two verses are described some of Lord K���a's eternal\\npastimes in V�nd�vana with the people of Vraja, pastimes also revealed in\\nthe K���a-sandarbha, Anucchedas 174-175. Those pastimes are also considered\\nhere. The gop�s were able to see Lord K���a as the Supreme. That is\\ndescribed in these words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.82.47):\\n\\n\\nadhy�tma-�ik�ay�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Having thus been instructed by K���a in spiritual matters, the gop�s\\nwere freed from all tinges of false ego because of their incessant\\nmeditation upon Him. And with their deepening absorption in Him, they came\\nto understand Him fully.\"***\\n     In this verse the word \"�tma\" refers to Lord K���a, and the word\\n\"siksaya\" means \"by His instruction\". Their bodies old and worn by\\nmeditating on Lord K���a in the mood of separation, the gop�s then could\\nsee Lord K���a in that way. Some thinkers interpret �r�mad-Bh�gavatam\\n10.82.45 in this way: \"According to ordinary perception it seems that Lord\\nK���a is here preaching the philosophy of Brahman, but that philosophy is\\nnot the true meaning of His words here. When Rukm� was disfigured Lord\\nBalar�ma preached to �r� Rukmi�� what also seemed to be the philosophy of\\nBrahman. However, because Rukmi�� was directly enjoying pastimes with Lord\\nK���a, that preaching of Lord Balarama's was only a specific pastime meant\\nto look like the activities of an ordinary person in this world. In the\\nsame way Lord K���a's preaching of this philosophy to the gop�s is also\\nonly a pastime. Actually the gop�s yearned to enjoy pastimes with Lord\\nK���a directly in His spiritual form. This is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.82.48):\\n\\n\\n�hus ca te nalina-n�bha pad�ravinda�\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear Lord whose navel is just like a lotus flower, Your lotus feet\\nare the only dhelter for those who have fallen into the deep well of\\nmaterial existence. Your feet are worshiped and meditated upon by great\\nmystic yogis and highly learned philosophers. We wish that these lotus feet\\nmay also be awakened within our hearts, although we are ordinary persons\\nengaged in household affairs.\"***\\n\\n     In this verse the gop�s say: \"Dear Lord whose navel is just like a\\nlotus flower, may Your lotus feet be awakened within the hearts of us, who\\nare overcome by always meditating on You. Following Your instructions, the\\npeaceful-hearted kings of yoga meditate on Your feet in their hearts.\" An\\nexplanation of this verse may be seen in the K���a-sandarbha, Anuccheda\\n170. This verse was spoken by �r�la �ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
