      "sourceStartLine": 25267,
      "content": "\\n\\n     All of this is revealed in a passage from the Padma Pur��a, Uttara-\\nkha��a, a passage quoted in K���a-sandarbha, Anuccheda 176. In that\\npasasage it is said that Lord K���a left Dv�rak� and returned to V�nd�vana.\\nThere, visible to everyone, even the ordinary people of this world, He\\nenjoyed pastimes for two months. After that Lord K���a gave the people of\\nV�nd�vana eternal samyoga pastimes with Him, although those pastimes were\\nnot visible to ordinary people. Lord K���a clearly exlained all this to\\nUddhava in �r�mad-Bh�gavatam's Eleventh Canto. There Lord K���a sais\\n(�r�mad-Bh�gavatam 11.12.10-11):\\n\\n\\nr�me�a s�rdha� mathur�� pra��te. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The residents of V�nd�vana, headed by the gop�s, were always\\ncompletely attached to Me with deepest love. Therefore, when My uncle\\nAkr�ra brought My brother Balar�ma and Me to the city of Mathur�, the\\nresidents of V�nd�vana suffered extreme mental distress because of\\nseparation from Me and could not find any other source of happiness.\"***\\n\\n\\nt�s t�� k�ap��. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Dear Uddhava, all of those nights that the gop�s spent with Me, their\\nmost dearly beloved, in the land of V�nd�vana seemed to them to pass in\\nless than a moment. Bereft of My association, however, the gop�s felt that\\nthose same nights dragged on forever, as if each night were equal to a day\\nof Brahm�.\"***\\n\\n     After that time there was no further separation of Lord K���a and the\\npeople of V�nd�vana. After that time the people of V�nd�vana always enjoyed\\nthe bliss of Lord K���a's association. Lord K���a described this truth in\\nthese words of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (11.12.12-13):\\n\\n\\nt� n�vidan mayy anu�a�ga-baddha-dhiya�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"My dear Uddhava, just as great sages in yoga trance merge into self\\nrealization like rivers merging into the ocean, and are thus not aware of\\nmaterial names and forms, similarly the gop�s were so completely attached\\nto Me within their minds that they could not think of their own bodies, or\\nof this world, or of their future lives. Their entire consciousness was\\nsimply bound up in Me.***\\n     \"All those hundreds of thousands of gop�s, understanding Me to be\\ntheir most charming lover and ardently desiring Me in that way, were\\nunaware of my actual position. Yet by intimately associating with Me, the\\ngop�s attained Me, the Supreme Absolute Truth.\"***\\n\\n     In these verses the word \"anu\" means \"after the great separation\", and\\n\"sanga-baddha-dhiyah\" means \"their hearts were overcome with bliss\", and\\nnavidan\" means \"they were bewildered with joy\". In that state it was as if\\nthey had become onbe with Lord K���a. An example to explain that state is\\ngiven here in the phrase beginning with the words \"just as great sages in\\nyoga trance\". A different interpretation of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam 11.12.13 has\\nalready been given in K���a-sandarbha, Anuccheda 177. What the gop�s\\nattained (pr�pu�) is described in this verse. It is briefly described\\nthere.  There it is said: \"The gop�s attained Me, the Supreme Brahman, who\\nam named K���a.\" Here Lord K���a hints: \"Unaware of My true nature, that I\\nam eternally the object of their love, the gop�s attained Me as their\\nparamour (j�ram). Then, desiring Me as their husband, they eventually\\nattained Me as their husband (rama�am).\" In this way it is seen that the\\ngop�s' relationship with Lord K���a after some time becomes parak�y�bh�sa\\n(the faint reflection of the love a girl has for her paramour). All this is\\nexplained in that passage of K���a-sandarbha, Anuccheda 177. My glorious\\nmaster, �r�la R�pa Gosv�m� explains in Ujjvala-n�lama�i (N�yik�-bheda-\\nprakara�a, Text 3):\\n\\n\\nne��� yad a�gini rase kavibhi� paro�h�\\n     tad gokul�mbuja-d���� kulam antare�a\\n��a�say� rasa-vidher avit�rit�n��\\n     ka�s�ri�� rasika-ma��ala-�ekhare�a\\n\\n\\n     \"The lotus-eyed gop�s of Gokula descended to this material world to\\nenjoy amorous pastimes with K���a, the crest jewel of those expert at\\nrelishing transcendental mellows, and the enemy of King Ka�sa. Except for\\nthose gop�s, learned scholars and philosophers do not consider amorous\\naffairs between a married woman and a paramour to be good or auspicious.\"\\n\\n     The scriptures explain that this was the activity at the time of Lord\\nK���a's descent to this world. The conclusion of all this is found in �r�la\\nR�pa Gosv�m�'s Lalita-M�dhava (7.18), where it is said:\\n\\n\\ndagdha� hanta dadh�nay� vapu�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"O moon-faced one, My dear friend ahs again come to Me. My desire to\\nsee Him scorched My body and wounded My heart with a monsoohn of pain. My\\nlife-breeath yearns to meet Him in V�nd�vana and enjoy pastimes with Him in\\na cottage by the Yamun�'s shore.\"\\n\\n     When the mistaken idea that He is their paramour is destroyed, then\\nthe gop�s enjoy the full bliss of perfect sambhoga pastimes with Lord\\nK���a. In this way vipralam,bha is divided into four parts and sambhoga is\\nalso divided into four parts. In sambhoga are many different pastimes, such\\nas l�l�-cauryam (playful theft), sa�g�nam (singing and music), r�sa (the\\nr�sa dance), jala-kr��� (water pastimes), and v�nd�vana-vih�ra (pastimes in\\nV�nd�vana forest). An example of playful theft is seen in these words of\\n�r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.22.9):\\n\\n\\nt�s�� v�s��sy up�d�ya\\n     n�pam �ruhya satvara�. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"Taking the girls' garments, He quickly climbed to the top of a\\nka�amba tree. Then, as He laughed loudly and His companions also laughed,\\nHe addressed the girls jokingly.\"***\\n\\n     The meaning of this verse is clear. This verse was spoken by �r�la\\n�ukadeva Gosv�m�.\\n\\n"
