      "content": "\\n\\n     An example of singing and music is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.33.9):\\n\\n\\nk�cit sama� mukundena. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"One gop�, joining Lord Mukunda in His singing, sang pure melodious\\ntones that rose harmoniously above His. K���a was pleased and showed great\\nappreciation for her performance, saying `Excellent! Excellent!' Then\\nanother gop� repeated the same melody, but in a special metrical pattern,\\nand K���a praised her also.\"***\\n\\n     Another example of singing and music is seen in these words of �r�mad-\\nBh�gavatam (10.34.20-21):\\n\\n\\nkad�cid atha govindo\\n     r�ma� c�dbhuta-vikrama�\\nvijahratur vraje r�try��\\n     madhya-gau vraja-yo�it�m\\n\\n\\n     \"Once Lord Govinda and Lord R�ma, the performers of wonderful feats,\\nwere playing in the forest at night with the young girls of Vraja.***\\n\\n\\nupag�yam�nau lalita�\\n     str� janair baddha-sauh�dai�\\nsv-ala�k�t�nulipt��gau\\n     sragvinau virajo-'mbarau\\n\\n\\n     \"K���a and Balar�ma wore flower garlands and spotless garments, and\\nTheir limbs were beautifully decorated and anointed. The women sang Their\\nglories in a charming way, bound to Them by affection.\"***\\n\\n     In general the Holi festival is celebrated in this way. Thus, to\\ncelebrate the Holi festival, Lord K���a and His brother, Lord Balar�ma,\\nenjoyed singing and many other pastimes with a host of girls in Vraja. This\\npastime is also described in the Bhavi�ya Pur��a, Uttara-kha��a. Even\\ntoday, the people of India celebrate the Holi festival in this way. It is\\nsaid in that passage of �r�mad-Bh�gavatam (10.34.22):\\n\\n\\nni��-mukha� m�nayant�v\\n     udito�upa-t�rakam. . .\\n\\n\\n     \"The two Lords praised the nightfall, signaled by the rising of the\\nmoon and the appearance of stars, a lotus-scented breeze and bees\\nintoxicated by the fragrance of jasmine flowers.\"***\\n\\n     The bliss of this festival, which occurs on the full-moon day of the\\n